(173, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Pengolahan Air Gambut Menjadi Air Bersih Menggunakan Reverse Osmosis untuk Kebutuhan Masyarakat Desa Danau Lamo', '1. Elma Mutmainnah\n2. Freddy Ilfan\n3. Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung\n4. Nadra Rahayu Faradilla\n5. Tri Marcelia Hijrani', 'Department of Environmental Engineering, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia', 'The village of Danau Lamo in Muaro Jambi Regency faces a serious challenge regarding water quality, where main water sources such as wells and rivers have been contaminated by peat water. This water, used for daily needs, has poor physical characteristics, such as a reddish-brown color, unpleasant odor, and slightly oily texture, often leading to health problems, particularly diarrhea in children. This research aims to treat peat water to produce clean water suitable for daily use using reverse osmosis (RO) technology, in accordance with the clean water quality standards set by the Indonesian Ministry of Health Regulation Number 32 of 2017. The applied method includes a coagulation-flocculation process using a bio-coagulant derived from mung bean powder, followed by filtration and purification using the RO OxFIL 400 GPD unit. Test results indicate a significant improvement in water quality, with physical parameters such as pH 6.6, TDS 97 mg/L, turbidity < 1.80 NTU, and water color < 3.47 TCU approaching standard quality benchmarks. Reverse Osmosis technology has proven effective in removing contaminants and producing water suitable for daily needs, thus offering a solution to the water quality issue in Danau Lamo Village.', 'Clean Water, Peat Water, Reverse Osmosis, Water Treatment', 'Elma Mutmainnah', 199, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS173-Elma Mutmainnah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS173-Elma Mutmainnah.pdf', '2024-08-26 10:16:24', '2024-09-12 05:13:34'),
(174, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Biosorbent From Uncharred Dry Leaves of Ficus Nota (Blanco) Merr. For Nickel (II) Removal in Wastewater', '*1) Joshua B. Bufi 2) Samuel Omar S. Gerio 3) Manuel Bayani S. Bukas 4) Maria Teresa M. Mina', '1)Kalinisan Chemical Corp., Cavite, Philippines 2)Camarines Norte, Philippines  3)A THPAL Nickel Corp. Surigao Del Norte, Philippines  4) Bicol University College of Engineering, Legazpi City, Albay, Philippines', 'The mining sector is facing increasing demands to adopt environmentally-friendly practices due to growing concerns about the negative impacts of its operations on the environment and local communities. As a result, the industry is actively exploring ways to reduce water usage and improve the handling of wastewater This situation has led to the development of improved mineral processing techniques and the integration of innovative approaches to treating wastewater\nThe utilization of biosorbent, derived from uncharred dry leaves of Ficus nota (blanco) merr, has been found to be a proficient adsorbent for the elimination of nickel (II) from polluted water It was found that in this study the adsorption process is most efficient at pH 4.5 based on the one-factor graph. Percent removal is decreasing beyond pH 7 because of the presence of nickel hydroxide precipitation. It was discovered that a high amount of sorbent dosage increases the percent removal of nickel in the aqueous solution. It was also found that as the initial concentration increases, the percent removal of nickel decreases because the capacity of the treatment process to remove nickel from the wastewater becomes overwhelmed as the concentruuation of nickel increases\nThis study examines the feasibility of using biosorbent made from uncharred dry leaves of Ficus nota (blanco) merr. to remove Nickel (II) from wastewater. The researchers aim to find a different use for uncharred dry leaves of Ficus nota (blanco) merr as a means of reducing waste disposal. The study offers potential solutions to combat Nickel (II) pollution in mining wastewater, while also promoting sustainable waste management practices.\n', 'biosorbent, ficus nota, adsorption', 'Maria Teresa M. Mina', 200, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS174-Maria Teresa M. Mina.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS174-Maria Teresa M. Mina.pdf', '2024-09-04 07:03:36', '2024-10-15 07:46:45'),
(175, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Geotechnical Parametric Estimation and Susceptibility Analysis of Bicol University East Campus, Legazpi City, Albay, Philippines', '1), Anna G. Bilaro, 2 2) Amelia B. Gonzales', 'Bicol University ', 'Aside from natural calamities, susceptibility of the area in terms of geotechnical hazards poses also a major risk in maintaining area’s public safety and its ability to economic development. Infrastructures must be built robustly to achieve sustainable and disaster resilient structures. It is essential that geotechnical aspects of structures be studied to comprehend the substrata. Research study aimed to determine shear strength parameters, evaluate bearing capacity, and assess the susceptibility against liquefaction potential/ or settlement. Field and laboratory tests were conducted determining soil properties. Data analysis was done using correlation and empirical equations alongside with NSCP. Susceptibility to geotechnical hazards were done using SPT and laboratory findings. Soil was consistently determined to be silty sand with varying shear strength for each borehole. Depth and composition of soil varies, with wide range of soil carrying capacity. BH04 had the highest likelihood of liquefaction, FS < 1.0. Settlement analysis shows high value of settlement for BH04. Foundation structure must be built robustly in BH04 to compensate the lower value of bearing capacity and susceptibility to liquefaction and settlement. The significance of thorough geotechnical studies for infrastructure sustainability and disaster resilience are essential for boosting the resilience of infrastructure and encouraging sustainable development. ', 'Geotechnical, borehole, soil properties, settlement, liquefaction', 'AMELIA B. GONZALES', 202, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS175-AMELIA B. GONZALES.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS175-AMELIA B. GONZALES.pdf', '2024-09-05 15:02:24', '2024-10-15 07:53:57'),
(176, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Optimization of Sustainable Empty Fruit Bunches as Heterogeneous Catalysts in Biodiesel Production: A Study of Modifications and Performance', 'Reno Fitriyanti\nSusila Arita\nLeily Nurul Komariah', 'Universitas Sriwijaya\nUniversitas PGRI Palembang', 'The growing demand for renewable energy has increased the need for sustainable catalysts. These catalysts are crucial in biodiesel production as they enhance reaction rates and selectivity, thus improving efficiency and reducing costs. Empty Fruit Bunches (EFB), a form of solid waste from the palm oil industry, are underutilized despite their potential as a valuable resource. This review explores the potential of EFB as a heterogeneous catalyst for biodiesel production. It leverages the high potassium content of EFB as a promising source of K₂O. Processing EFB at low combustion temperatures is essential to preserve potassium. This preservation enhances the catalytic performance. Various modifications have been investigated to further improve EFB’s effectiveness. These include acid and base-impregnated catalysts, magnetic catalysts, bifunctional catalysts, and nano-catalysts. Biodiesel production using EFB catalysts has achieved a conversion rate of 98%. This demonstrates significant potential in supporting the circular bioeconomy. This approach not only  transforms agricultural waste into valuable catalysts but also reduces environmental impact and adds economic value. However, challenges such as optimizing processing conditions, scaling up production, and ensuring consistent catalyst quality remain. Further research into more efficient and cost-effective processing methods is crucial for advancing the use of EFB catalyst in industrial applications.', 'EFB, Catalyst, Biodiesel, Modification, Sustainability', 'RENO FITRIYANTI', 204, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS176-RENO FITRIYANTI.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS176-RENO FITRIYANTI.pdf', '2024-09-08 14:34:29', '2024-09-12 05:20:12'),
(177, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Optimizing Chinese Cabbage (Brassica chinensis var parachinensis) Growth and Yield in Sandy Soils Through Various Viofertilizatio Approaches', 'Syarafina Ratna Putri (ab), Septy Wulan Cahyani (b), Ayu Ratri Wardyaningrum (b), Bella Meyliana Effendi (b).', '(a) Doctoral Program, Faculty of Biology, Universitas Gadjah Mada, Sleman, DI Yogyakarta, Indonesia. \n(b) Department of Microbiology and Product Development Laboratory, PT. Indoraya Mitra Persada 168, Sleman, DI Yogyakarta, Indonesia. \n', 'Agricultural use of sandy soils in Indonesia presents significant challenges due to their low water retention capacity and limited nutrient availability. This study evaluates the impact of various fertilization strategies, particularly focusing on the formulation of Plant Growth-Promoting Rhizobacteria (PGPR), on the growth and yield of Chinese cabbage (Brassica chinensis var. parachinensis) grown in sandy soils. Four fertilization treatments were assessed: conventional chemical fertilizer (NPK), NPK coated with liquid biofertilizer, two formulations of biofertilizer combined with soil amendments, and pure biofertilizer application. The results indicate that the combination of soil amendments with biofertilizer (M) significantly improved all growth parameters, including plant height (45.2 cm), leaf count (33.29 leaves), leaf size (length and width), and flower count. This treatment also achieved the highest plant biomass (196.5 g) and total yield (23,616 kg/200 m²), outperforming the conventional NPK treatment, which had the lowest values in all growth and yield parameters. The integration of soil amendments with biofertilizer (M) enhanced soil microbial activity and nutrient bioavailability, optimizing growth conditions for crops in sandy soils. These findings highlight the need for developing more effective and sustainable fertilization strategies to boost crop productivity in sandy soils while reducing reliance on conventional chemical fertilizers.', 'Chinese cabbage, Sandy soils, Plant Growth-Promoting Rhizobacteria, Biofertilizer, Sustainable agriculture.', 'Syarafina Ratna Putri', 207, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS177-Syarafina Ratna Putri.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS177-Syarafina Ratna Putri.pdf', '2024-09-11 00:39:44', '2024-09-12 05:17:47'),
(178, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of the Effect of Eco Enzyme on the Quality of Leachate from Domestic Solid Waste', '1.Hariestya Viareco\n2. Shally Yanova\n3. Jalius\n4. Muhammad Naswir', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'Leachate is a percolate liquid formed from the dissolution of organic and inorganic materials in waste, which can pollute the environment if not properly managed. Eco enzyme, as an environmentally friendly biotechnology alternative, is claimed to have the potential to enhance waste decomposition processes and reduce leachate pollution. This study focuses on changes in leachate quality resulting from domestic waste treated with eco enzyme solution applied via aerosol. The eco enzyme solution in this study was prepared using fermentation of lime, orange, and lemon peel residues. The domestic solid waste used came from residential sources. The solid waste was placed into two closed containers. One container was sprayed with 500 ml of plain water as a baseline, and the other container was sprayed with 500 ml of eco enzyme solution. Spraying was done three times within 24 hours over a period of 7 days. The leachate quality parameters analyzed included pH and Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD). The biological decomposition process within the eco enzyme affected the reduction of COD levels in the leachate, with an efficiency removal of 28% compared to the control without eco enzyme. This is attributed to the enzymes breaking down the leachate. A decrease in pH was also observed, indicating an increase in biological decomposition. These findings demonstrate the potential of eco enzyme as an effective method for reducing the environmental impact of domestic solid waste and offer a sustainable alternative in waste management. Further research is needed to optimize eco enzyme application conditions and gain a deeper understanding of its mechanisms.', 'Eco enzyme, Leachate, Domestic Solid Waste', 'Hariestya Viareco', 208, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS178-Hariestya Viareco.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS178-Hariestya Viareco.pdf', '2024-09-11 13:53:03', '2024-10-15 07:43:52'),
(179, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Training of using sluice box for separated alluvial gold towards traditionald gold miners', 'Afni Nelvi, Deri Kurniadi, Annisa Intan Yustisia Rahmalina, Khalik Dwika Putra, Mutiara Salvira, Hisni Rahmi', 'Sekolah Tinggi Teknologi Industri Padang, Padang\nUniversitas Jambi, Jambi \n', 'The gold panning technique used by the Sikako Traditional Miners group is still manual, relying on wooden panning tools. As a result, the recovery of gold is low because the separation process is less effective and time-consuming. The sluice box is designed to increase the concentration of gold minerals, allowing it to capture or deposit more gold particles. It is constructed from aluminum and equipped with riffles and a special carpet to trap higher-density material. The goal of this community service is to provide training on using sluice boxes to separate gold from impurities more effectively, resulting in higher recovery rates. This initiative is a collaboration between Mining Engineering and Industrial Engineering lecturers from the Padang College of Industrial Technology (STTIND) and Mining Engineering lecturers from Jambi University. The service activities include constructing the sluice box, training on its use, mentoring, and evaluation. As a result of the training, there was a significant improvement in empowerment, with participants gaining increased knowledge and skills in using the sluice box to separate gold from impurities. In addition, the sluice box is portable so that it is easy to carry everywhere, especially for new location survey activities.', 'Gold Separation, Recovery, Sluice Box', 'Afni Nelvi', 209, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS179-Afni Nelvi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS179-Afni Nelvi.pdf', '2024-09-12 02:15:47', '2024-10-24 10:13:42'),
(180, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Visualization of Clustering and Forecasting the Availability of Waste Disposal Land in North Penajam Paser City and the IKN Sector Using Advanced Analysis Methods and Regression Tests', 'Dwi Arief Prambudi*1), Nia Febrianti 2), Mufid Ubaidillah 3) ', 'kalimantan institute of technology', 'Waste production will continue to increase along with population growth. As the population increases, social needs such as consumption will also increase and this will affect the increasing capacity of storage at Final Processing Sites. According to data from the North Penajam Paser Central Statistics Agency in the 2010 – 2022 population census, the population of North Penajam Paser continues to increase with an average of 3,576 people per year. This population growth will be followed by a large level of waste production in this region. And North Penajam Paser only has one Final Processing Site (TPA). This research uses a linear regression algorithm method to predict the amount of waste growth and estimate the availability of waste disposal land in Penajam Paser Utara until 2029. The estimation results show that total waste production has reached 306,242.4 tons with a population of 1,378,141 people. Further analysis indicates that the estimated remaining waste disposal area has a deficit of 187,161.6 m2. In conclusion, the land area at the Buluminung landfill will not be sufficient to accommodate the projected volume of waste until 2029. Evaluation of the linear regression model using RMSE shows an error rate of 40.27, which provides an illustration of the accuracy of the model predictions.', 'Data Mining, Estimation,Linear Regression, Waste , Landfill / Final Processing Site (TPA).', 'DWI ARIEF PRAMBUDI', 210, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS180-DWI ARIEF PRAMBUDI.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS180-DWI ARIEF PRAMBUDI.pdf', '2024-09-13 03:58:34', '2024-09-13 22:28:01'),
(181, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'The Development of Caffeine Analyzer Device Based on Ultraviolet-Visible (UV-Vis) Spectra Database', 'Sutrisno*1), Samsidar 2), Rahmi1) , M. Ficky Afrianto2), and Lucky Zaher Maulana2),  ', '1) Chemistry, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi,36361, Indonesia\n2) Physics, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi,36361, Indonesia\n', 'Caffeine Analyzer was developed to simplify identifying caffeine content in coffee powder based on coffee variety, roasting methods, and powder size. The UV-Vis test was carried out in this study on 51 Arabica, Liberica, and Robusta coffee powder samples. The results of that measurement are then used as a database programmed in the Caffein Analyzer device. The Caffeine Analyzer apparatus has three parameter systems: pH monitor, mass scale, and TCS 3200 color sensor. In order to identify the caffeine content, the mass of the coffee powder sample must first be determined using a mass balance. Then, the coffee powder is prepared in the box to get the RGB code from the TCS 3200 color sensor. The measurement results are displayed as a percentage (%) and weight (mg) of caffeine content. \n', 'Caffeine; Analyzer; Uv-Vis.', 'Sutrisno', 211, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS181-Samsidar.pdf', '2024-09-13 05:16:31', '2024-09-18 03:14:52'),
(182, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Green Synthesis of Fe3O4 Magnetic Nanoparticles from Iron Sand of The Batanghari River, Jambi Using Pandanus amaryllifolius Extraction ', 'Frastica Deswardani*, Rista Mutia Anggraini, Febri Berthalita Pujaningsih, Aulia Putri Berliana, Lucky Zaehir Maulana', 'Departement of Physics, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia', 'Green synthesis of Fe3O4 magnetic nanoparticles has been carried out from the iron sand of the Batanghari River in Jambi using Pandanus amaryllifolius extract. The extract contins of flavonoids and phenolic acid, where these biomolecules can act as reducing and stabilizing agents in the synthesis of metal nanomaterials. The parameter in this research is to see the effect of variations in the volume of Pandanus amaryllifolius extract on the results of  green synthesis Fe3O4. Variations in extraction volume are 15 mL, 10 mL and 15 mL. The synthesis results were then characterized by XRD (X-Ray Diffractometer) showing the presence of the Spinel Ferrite crystal structure of Fe3O4. The crystal size become smaller as the of Pandanus amaryllifolius extract increase. The FTIR (Forier Transform Infrared) results show the presence of the O-H functional group which indicated the presence of water compounds, apart from that there is also the Fe-O functional group which indicates the presence of Fe3O4 in sample.', 'Fe3O4, FTIR, green synthesis, Pandanus amaryllifolius, XRD', 'Frastica Deswardani', 215, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS182-Frastica Deswardani.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS182-Frastica Deswardani.pdf', '2024-09-14 10:06:16', '2024-10-04 14:17:16'),
(183, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'NETWORK ANALYSIS TO EXPLORE THE ANTI- CANCER  POTENTIAL OF CIPLUKAN LEAVES (PHYSALIS  ANGULATA. L)', 'Silvina faradilla syahrum ', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'Physalin is a secondary metabolite compound that contains withanolides, flavonoids, phenolic acids \r\nand glycosides which have anti-cancer, anti-bacterial and anti-diabetic properties. Therefore, the \r\nphysalin compound can be used as a natural inhibitor. This research aims to identify proteins that can \r\nbe associated with cancer using network analysis pharmacology methods to look at genes associated \r\nwith physalin. The target PASS and OMIM web servers are used to analyze genes that resonate with \r\ncancer, while the STRING web server is used to view target proteins. The final interaction results were \r\nanalyzed using Cytoscape to see the target drug-protein. The results of the research show that one of \r\nthe physalin compounds, namely physalin B, has a higher probability of being active against cervical \r\ncancer than other physalin compounds, namely 0.994 with a molecular weight of 526.53 gr/mol. \r\nPhysalin B may be associated with cervical cancer in the NF-KB treatment pathway. This research can \r\nbe continued with molecular docking methods to validate our research results. The results of this study \r\nare important because they show the potential of physalin B as a strong candidate for cervical cancer \r\ntherapy.', 'Ciplukan, physalin, networking ', 'silvina faradilla ', 217, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS183-silvina faradilla .pdf', '2024-09-14 13:09:24', '2024-09-18 03:16:16'),
(184, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Network Analysis in Exploring Plant Therapeutic Mechanisms Ciplukan (Physalis Angulata Linn) against Type 2 Diabetes', 'nesfitra zakwan zaki', 'universitas jambi', 'Quercetin is a secondary metabolite compound found in Physalis leaves and has potential as\nan anti-diabetic agent. Therefore, quercetin can be used as a natural inhibitor. This study aims\nto identify proteins associated with diabetes using network pharmacology analysis to observe\ngenes associated with quercetin. The PASS target web server and OMIM were used to analyze\ngenes that resonate with quercetin, while the STRING web server was used to identify target\nproteins. The final interaction results were analyzed using Cytoscape to visualize drug-protein\ntargets. The study results indicate that quercetin has a probability of being active against the\nNOX4 protein, with a score of 0.982 and a molecular weight of 346.51 g/mol. This research\ncan be further developed using molecular docking methods to validate our findings. The results\nare significant as they demonstrate the potential of quercetin as a strong candidate for diabetes\ntreatment.\n', ' Quercetin, network analysis pharmasology, diabetes', 'nefitra dzakwan zaki', 218, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS184-nefitra dzakwan zaki.pdf', '2024-09-14 13:27:00', '2024-09-18 03:17:17'),
(185, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Global Profile in Ectrodactyly Research: A Bibliometric Study', 'Firdaus', 'Postgraduate Program, Faculty of Biology UGM, Yogyakarta, Indonesia', 'Congenital abnormalities pose a significant challenge in realizing the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) due to their potential for long-term disability, affecting individuals, health care systems and society. Ectrodactyly, a congenital abnormality characterized by the absence of fingers or toes, has long been studied throughout the world. This study presents a global profile of ectrodactyly research indexed by Scopus and Web of Science with a bibliometric approach using the R package \"bibliometrix\". A total of 1,178 documents related to ectrodactyly were published in 1915-2024, with an average annual scientific production of 10-11 documents. The United States produced the largest number of documents (n=773) among 67 countries, followed by Italy (n=230) and Japan (n=207). The United States also exhibited the largest number of collaborations, followed by Germany and the Netherlands.  Most of the sources and affiliations in the field of ectrodactyly also come from the United States. A central topic in ectrodactyly research is the role of the p63 gene which is responsible for maintaining the Apical Ectodermal Ridge (AER). Apart from “ectrodactyly”, the most frequently used keywords are \"EEC syndrome\", \"p63\", and \"ectodermal dysplasia\". The data presented in this study offer valuable insights that will be useful for further research.', 'bibliometrics, congenital abnormalities, ectrodactyly', 'Firdaus', 219, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS185-Firdaus.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS185-Firdaus.pdf', '2024-09-14 13:32:23', '2024-09-18 03:17:42'),
(186, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Fabrication and Characterization of Hydroxyapatite Scaffold from Channa striata and Scomberomorini commerso Fish Bone as Bone Tissue Engineering', 'Rista Mutia Anggraini (1)\nYoza Fendriani (1)\nMeisa Rohania (1)\nFrastica Deswardani*(1)\n\n*ORCHID IDs: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9225-6593\n\n1)Department of Physics, Universitas Jambi, Jambi 36361, Indonesia\n*email: frastica.deswardani@unja.ac.id', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Hydroxyapatite (HAp) has been successfully obtained from Channa striata and Scomberomorus commerso. HAp scaffold is a material that has the potential to produce bone tissue engineering with characteristics that apply to specific applications. The freeze-drying method can be used to make scaffolds. It minimizes contamination and the possibility of producing carbon resulting from combustion because it uses very low temperatures. Natural polymer materials from corn starch are used to improve the mechanical properties of the scaffold. Variations of PVA were carried out. Characterization was carried out using XRD to see the crystal structure and phase formed, which were adjusted to JCPDS (Joint Committee on Powder Diffraction Standard), SEM-EDX to determine the morphology and scaffold pores diameter, and FTIR to see the functional groups formed. The variations of PVA give different pore diameters. The increased PVA content caused the FTIR absorption results on the scaffold to shift and increase the intensity. Then, the crystallinity of the scaffold is much better, and PVA is increased.\n', 'scaffold, hydroxyapatite, fish bone, freeze-drying', 'Rista Mutia Anggraini', 220, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS186-Rista Mutia Anggraini.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS186-Rista Mutia Anggraini.pdf', '2024-09-14 15:18:56', '2024-10-27 10:54:16'),
(187, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Climate Change Vulnerability of Coastal Communities of Albay Province, Philippines', 'Angelo P. Candelaria, Christian L. Baiño', 'Bicol University, Legazpi City, Albay, Philippines', 'Coastal vulnerability assessment using the Integrated Sensitivity, Exposure, and Adaptive Capacity to Climate Change Vulnerability Assessment (ICSEA-C-Change) tool provides impacts of climate change on coastal zones. Vulnerability ratings used rubrics presented to stakeholders during focused group discussions. Scores were consolidated to generate the overall vulnerability rating. These were based on the status of coastal habitats’ reliance on near-shore fishing and quality measures like fisheries ecosystem dependency, population, and water quality of the coastal habitats. Ratings identified 12 barangays out of 23 that are highly vulnerable to climate change impacts like waves, storm surges, sea level rise, surface temperature increase, and extreme rainfall. Results were highly influenced by absence of three major marine habitats: coral reefs, seagrass/seaweeds, and mangroves. Eleven barangays out of 23 obtained moderate vulnerability scores. This was attributed to the presence of marine habitats that although in poor state, may serve their ecological functioning when properly protected. Highly vulnerable barangays must be prioritized in coastal rehabilitation and disaster risk reduction management planning. Parameters encompassing sensitivity and adaptive capacity must be considered to reduce impacts attributed to climate change. Vital information from the assessment serves as basis for developing plans for improving adaptation strategies of the local government units.', 'Sensitivity, Exposure and Adaptive Capacity', 'ANGELO P. CANDELARIA', 201, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS187-ANGELO P. CANDELARIA.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS187-ANGELO P. CANDELARIA.pdf', '2024-09-14 16:09:32', '2024-09-18 03:15:29'),
(189, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'TESTING', 'COBA VOUCHER', 'UNJA', 'UNJA', 'UNJA', 'testing', 223, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS189-testing.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS189-testing.pdf', '2024-09-30 02:32:23', '2024-09-30 02:33:30'),
(190, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Mathematical Resilience of Elementary School Students in Medan city', 'Suwanto\nHasratuddin\nHermawan Syahputra', 'Universitas Negeri Medan', 'Low mathematics learning outcomes are influenced by many factors both from internal and external students. Mathematical resilience is one of the internal factors that convincingly affects the mathematics learning outcomes of elementary school students. The research wants to describe more deeply the condition of mathematical resilience of elementary school students in Medan City by looking at what hinders and what supports this ability. Therefore, this study is descriptive research and uses test instruments and interviews to collect research data. Forty-four grade 5 primary school students were sampled from five schools in Medan City. The data obtained showed that mathematical resilience ability was moderate, and some were low. The dominant factors were support from people around them and learning media used to learn mathematics. Rewards and guidance from parents/teachers when learning mathematics provide encouragement for students to want to learn mathematics, but because it is not accompanied by appropriate learning media/teaching materials, the struggle and meaningfulness of mathematics is not so visible. So, it needs to be when children learn math need to pay attention to the mental readiness of students and the media to be used.', 'Mathematical Resilience; Elementary School; Mathematics; Education', 'Suwanto', 224, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS190-Suwanto.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS190-Suwanto.pdf', '2024-09-30 04:33:21', '2024-10-04 14:13:56'),
(191, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Utilization of Fiberglass as an Additive in Styrofoam-Based Cellular Lightweight Concrete', 'Yoga Charol Vincenthius Tethool, Zihadah, Radinal Bakri', 'Universitas Papua', 'Lightweight bricks are characterized by reduced mass compared to conventional bricks. Cellular Lightweight Concrete (CLC) is a variant whose drying process occurs naturally or at room temperature. The composition includes cement, sand, water, and a foam agent, with Styrofoam serving as an innovative substitute for the foaming agent at a ratio of 4.2%. Additionally, fiberglasses are incorporated at proportions of 0%, 0.25%, 0.5%, and 0.75% relative to the cement weight, serving as fillers and binders. This research aims to assess the effects of fiberglass incorporation on compressive strength and modulus of elasticity using 20 specimens (dimension 15×15×15 cm) with a curing age of 28 days. The study reveals that the compressive strengths for mixtures with fiberglass proportions of 0%, 0.25%, 0.5%, and 0.75% are 1.75 MPa, 1.79 MPa, 1.42 MPa, and 1.34 MPa, respectively. Correspondingly, the modulus of elasticity values are 3 MPa, 3.34 MPa, 2.43 MPa, and 2.02 MPa. Notably, the addition of fiberglass at 0.25% enhances compressive strength, whereas higher ratios result in decreased strength due to mixing difficulties and non-homogeneity.', 'cellular lightweight concrete, styrofoam, fiberglass', 'Yoga Charol Vincenthius Tethool', 222, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS191-Yoga Charol Vincenthius Tethool.pdf', '2024-09-30 11:32:43', '2024-10-01 22:15:07'),
(192, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Estimation of Carbon Stock in Halodule pinifolia at Andai Beach, Manokwari, West Papua', 'Sisilia N. Y. Mudarehi1), Agatha C. Maturbongs*2), Fajar R. D. N. Sianipar3), Johanis P. Kilmaskossu4), Paskalina Th. Lefaan5), Simon Sutarno6)', 'Universitas Papua', 'Seagrasses are underwater vegetation that possess a high capacity for absorbing and storing carbon dioxide, significantly contributing to the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions. The aim of this study is to calculate the density, biomass, and estimate the carbon content and carbon stock of Halodule pinifolia in Andai Beach Manokwari. The method employed was purposive sampling, utilizing three transects at each station, with quadrats measuring 30 cm. The density of Halodule pinifolia was determined by counting the number of shoots divided by the observation area. Biomass was obtained by weighing the dry weight of the seagrass. Carbon analysis was conducted using the ashing method for each quadrat at the three stations. The results showed that the density of H. pinifolia ranged from 942 to 3654 individuals/m². The above-substrate biomass of H. pinifolia ranged from 15.11 to 43.19 g dry weight/m², while the below-substrate biomass ranged from 18.48 to 86.41 g dry weight/m². The estimated carbon content of H. pinifolia was approximately 19.14 to 58.88% for the above-substrate and 22.42 to 128.17% for the below-substrate. The estimated carbon stock of H. pinifolia was between 663.63 and 2059.64 gC/m² for the above-substrate and ranged from 955.07 to 1842.75 gC/m² for the below-substrate.', 'Biomass, Carbon stock, Halodule pinifolia, Seagrass, West Papua', 'Agatha Cecilia Maturbongs', 226, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS192-Agatha Cecilia Maturbongs.pdf', '2024-09-30 13:20:32', '2024-10-01 22:17:55'),
(193, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Antihyperglycemic Activity of Kombucang (Sappanwood Kombucha) against Alloxan-Induced Mice (Mus musculus) ', 'Ashif Irvan Yusuf, Ahmad Sazali, Hasna Ul Maritsa', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'The research focuses on evaluating the antihyperglycemic potential of Kombucang (Kombucha from sappanwood) on mice (Mus musculus) induced with alloxan to stimulate diabetes. Diabetes mellitus is a global health problem that requires breakthroughs in the development of effective and affordable alternative therapies. Sappanwood is known to have antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties that may contribute to managing blood glucose levels. The purpose of this study is to determine the effectiveness of Kombucang in lowering blood glucose levels in a diabetic mouse model. The study used a completely randomized design (CRD) with 3 dosage treatments: 0.26 ml/20g body weight (P1), 0.52 ml/20g body weight (P2), and 0.78 ml/20g body weight (P3), and 3 control treatments: normal control without treatment (K), positive control given alloxan (KP), and negative control given alloxan and glibenclamide (KN). Each treatment consisted of 5 replications with a treatment duration of 14 days. The results showed that administration of Kombucang at a dose of 0.52 ml/20g body weight was able to lower the blood sugar levels of alloxan-induced mice close to the normal control, and as effectively as the administration of glibenclamide as a negative control. This indicates that Kombucang has potential as an alternative therapy for diabetes patients.', 'kombucha, sappanwood, antihyperglycemic, glucose level, therapy', 'Ashif Irvan Yusuf ', 228, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS193-Ashif Irvan Yusuf .pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS193-Ashif Irvan Yusuf .pdf', '2024-09-30 15:33:45', '2024-10-04 14:16:28'),
(194, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Computational Insights into CO₂ Binding Energy with a New Iron(II) Vinylic Polyamine Complex for Exploratory CO₂ Reduction Studies', 'Muhammad Irhash Shalihin', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Catalytic carbon dioxide (CO₂) reduction proposes solutions to many environmental concerns. So far, iron-quaterpyridine complexes are among the forefront of the best candidates for CO₂-to-CO conversion. Herein, a vinylic polyamine, (1Z,1\'Z)-N1,N1\'-((Z)-ethene-1,2-diyl)bis(ethene-1,2-diamine) (ZE-DiAm) is proposed as a new ligand alternative to quaterpyridine, which offers less structural complexity and comparable ligand position. Notably, ZE-DiAm also possesses higher basicity, which leads to a more stable complex. In this study, the theoretical binding energy of CO₂ with the iron-ZE-DiAm complex, represented as [Fe(II)(ZE-DiAm)MeCN]²⁺, was calculated using density functional theory (DFT). The binding energy was found to be 262.262 kJ/mol which is significantly higher than the typical binding energies calculated for CO₂ interactions with iron complexes that generally range from 33.890 kJ/mol to 125.520 kJ/mol. It shows that the complex [Fe(II)(ZE-DiAm)MeCN]²⁺ forms a more stable bond with CO₂, enhancing its potential as a catalyst in the activation of CO₂. This investigation therefore lays the platform for further laboratory work on the catalytic properties of [Fe(II)(ZE-DiAm)MeCN]²⁺, aiming to facilitate the reductive transformations of CO₂ to CO and other reduced products.', 'CO₂ Conversion, Iron Complexes, ZE-DiAm, DFT Analysis, Binding Energy', 'Muhammad Irhash Shalihin', 221, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS194-Muhammad Irhash Shalihin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS194-Muhammad Irhash Shalihin.pdf', '2024-09-30 15:34:35', '2024-10-24 10:10:17'),
(195, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of the Model\'s Fitness of the Modern Test Theory Model in Measuring the Ethnomathematics Knowledge of Prospective Mathematics Teachers: Jambi Cultural Context ', '1) Ilham Falani \n2) Yelli Ramalisa \n3) Muhammad Adi Saputra', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Ethnomathematics knowledge is essential for a teacher to implement ethnomathematics-based learning successfully. This study aims to test the modern test theory model\'s fitness in measuring prospective mathematics teachers\' ethnomathematics knowledge in the context of Jambi Culture. This study is an initial test that needs to be done before conducting an analysis using modern test theory. This study is a descriptive statistical study involving several testing methods, namely the Chi-square test, Likelihood Ratio Test (LRT), Information Criteria (AIC / BIC), Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC), and Item Fit Statistics. The research data were collected using an ethnomathematics test of seven indicators derived into 43 items. Scoring was done using the dichotomy scoring technique. This study\'s modern test theory models included 1PL, 2PL, 3 PL, and Rasch. Based on the test results, the Rasch Model showed the highest suitability compared to other models. Therefore, the researcher recommends this model as a further analysis model in measuring ethnomathematics knowledge of prospective mathematics teachers: Jambi Cultural Context. Furthermore, this study can provide an overview of further research related to testing the fitness of the modern test theory model.', 'Good of fitness, modern test theory model, Ethnomathematics', 'Dr. Ilham Falani, S.Pd., M.Si.', 225, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS195-Muhammad Adi Saputra.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS195-Muhammad Adi Saputra.pdf', '2024-10-01 03:24:14', '2024-10-15 07:48:59'),
(196, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Analytical Method Verification for Quantification of Ethylene Glycol and Diethylene Glycol in Sorbitol by GC-FID', 'Ayu Nadila Safitri 1 *1), Irham Fauzi Abshar 2 2), Khairul Ridwan 3 3)', 'Department of Chemistry, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi\n\n', 'Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol commonly used as a sweetener and humectant in various pharmaceutical formulations, especially for pediatric syrup medicines this formula is designed specifically for children to treat various health conditions. An analytical method for determining ethylene glycol and diethylene glycol in sorbitol was developed and validated according to current legislation. This work uses the liquid-liquid extraction sample preparation method to determine the polar ethylene glycol and diethylene glycol in liquid samples. This method includes the application of sample extraction with the ratio of acetone and water, vortexing, and standing times on the extraction followed by quantification using a gas chromatography-flame ionization detector. Analytical performances of the method were satisfactory, showed good linearity in the selected range (R2 > 0.999), and the average value of s/n limits of quantification (LOQ) for EG and DEG were 11.299 and 18.792. Average recoveries in the ranges of 93.9-99.5% (RSD, 1.1-2.4%) for EG, and 97.3-105.5% (RSD, 1.0-2.8%) for DEG were obtained appropriately. The specificity of the method is also good because it is able to analyze compounds without interference from other components. Finally, the present method was applied for the determination of ethylene glycol and diethylene glycol in sorbitol solutions, proving to be reliable.', 'Sorbitol, Ethylene glycol, Diethylene glycol, Analytical method, Gas Chromatography', 'Ayu Nadila Safitri', 229, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS196-Ayu Nadila Safitri.pdf', '2024-10-01 15:20:13', '2024-10-01 22:14:37'),
(197, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Modification of Eco-Enzyme Production Based on Pineapple Peel Waste (Ananas comosus) with the Addition of Ganoderma lucidum as an Antidermatophytic Agent', 'Hasna Ul Maritsa , Anggari Linda Destiana , Uni Baroroh Husnuddin ', 'Biology Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Jambi', 'Pineapple peel waste from Tangkit has not been optimally utilized and is still often considered as waste. One effort to improve its management is by producing a bioproduct known as eco-enzyme. The production process of eco-enzyme typically requires up to 3 months of fermentation; therefore, an inducer is needed to help accelerate the degradation of Tangkit pineapple peel into this bioproduct. Besides potentially shortening the fermentation time, the bioactivity of the eco-enzyme, particularly as an antimycotic agent, could also be enhanced. This is because Ganoderma lucidum has anti-inflammatory and anti-allergic properties. The aim of this study is to assess the effect of adding the fungus Ganoderma lucidum as a decomposer agent in the production of eco-enzyme based on Tangkit pineapple peel waste and to determine the antifungal ability of the modified Tangkit pineapple peel waste eco-enzyme against dermatophyte fungi. The research method used is an experimental study with qualitative descriptive data. The eco-enzyme is combined with G. lucidum and compared to a control (without G. lucidum) to test its ability to inhibit Trichophyton rubrum using concentration variations of 0%, 20%, 40%, 60%, 80%, and 100% with incubation periods of 15 days and 45 days on PDA media. The results show that in the 15-day fermentation period, the modified eco-enzyme was not able to inhibit the growth of T. rubrum. However, with a 45-day incubation period at all concentration variations (except in the control), Trichophyton was unable to grow on this modified substrate. This indicates that the modification using G. lucidum can act as an antidermatophytic agent against T. rubrum', 'Eco enzymes Ananas comosus, Trycophyton rubrum, bioactivity microbial', 'HASNA UL MARITSA', 230, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS197-HASNA UL MARITSA.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS197-HASNA UL MARITSA.pdf', '2024-10-01 15:29:26', '2024-10-27 03:24:55'),
(198, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Index of Cultural Significance of Useful Plants in the Kenduri SKO Traditional Ceremony of the Kerinci Community, Hamparan Rawang District, Sungai Penuh City, Jambi Province', 'Ade Adriadi, Revis Asra, Fitra Wahyuni', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The indigenous people of Kerinci are called the \"Kerinci Tribe\". The population of this tribe is around 300,000 people\nwith a clustered village pattern. The Kerinci tribe is known to have rules of life\nwhich has been around for hundreds of years. Administratively, Kerinci has experienced expansion into\n2 Regency and City areas, namely Kerinci Regency and Sungai Banyak City. Administration has been completed\nseparate, but in terms of customs and culture, both are still in the same family, namely the Kerinci Tribe.\nOne of the sub-districts in Sungai Banyak is Hamparan Rawang Sub-district which has\nculture and various traditions that are still preserved, one of which is traditional ceremonies. Wrong\nOne traditional ceremony in Hamparan Rawang District is the Kenduri Sko Traditional Ceremony.', 'Ethnobotany, Kenduri sko', 'Ade Adriadi', 234, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS198-Ade Adriadi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS198-Ade Adriadi.pdf', '2024-10-03 07:40:43', '2024-10-23 04:40:03'),
(199, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of Students\' Scientific Literacy Abilities in Conservation Biology Courses', 'Ade Adriadi, Dawam Suprayogi, Andini Vermita Bestari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Conservation biology is a subject that has high relevance, especially to conditions\nIndonesia is currently experiencing a massive loss of diversity from year to year1\n. Effort\nMuch development of this course has been carried out, including the integration of lecture-based courses\nlocal wisdom 2\n; mini research activities3\n; learning species identification response4\n; integrate\nwith religious values ​​5\n; contextual learning6\n; learning standing banner model7\n; as well as\ndevelopment of a citizen Science Project-based conservation biology lecture program8\n. All this effort\nThis is done so that learning in conservation biology courses becomes more meaningful, meaning science\nWhat students learn in conservation biology courses can be applied in their lives. Wrong\nOne important ability that students must have in conservation biology courses is\nscientific literacy skills. Scientific literacy is a person\'s ability to understand science,\ncommunicating science, as well as applying scientific knowledge to solve problems so that\nhave a high attitude and sensitivity towards themselves and their environment in making decisions\nbased on scientific considerations9\n. The concept of scientific literacy expects students to have a sense\nhigh concern for oneself and one\'s environment in facing life\'s problems\neveryday life and make decisions based on the scientific knowledge they understand10\n. For\ncan produce students who have good scientific literacy skills in courses\nConservation biology requires data to determine the extent of students\' scientific literacy abilities\nthe. Therefore, this research will focus on analyzing scientific literacy abilities\nstudents in conservation biology courses', 'Biology Conservation, Literation', 'Ade Adriadi', 234, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS199-Ade Adriadi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS199-Ade Adriadi.pdf', '2024-10-03 08:11:22', '2024-10-23 04:40:28'),
(200, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'Implementasi Implementation Pada On Suatu a Benda Thing', 'All Authors', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Lorem ipsum odor amet, consectetuer adipiscing elit. Blandit sodales aliquam egestas at parturient magna aliquet. Eleifend consectetur consequat ligula vulputate cursus velit integer duis molestie. Torquent sem bibendum; eleifend urna molestie purus gravida. Lacinia posuere ex fermentum at id maximus sollicitudin finibus bibendum. Porta porta nulla ullamcorper pharetra bibendum. Leo ultrices posuere ligula nulla erat blandit. Mauris lectus sagittis consequat finibus torquent id cubilia arcu. Porta iaculis metus libero tellus aenean diam aliquet nisl.', 'Lorem ipsum odor amet, consectetuer adipiscing elit.', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 197, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS200-Mukhtada Nasution.pdf', '2024-10-03 08:42:54', '2024-10-23 14:54:21'),
(201, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'The Adsorption of Heavy Metals Ions from Green Synthesis Nanoparticles Fe3O4  based on Pleomele Angustifolia from the Batanghari River Iron Sand as Water Purifier', 'Lucky Zaehir Maulana *, Frastica Deswardani, Rista Mutia Anggraini, Febri Berthalita Pujaningsih, and Muhammad Ficky Afrianto, Ade Malemna, and Lamria Br Galingging', 'Department of Physics, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi', 'Since the ancient era, the river is the center of the civilization. Jambi is also famous due to the long line Batanghari river. Most of the economy comes from the exploration of the sediment. Those sediments have an abundance of minerals. Iron oxide is one promising mineral that can be synthesized into nanoparticle magnetic Fe3O4 (Magnetite). On the other side, wet methods, which are common methods, need a lot of chemical solutions. Here, we successfully synthesize magnetic nanoparticles based on green synthesis incorporating Pleomele Angustifolia as the green agent. The end product of this research will utilize the nanoparticles as a water purifier. For that purpose, we characterize our sample with several methods, such as UV-Vis, FTIR, and XRD. The results show that our nanoparticle Fe3O4 is promising as a water purifier adsorbent.', 'Green synthesis, pleomele angustifolia, magnetite, heavy metal.', 'Lucky Zaehir Maulana', 212, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS201-Lucky Zaehir Maulana.pdf', NULL, '2024-10-03 09:07:17', '2024-10-04 14:17:43');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(202, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Development of Air Manjunto Watershed Flood Inundation Model USING HEC-RAS', 'Gusta Gunawan', 'University of Bengkulu, Civil Engineering Departement, ', 'Abstract. This study presents the development of a flood inundation model using HEC-RAS for the Manjunto Watershed, aiming to enhance predictive accuracy and flood management strategies. Employing a robust mathematical simulation approach, the model integrates peak discharge predictions for a 50-year return period, derived from the synthetic unit hydrograph (SUH) ITB-1 and Nakayasu methods. The HEC-RAS outputs are further refined into detailed flood inundation maps using Arc-GIS 10.8. Comparative analysis of flow rates from the Nakayasu and ITB-1 models against observed data reveals peak flows of approximately 1,400 m³/s, with Nakayasu predicting higher peaks around 1,600 m³/s, and ITB-1 lower peaks around 1,100 m³/s. The spatial analysis of flood depths within the Manjunto Watershed offers critical insights into flood risk distribution, markedly influenced by topographical variations. The resulting flood depth maps delineate high, moderate, and low-risk zones, providing essential data for targeted flood mitigation efforts in Mukomuko Regency, Bengkulu Province. This research underscores the imperative for precise, localized flood management strategies to mitigate future flood risks effectively.', 'Flood, SUH, HEC-RAS, Manjunto Watershed, GIS', 'Gusta Gunawan', 233, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS202-Gusta Gunawan.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS202-Gusta Gunawan.pdf', '2024-10-03 09:08:13', '2024-10-07 06:52:36'),
(203, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'test', 'tes', 'unja', 'unja', '123', 'testing1', 237, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS203-testing1.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS203-testing1.pdf', '2024-10-03 14:47:12', '2024-10-03 15:13:08'),
(204, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Preliminary Study of a Room Light Intensity, Temperature, and Humidity Measurement System Based on DHT11 and BH1750 Sensors', 'Jesi Pebralia*1), Yoza Fendriani 1), Muhammad Ficky Afrianto 1)', 'Physics Department, Jambi University, Indonesia', 'Indoor light intensity, temperature, and humidity are among the physical parameters that can affect indoor air quality as regulated by the Minister of Health Regulation (PERMENKES) of the Republic of Indonesia No. 2 of 2023. In this study, a preliminary investigation of a light intensity measurement system based on the BH1750 sensor and a temperature measurement system based on the DHT11 sensor was conducted. The purpose of this research is to evaluate the performance of the sensor-based measurement system. The research method used is the research and development method with a waterfall model. Data collection was carried out in a meeting room. The results of the study show that the sensor-based measurement system works well, with an accuracy level of 90.13% and an error rate of 9.87%.', 'BH1750 sensor, DHT11 sensor, humidity, indoor light intensity, temperature', 'Jesi Pebralia', 240, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS204-Jesi Pebralia.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS204-Jesi Pebralia.pdf', '2024-10-04 09:10:12', '2024-10-15 07:48:27'),
(205, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Identification of the Distribution of Gadang House in Sijunjung Traditional Village, Nagari Padang Ranah', 'Ariyati*1), Nadya Bestnissa2), Al Busya Fuadi3), Soni Pratomo4)', '1,3) Prodi Arsitektur, Fakultas Teknik dan Perencanaan,Universitas Bung Hatta, Indonesia\n2,4) Prodi Arsitektur, Fakultas Teknik dan Ilmu Komputer, Universitas Adiwangsa Jambi, Indonesia', 'This paper critically examines how Rumah Gadang Architecture refers to the physical, material and cultural relationship between the tectonic aspects of the building and the place where the building is located. The research aims to identify the distribution of Rumah Gadang and provide a living understanding of the means, criteria, methods, and tools used to find out the distribution pattern of Rumah Gadang. This paper focuses on the Sijunjung Traditional village, Nagari Padang Ranah, based on the characteristics of its location. Nagari Padang Ranah is located in the hills with slope terrain, surrounding environment and climate. The analysis of the constructed work includes documentation and comparative investigation of the identification of damage to the foundation building elements and connections, as well as the dimensions and proportions of the building elements, their spacing, and the overall arrangement of the building. The research will also uncover the logical reasons and abstract meanings behind construction decisions to select materials, tools and methods to alter the soil concerning the building. Ultimately, this paper will contribute to a new and critical approach to understanding how the people of Nagari Padang Ranah have traditionally connected themselves to the place itself.', 'distribution, Gadang House, identify, Sijunjung', 'Nadya Bestnissa and Soni Pratomo', 242, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS205-Soni Pratomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS205-Soni Pratomo.pdf', '2024-10-07 06:37:27', '2024-10-15 07:49:32'),
(206, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Identification of the Distribution of Gadang House in Sijunjung Traditional Village, Nagari Padang Ranah', 'Ariyati*1), Nadya Bestnissa2), Al Busya Fuadi3), Soni Pratomo4)', '1,3) Prodi Arsitektur, Fakultas Teknik dan Perencanaan,Universitas Bung Hatta, Indonesia\n2,4) Prodi Arsitektur, Fakultas Teknik dan Ilmu Komputer, Universitas Adiwangsa Jambi, Indonesia', 'This paper critically examines how Rumah Gadang Architecture refers to the physical, material and cultural relationship between the tectonic aspects of the building and the place where the building is located. The research aims to identify the distribution of Rumah Gadang and provide a living understanding of the means, criteria, methods, and tools used to find out the distribution pattern of Rumah Gadang. This paper focuses on the Sijunjung Traditional village, Nagari Padang Ranah, based on the characteristics of its location. Nagari Padang Ranah is located in the hills with slope terrain, surrounding environment and climate. The analysis of the constructed work includes documentation and comparative investigation of the identification of damage to the foundation building elements and connections, as well as the dimensions and proportions of the building elements, their spacing, and the overall arrangement of the building. The research will also uncover the logical reasons and abstract meanings behind construction decisions to select materials, tools and methods to alter the soil concerning the building. Ultimately, this paper will contribute to a new and critical approach to understanding how the people of Nagari Padang Ranah have traditionally connected themselves to the place itself.', 'distribution, Gadang House, identify, Sijunjung', 'Nadya Bestnissa and Soni Pratomo', 242, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS206-Soni Pratomo.pdf', '2024-10-07 06:40:10', '2024-10-07 07:07:57'),
(207, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'A Comprehensive Guide to Writing Engineering Journal Articles', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Lorem ipsum odor amet, consectetuer adipiscing elit. Nunc suspendisse praesent maecenas condimentum parturient elementum. Amet parturient litora amet leo nibh. Facilisis pretium convallis potenti mus felis. Fusce vestibulum consectetur nostra parturient nullam venenatis egestas volutpat finibus. Magna nisi risus parturient gravida etiam. Faucibus nibh pretium amet vitae amet maecenas. Tristique blandit nec nibh inceptos natoque quam lectus porttitor.\n\nTristique sapien vehicula dictumst, malesuada habitant condimentum ad convallis tempor. Aliquet odio etiam laoreet aenean sem, taciti libero ultrices integer. Auctor habitant integer tincidunt integer tellus dictum. Phasellus condimentum ad torquent dolor enim adipiscing phasellus. Vitae aptent viverra id phasellus aliquam bibendum sed. Natoque nunc ex porttitor suspendisse volutpat elementum urna metus. Tellus donec ligula morbi vehicula blandit imperdiet parturient in phasellus. Venenatis molestie velit aliquam facilisis molestie himenaeos vitae.\n\nVarius diam imperdiet euismod sem felis aliquam magnis bibendum. Nam consectetur mattis ullamcorper facilisi est bibendum nostra facilisis? Augue id volutpat sed vitae rhoncus ullamcorper velit dictum. Fusce placerat ex interdum mollis montes pulvinar. Pretium ante nostra at ultrices sit magna; netus primis. Velit natoque placerat volutpat taciti nostra placerat. Viverra placerat lobortis interdum velit vehicula ultrices inceptos imperdiet amet. Mattis ex interdum auctor etiam felis; commodo maecenas luctus.', 'Lorem ipsum odor amet, consectetuer adipiscing elit. Nunc suspendisse ', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 197, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS207-Mukhtada Nasution.pdf', '2024-10-07 15:32:24', '2024-10-23 14:53:00'),
(208, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'A Comprehensive Guide to Writing Engineering Journal Articles Artikel', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 'Universitas Jambi', 'efwrgoion rgopnioegr', 'qroemwgo weofngn', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 197, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, NULL, '2024-10-09 12:22:18', '2024-10-23 14:52:10'),
(209, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Paper-Based Sensor for Colorimetric Detection of Creatinine Using  Silver Nanoparticle ', 'Martina Asti Rahayu,1* Indra Lasmana Tarigan2, Munifilia Eka Sari3, Yolanda Wulandari4', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Creatinine is a clinically significant analyte used to diagnose kidney conditions. The purpose of this study was to develop a sensor for the detection of creatinine using filter paper immobilized by silver nanoparticles (AgNPs). Colorimetric detection of creatinine was developed based on the AgNPs aggregation. The average diameter of the synthesized AgNPs was estimated to be around 47,1±0,549 nm. The method is based on the colorimetric reaction between creatinine and silver nanoparticles, where the solution color changes from yellow to dark blue. Its assembly method includes cutting paper discs arranged into several reading units within a plastic pouch, enabling effective creatinine quantification. Under optimal conditions, the developed method allowed creatinine quantification in the dynamic range of 0,5-10 mg/dL, with a limit of detection (LOD) of 0,34 mg/dL and a limit of quantification (LOQ) of 1,13 mg/dL. The color intensity developed was processed in ImageJ software, based on digital image scanning, performed in 10 minutes after the sample insertion. The device is stable for up to one week when stored in a vacuum at 4ºC. The proposed method could offer simple, rapid, and inexpensive creatinine analysis for clinical diagnosis', 'creatinine, paper-based sensor, colorimetric detection, silver nanoparticle', 'Martina Asti Rahayu', 246, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS209-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS209-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', '2024-10-09 13:29:42', '2024-10-21 14:30:53'),
(210, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Nutrient Status As A Basic Diagnostic For Revealing Micro-Macroorganism Community of Tropical River: Case Study Batanghari River Indonesia ', 'Tedjo Sukmono1, Tri Suryono2, Dyah Muji Rahayu3*, Martina Asti Rahayu4, Sarwo Edy Wibowo5, Bs Monica Arfiana3, Ester Restiana Endang Gelis3, Wulandari3, Muhammad Harisky3', 'Harisky3\n1Biology Department, Science and Technology Faculty, Jambi University, Jambi 36361, Indonesia\n2National Research and Innovation Agency, BRIN, Gedung B.J. Habibie, Jakarta Pusat 10340, Indonesia\n3Fisheries Department, Animal Husbandry Faculty, Jambi University, Jambi 36361, Indonesia\n4Chemistry Department, Science and Technology Faculty, Jambi University, Jmabi 36361, Indonesia\n5Animal Health Department, Animal Husbandry Faculty, Jambi University, Jambi 36361, Indonesia\n', 'Increasing human activities such as agriculture, settlements, and fish farming have contributed to nutrient inputs that have led to a decline in the quality of the Batanghari River. The aim was to determine the upstream-to-downstream status that occurs with different human activities along the Batanghari River based on nutrients and how it can predict the structure of micro-macroorganisms in this river. The study was conducted during the dry season and divided into six stations. The results of the Pollution Index (PIj) showed that the condition of the Batanghari River was slightly polluted with nutrients. The highest nutrient was TOM with a range of 77,38 – 162,54 mg/l in the water column and the highest nutrient in the sediment was TN with a range of 1,56 – 5,88 mg/l. Based on the comparison with water quality standards, the concentrations of TP, TN, and ammonium exceeded the quality standards, especially in the middle and downstream zones with unfit categories for Class I and Class II. The community structure predicted by these conditions is that the eukaryote group is dominated by the kingdom Animalia, while the protist group is dominated by bacteria, especially N, P, and ammonium degraders. The high level of nutrients in the Batanghari River is a result of the high load of organic waste entering the waters and is worrying nutrient pollution in the Batanghari River as a source of water and biotic life.\n', 'Batanghari River; Nutrients; Upstream to downstream; Water and sediment', 'Tedjo Sukmono', 247, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS210-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', '2024-10-10 03:11:37', '2024-10-15 01:47:27'),
(211, 'science', 'oral presentation', ' Monitoring Fish and Their Habitats in Klari Lake in the Muara Jambi  Cultural Heritage Area After the Flood', 'Tedjo Sukmono *1), Yoppie Wulanda 2), Lauura Hermala Yunita, \nTri Syukria3), Rizki Janatul Magwa2), Pradita Eko Prasetyo4)\n', '1) Department of Biology,Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n2) Department of Fisheries, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n3) Environmental Engineering, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n4)Department of Information System, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n', 'Research was conducted at Lake Klari on 16-21 July 2024, which is located in the Muaro Jambi National Cultural Heritage area,. In late 2023 and early 2024, a severe flood affected much of Jambi Province, including the KCBN area, resulting in considerable changes in the connectivity between water bodies. These changes impacted fish distribution and abundance, prompting the need for post-flood monitoring to assess the ecosystem and biodiversity status in Danau Klari.,,The primary objective of this research was to evaluate the post-flood diversity of fish and the condition of their habitats in Danau Klari. Sampling was carried out at three distinct points using a range of capture methods, including gill nets and angling. The findings revealed 48 fish from 8 species, most of which were native to the area. Analysis of water quality showed that the environmental conditions remained suitable for fish survival, with a temperature of 31.6°C, dissolved oxygen (DO) at 4.1 ppt, and pH at 6.77.\nThe study demonstrates that Danau Klari ecological condition remains favorable for supporting fish biodiversity, despite the significant impacts of recent flooding\n', 'Keywords: Danau Klari, Monitoring,KCBN, Biodiversity.', 'Tedjo Sukmono', 247, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS211-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', '2024-10-10 16:39:03', '2024-10-15 01:47:55'),
(212, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Development of Android-Based Mathematics Learning Media Using Powerpoint and I-Spring Suite on the Matter of Integers in Class VII UPTD SMP Negeri 11 Kupang', 'Marselina Sarlince Tahu*1), Siprianus Suban Garak2), Imelda Hendriani Eku Rimo3)', 'Mathematics Education, Nusa Cendana University, Indonesia', 'The rapid development of science and technology requires us to be more active in learning how to use technology to help us solve everyday problems, especially in the field of education. Android-based smartphones are one of the products of modern technology that can be utilized as media in learning activities. Therefore, this study aims to develop android-based mathematics learning media using Powerpoint and I-Spring suite on integer material in class VII UPTD SMP Negeri 11 Kupang which is valid, practical and effective. The type of research used in this study is Research and Development (R&D) with the ADDIE model which consists of 5 stages, namely Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, and Evaluation. The data collection techniques used are interviews, questionnaires and tests. The data analysis techniques used are qualitative and quantitative analysis.The results showed that android-based learning media on whole number material met the criteria of valid, practical and effective. The average total validity (RTV) obtained from 3 material and media experts is 4.32 and 4.25 with a very valid category. The average total practicality (RTP) from teachers was 4.12 with a practical category and small and large class students were 4.8 and 4.41 with a very practical category, respectively. The percentage of learning completeness of small class students was 80% and the large class obtained a percentage of completeness of 75% so that the learning media was categorized as effective. ', 'Development, Android-Based Learning Media, Integers', 'Marselina Sarlince Tahu', 250, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS212-Marselina Sarlince Tahu.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS212-Marselina Sarlince Tahu.pdf', '2024-10-12 04:30:59', '2024-10-24 00:30:06'),
(213, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Development of Android-Based Mathematics Learning Media Using Powerpoint and I-Spring Suite on the Matter of Integers in Class VII UPTD SMP Negeri 11 Kupang', 'Marselina Sarlince Tahu*1), Siprianus Suban Garak2), Imelda Hendriani Eku Rimo3)', 'Mathematics Education, Nusa Cendana University, Indonesia', 'The rapid development of science and technology requires us to be more active in learning how to use technology to help us solve everyday problems, especially in the field of education. Android-based smartphones are one of the products of modern technology that can be utilized as media in learning activities. Therefore, this study aims to develop android-based mathematics learning media using Powerpoint and I-Spring suite on integer material in class VII UPTD SMP Negeri 11 Kupang which is valid, practical and effective. The type of research used in this study is Research and Development (R&D) with the ADDIE model which consists of 5 stages, namely Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, and Evaluation. The data collection techniques used are interviews, questionnaires and tests. The data analysis techniques used are qualitative and quantitative analysis.The results showed that android-based learning media on whole number material met the criteria of valid, practical and effective. The average total validity (RTV) obtained from 3 material and media experts is 4.32 and 4.25 with a very valid category. The average total practicality (RTP) from teachers was 4.12 with a practical category and small and large class students were 4.8 and 4.41 with a very practical category, respectively. The percentage of learning completeness of small class students was 80% and the large class obtained a percentage of completeness of 75% so that the learning media was categorized as effective. ', 'Development, Android-Based Learning Media, Integers', 'Marselina Sarlince Tahu', 250, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS213-Marselina Sarlince Tahu.pdf', '2024-10-12 05:30:06', '2024-10-24 00:29:53'),
(214, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'Comparative Analysis of Triangulation Methods for Optimal Solutions to the Art Gallery Problem', 'Jefri Marzal, Niken Rarasati, Akhiyar waladi  , Yogi Perdana , Hasanatul.Iftititah, Nindy Raisahanum ', 'Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi Universitas Jambi', 'Triangulation, the process of partitioning a polygon into triangles, is a critical step in determining the optimal placement and number of guards in the Art Gallery Problem (AGP). Among the various triangulation methods, Ear Clipping and Minimum Weight are two of the most widely used approaches. However, the difference between these methods in terms of the number of guards required compared to the upper bound of ⌊n/3⌋ guards remains unknown. This study aims to develop an AGP simulation application and evaluate the difference in guard counts for both triangulation methods against the theoretical upper bound. A total of 228 polygons, with vertex counts ranging from 10 to 110, were tested. The polygons were classified into three groups based on the ratio of convex to concave vertices: fewer convex vertices, equal convex and concave vertices, and more convex vertices. Simulation results indicate that the Ear Clipping method consistently produces fewer guards compared to the Minimum Weight method, with average deviations from the upper bound of 11.8% and 6.9%, respectively. These findings are further validated by consistently lower guard counts across all polygon groups, highlighting the greater efficiency of the Ear Clipping method in minimizing guard requirements.', 'triangulation, ear clipping, minimum weight, Art Gallery Problem', 'Jefri Marzal', 239, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS214-Jefri Marzal.pdf', '2024-10-15 03:00:38', '2024-10-15 14:04:56'),
(215, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Hydrothermal impregnation of Nickel metal into activated carbon from palm fiber and its application as a catalyst for the cracking of used cooking oil into biofuel', 'Nazarudin, Ulyarti, Hadistya Suryadri', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Hydrothermal impregnation of Nickel metal into activated carbon from palm fiber and its application as a catalyst for the cracking of used cooking oil into biofuel', 'Hydrothermal', 'Nazarudin', 223, 'not yet reviewed', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS215-Nazarudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS215-testing.pdf', '2024-10-15 13:38:32', '2024-10-15 13:59:11'),
(216, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Hydrothermal impregnation of Nickel metal into activated carbon from palm fiber and its application as a catalyst for the cracking of used cooking oil into biofuel', 'Nazarudin, Ulyarti, Hadistya Suryadri', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Hydrothermal impregnation of Nickel metal into activated carbon from palm fiber and its application as a catalyst for the cracking of used cooking oil into biofuel', 'Hydrothermal', 'Nazarudin', 223, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS216-Nazarudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS216-Nazarudin.pdf', '2024-10-15 14:07:20', '2024-10-15 14:09:06'),
(217, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Photodegradation of humic acid in peat water under visible light using N-TiO₂ photocatalyst', 'Nurul Pratiwi, Gunawan Simanungkalit, Restina Bemis, Freddy Ilfan', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'Indonesia is extensive peatlands, including those in Gambut Jaya Village, Jambi, face clean water challenges due to the presence of humic acid in peat water, which can bind metal ions and form harmful byproducts like Trihalomethanes (THMs) during disinfection. This research aims to develop an effective treatment method for peat water using nitrogen-doped titanium dioxide (N-TiO₂) photocatalysis, focusing on humic acid degradation under visible light. N-TiO₂ was synthesized and characterized using Diffuse Reflectance Spectroscopy (DRS UV-Vis) to determine its band gap. UV-Vis spectroscopy was employed to analyze the degradation of humic acid in water treated with N-TiO₂. The photocatalytic activity was evaluated under visible light, comparing the performance to untreated TiO₂. The results demonstrate that N-TiO₂ effectively degrades humic acid under visible light within 4 hours, especially in acidic conditions. DRS UV-Vis analysis confirmed that the doping shifted the band gap of TiO₂, enhancing its activity in the visible light spectrum. UV-Vis spectroscopy showed a significant reduction in humic acid concentration after treatment. This research highlights N-TiO₂ as a promising solution for peat water treatment, reducing harmful compounds and improving water quality in regions like Gambut Jaya Vilage.', 'Degradation, Humic Acid, N-TiO2, Photocatalyst, Visible Light', 'Nurul Pratiwi', 231, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS217-Nurul Pratiwi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS217-Nurul Pratiwi.pdf', '2024-10-16 07:58:32', '2024-10-25 02:52:49'),
(218, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'RARE EARTH ELEMENT (REE) CONTENT ANALYSIS BASED ON COAL CHARACTERIZATION', 'Yudi Arista Yulanda; Wahyudi Zahar; Jarot Wiratama; Muhammad El Hakim; Aulia Permata Assyura;', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The global transition towards green energy, particularly in the context of electric vehicle development and renewable technologies, has highlighted the critical importance of rare earth elements (REEs). Indonesia’s coal deposits, specifically in the South Sumatra Basin, have been identified as potential sources of REEs. This study aims to evaluate the REE content in coal and parting layers using proximate analysis and ICP-OES methods, focusing on samples KS100, KS300, and Parting KS200. The primary objective is to assess the relationship between coal quality, represented by ash content and calorific value, and the concentration of REEs. Proximate analysis revealed that KS300, a high-quality coal sample with a calorific value of 6500 kcal/kg and ash content of 4.5%, contained the highest REE concentrations, with Ce at 150 mg/kg, Nd at 91.76 mg/kg, and La at 67.24 mg/kg. In contrast, KS100, with a lower calorific value of 5800 kcal/kg and higher ash content of 6.3%, exhibited reduced REE concentrations. The parting layer (KS200) demonstrated the lowest REE content. These findings suggest a positive correlation between coal quality and REE enrichment, supporting the potential of high-quality coal as a viable REE source. Further exploration into coal by-products, such as fly ash, could enhance REE extraction efficiency.', 'Rare Earth Elements, Coal Characterization, Proximate Analysis, ICP-OES', 'Yudi Arista Yulanda', 256, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS218-Yudi Arista Yulanda.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS218-Yudi Arista Yulanda.pdf', '2024-10-16 09:34:12', '2024-10-21 14:30:23'),
(219, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'From Bamboo Leaves to Zeolite: Investigating the Influence of Time on Hydrothermal Synthesis and Mn2+ Ion Adsorption ', 'Lince Muis *1), Hadistya Suryadri  2)', 'University of Jambi', 'From Bamboo Leaves to Zeolite: Investigating the Influence of Time on Hydrothermal Synthesis and Mn2+ Ion Adsorption\n\nLince Muis *1), Hadistya Suryadri  2)\n*Corresponding author\n*ORCHID IDs: https://orcid.org/0000-000X-XXXX-XXXX\n1)Affiliation of 1st author (Chemical engineering, University of Jambi, Indonesia)\n2)Affiliation of 2nd author (Chemical engineering, University of Jambi, Indonesia)\n*email: lince.muis@unja.ac.id\nAbstract\n\nZeolite is a porous material with a unique crystalline structure, capable of effectively absorbing various types of molecules.  Bamboo was chosen as the raw material dua to its abundant availability and environmentally friendly properties.  To synthesize zeolite from bamboo, the hydrothermal method was applied by reacting bamboo leaves with NaOH.  The synthesis process was carried out with different time variation, namely 2 hours, 4 hours, and 6 hours, to observes its influence on the characteristics of the produced zeolite. XRD test results showed that the ZGA/Al-A1 sample produced mesolite zeolite, ZGA/Al-A2 produced 4A-type zeolite, and ZGA/Al-A3 produced sodalite zeolite.  The adsorpstion capacity of ZGA/Al-A3 was recorded at 24.92 mg/g with an adsorption efficiency of 99.69%.  this data indicates that zeolite synthesized from bamboo leaves has potential as an effective adsorbent for the adsorption of Mn2+ metal ions.  This synthesis not only offers an environmentally friendly solution but also provides an alternative use of biomass for zeolite industrial application requiring heavy metal-adsorbing materials.  Bamboo-derived zeolite has the potential to become a leading material in environmental applications and filtration technology.\n', 'zeolite, Bamboo, hydrothermal, Adsorption, ion Mn2', 'Lince Muis', 205, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS219-Lince Muis.pdf', '2024-10-18 09:16:05', '2024-10-18 10:23:35'),
(220, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Spatial Distribution Of Rattan In The Customary Forest Of Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Rantau Kermas Village Merangin Jambi District', 'Mahya Ihsan\nBambang Hariyadi\nPinta Murni\nUni Baroroh Husnudin\nNur Apriani', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Customary Forest in Rantau Kermas Village, Merangin Jambi is a mountainous forest located at an altitude of 950-1100 meters above sea level with humid environmental conditions and air temperatures ranging from 21,8-27,30C. This study aims to identify species, distribution patterns, and rattan conservation status in the Adat Depati Karo Jayo Tuo forest. Sampling points were determined using the purposive sampling method. At each sampling point, 20 m x 20 m quadrat plots were placed systematically along the transect lines, totaling 80 plots with an unspecified distance between plots. The results showed seven rattan species including Calamus micracanthus Griff, Calamus javensis Blume, Calamus sp, and Calamus oblongus Reinw. ex Blume, Korthalsia laciniosa (Griff.) Mart, Calamus manan Miq, and Calamus castaneus Griff with a cluster distribution pattern based on Morishita Index calculation. Six species are categorized as Not evaluated in the IUCN Red List and one species has not been identified to the species level so its status category is unknown. CITES data does not indicate that the species is included in international trade and is not protected under Government Regulation Number 7 of 1999 article 4 paragraph (1) concerning the preservation of plant and animal species. This study provides an overview of rattan distribution patterns and conservation status to support better forest management and development efforts.', 'Rattan, Spatial distribution, Conservation status', 'Mahya Ihsan', 259, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS220-Uni Baroroh Husnudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS220-Uni Baroroh Husnudin.pdf', '2024-10-18 09:48:28', '2024-10-21 14:29:17'),
(221, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Inventory of Ferns from the Aspleniaceae Family in the Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Customary Forest, Merangin, Jambi', 'Uni Baroroh Husnudin\nBambang Hariyadi\nMahya Ihsan\nHasna Ul Maritsa\nJumiatun Nazila', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Ferns have a high diversity of species and are able to live in varied environments. Ferns have ecological (producers, ground cover, bioindicators) and economic (food, medicine and crafts) functions. Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Customary Forest in Merangin, Jambi is one of the forest areas that is preserved by the people of Rantau Kermas Village for generations. This study aims to determine the types of ferns from the Aspleniaceae family in the Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Customary Forest. Data collection in this study was carried out by exploring method and analyzed descriptively. The results showed a total of 18 species of ferns derived from 3 genus namely Aspenium (6 species), Diplazium (8 species) and Athyrium (4 species). The ferns found are ferns that live epiphytically and terrestrially. The Depati Karo Jayo Tuo customary forest is located in an area that has an altitude of > 900 masl so that it tends to be humid, this condition is in accordance with the habitat of ferns. Forest conditions that are still natural and maintained by local people also make the Depati Karo Jayo Tuo Customary Forest a suitable habitat for various ferns.', 'Inventory, Fern, Aspleniaceae', 'Uni Baroroh Husnudin', 259, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS221-Uni Baroroh Husnudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS221-Uni Baroroh Husnudin.pdf', '2024-10-18 10:09:29', '2024-10-21 14:28:49'),
(222, 'science', 'oral presentation', '“Durian Sejuk”: A New Challenge Amidst the Glory of Selat Durian', 'Bambang Hariyadi, Mahsa Ihsan, Uni Baroroh Husnudin', 'Biology Study Program, Natural Science Department, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi  ', 'Selat is known as one of the top producers of durian in Jambi Province, with a legendary reputation. However, in recent years, a new phenomenon has emerged: durian sejuk– durians that lack both taste and smell, making them unsuitable for consumption by humans or animals. It is estimated that around 25% of the durian population falls into the category of durian sejuk. Additionally, the conversion of durian orchards into palm oil plantations has also contributed to the decline in durian populations, threatening the preservation of the legendary Selat durian. The exact cause of durian sejuk remains unclear, but it is suspected to be related to climate change, forest fires, and shifts in durian tree growth patterns.\nThis research is an initial study to explore the phenomenon of durian sejuk and its impact on the local economy and ecosystem. We also examine agroforestry patterns and land conversion, which affect durian populations. By understanding these challenges, we hope to offer solutions to preserve the glory of Selat durian, whether through further research, crop diversification, or adapting planting patterns.\n', 'tasteless durian, Selat durian, land conversion, durian agroforestry', 'Bambang Hariyadi', 260, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS222-Bambang Hariyadi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS222-Bambang Hariyadi.pdf', '2024-10-18 23:35:28', '2024-10-21 14:27:41'),
(223, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'EGG MORPHOMETRICS AND PARASITES IN MALEO BIRDS (Macrocephalon maleo Muller, 1846) FROM BANGGAI REGENCY', 'Wahyudin Abd. Karim 1 *1), Suwarno Hadisusanto 2 2), Raden Roro Upiek Ngesti Wibawaning Astuti 3 *3), Satyawan Pudyatmoko 4 *4)', 'Gadjah Mada University', 'Maleo birds are a fascinating species endemic to the island of Sulawesi and nearby smaller islands in Indonesia. Known for their unusual nesting behavior, these birds are considered critically endangered due to habitat loss and poaching. The aims of this research are to determine (1) the diversity of parasites in the faecal of maleo birds, (2) the egg morphometrics in the natural habitat of maleo. The fecal sampels are collected from captivity areas in PAU and LNG, then by using McMaster simple flotation methods the parasites are identified. Mortar and pestle to grind the feces, glassware to place the preparation/solution to be observed using a microscope. Maleo bird egg samples were measured using a caliper, measuring tape and digital scales. Result showed that the types of parasite was belong to nematode eggs.  Each research location has different length, width, diameter and weight of eggs. This is due to differences in the habitat of each maleo egg obtained.', 'Maleo, Morphometrics, Parasites, Banggai Regency', 'Wahyudin Abd. Karim', 262, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS223-Wahyudin Abd. Karim.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS223-Wahyudin Abd. Karim.pdf', '2024-10-19 04:53:13', '2024-10-21 14:28:18'),
(224, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'USE OF ACTIVATED CARBON-KOH FROM PALM SHELL AS AN ADSORBENT DYE WASTE ', '4', 'Universitas Jambi', 'In this research, activated carbon was made through chemical activation of palm kernel shells using KOH as an activator. The palm kernel shells are first carbonized in a furnace at temperature of 400-500 °C, then the palm shells are activated by soaking in KOH solution 2M KOH solution for 8 hours. The activated palm shell activated carbon is then carbonized at a temperature of 600-800 °C. The activated carbon obtained was characterized by XRD, SEM and FTIR. Characterization using SEM shows that activated carbon has pores, and using FTIR shows the presence of several functional groups that can be used for adsorption. Adsorption studies were carried out using batch experiments with synthetic Naphthol and Remazole dye solutions with initial concentration variations of 10-90 ppm. The adsorption study was carried out in batch, based on isotherm assumptions following the Langmuir isotherm model. These results indicate palm shell activated carbon can have potential as an adsorbent to remove Naphthol and Remazole dyes in solution.', 'activated carcon, dye, palm shell, naftol, Remazol.', 'Intan Lestari', 258, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS224-Intan Lestari.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS224-Intan Lestari.pdf', '2024-10-19 13:22:50', '2024-10-27 14:44:14'),
(225, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Prototype of Semut for PPEPP Cylcle - Internal Quality Audit in Universitas Jambi to be effective and efficient', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo *1), Ira Galih Purbasari 2), Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung 3) , Ade Adriadi 4', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The higher education evaluation Internal Quality Assurance System (SPMI) is the implementation of an Internal Quality Audit (AMI). AMI is an annual routine activity carried out to evaluate the suitability of implementing SPMI standards in order to maintain and improve a culture of quality in a higher education institution. Realizing the importance of the role of AMI, every university must be able to implement AMI well so that documentation and implementation can be carried out thoroughly against the SPMI Standards that have been set. The mechanism of SPMI is that each PT must implement an activity cycle which is abbreviated as PPEPP. To realize the objectives of SPMI and overcome these problems, a website-based information system is needed that can manage the results of quality control from PT as a result of implementing SPMI more quickly and is able to digitize the system and automate the implementation of AMI so that it becomes effective and efficient using the method RAD. The expected results from this research are Design of a Prototype of an Internal Quality Assurance Information System that provides convenience in the PPEPP process, especially in monev and AMI results.', 'SPMI, AMI, audit, Information, System', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS225-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS225-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2024-10-20 14:44:25', '2024-10-26 11:41:15'),
(226, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Optimalisasi Pengelolaan Sampah di SMAS Islam Al Arief Kecamatan Mestong, Muaro Jambi', '1. Febri Juita Anggraini \n2. Winny Laura Christina  \n3. Zuli Rodhiyah\n4. Hariestya Viareco \n5. Tri Syukria Putra', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Pengelolaan Sampah yang optimal merupakan aspek penting dalam menjaga kebersihan lingkungan sekolah. Sekolah Menengah Atas Swasta (SMAS) Islam Al Arief yang terletak di Kecamatan Mestong, Muaro Jambi, tengah menghadapi tantangan dalam pengelolaan sampah yang efektif dan berkelanjutan. Pengolahan sampah yang ada saat ini adalah dengan membakar sampah secara terbuka atau dibuang ke lingkungan tanpa pengolahan lebih lanjut. Perlakuan ini akan memberikan dampak negatif bagi lingkungan sekolah dan sekitarnya. Kegiatan pengabdian ini bertujuan untuk mengoptimalkan pengelolaan limbah di lingkungan SMAS Islam Al Arief yang terletak di Kecamatan Mestong, Muaro Jambi. Melalui tahapan identifikasi masalah, penatapan strategi pengelolaan, implementasi solusi, dan evaluasi terhadap efektivitasnya, kegiatan ini diharapkan mampu memberikan jawaban atas tantangan yang dihadapi sekolah dalam mengelola sampah secara efektif dan berkelanjutan. Peserta kegiatan ini adalah anak kelas 12 yang berjumlah 40 siswa. Berdasarkan survei pendahuluan, diketahui bahwa limbah organik domestik yang ada di sana belum pernah diolah dan hanya dibuang begitu saja. Untuk itu, tim pengabdian memberikan sosialisasi terkait jenis jenis sampah dan workshop terkait pengolahan limbah organik yang dihasilkan dengan cara pengomposan menggunakan bag composter. Tim pengabdian juga menyerahkan set bag composter untuk pengomposan pada pihak sekolah. Berdasarkan hasil survei sebelum dan sesudah sosialisasi, diperoleh bahwa terdapat peningkatan pengetahuan peserta kegiatan tentang pengetahuan pengolahan limbah organik dan jenis jenis sampah.', 'komposter, limbah organik, sampah ', 'Febri Juita Anggraini', 264, 'rejected', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, '', '2024-10-21 03:45:10', '2024-10-21 04:03:32'),
(227, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'Enhancing Higher Education Quality Through Internship Program Design, Faculty Resource Branding, and Sustainable Entrepreneurial Ecosystem Development at the Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi ', 'Jefri Marzal , Rakhmatul Arafat, Ulfa Khaira, Mutia Fadhila Putri', 'Faculty sains and technology, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia', 'This study aims to enhance the quality of higher education at the Faculty of Science and Technology (FST), Universitas Jambi, by addressing key challenges such as stakeholder collaboration, institutional branding, and the increasing demands of Industry 4.0. The research focuses on developing effective strategies for internship programs, branding faculty resources, and fostering a sustainable entrepreneurial ecosystem. The three main areas identified are: optimizing the internship experience, improving FST\'s image through strategic branding of faculty expertise, and building a robust and sustainable entrepreneurship ecosystem. The study proposes an integrative approach that includes designing a comprehensive internship program with clear guidelines, implementing faculty branding through digital platforms, and developing a sustainable entrepreneurial ecosystem through industry collaborations. This holistic strategy aims to equip students with the skills required in the workforce and cultivate successful entrepreneurs. Through optimizing internships and strong faculty branding, this study aspires to significantly contribute to strengthening FST’s image, enhancing stakeholder collaboration, and improving the overall quality of education at FST, thereby fostering a more holistic and sustainable higher education environment.', 'Higher Education Quality, Internship Program Design, Faculty Branding, Entrepreneurial Ecosystem', 'Mutia Fadhila Putri', 266, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS227-Mutia Fadhila Putri.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS227-Mutia Fadhila Putri.pdf', '2024-10-22 01:26:17', '2024-10-24 05:53:08'),
(228, 'engineering', 'oral presentation', 'CHARACTERIZATION OF SILICA SAND AS RAW MATERIAL FOR SOLAR PANEL BASED ON GEOCHEMICAL ANALYSIS IN TELUK RENDAH VILLAGE, JAMBI', 'Muhammad El Hakim, S.T., M.T, Wahyudi Zahar, S.T., M.T, Jarot Wiratama, S.T., M.T, Yudi Arista Yulanda, M.T', 'Universiats Jambi', 'The use of solar panels to generate electricity can reduce dependence on fossil fuels. This is in accordance with one of the goals of The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs 2030), namely ensuring access to affordable, reliable, sustainable and modern energy. Indonesia has a large potential for silica sand mining materials with resources of around 25 billion tons and reserves reaching 330 million tons. Silica sand has abundant resources, is stable for use as a heat storage, and requires low costs as a heat storage with temperatures up to 1,2000C. This study uses geochemical tests and measurements on silica sand samples in Teluk Rendah Village, Jambi. The samples were tested using the Atomic Absorption Spectrometry (AAS) method to determine and analyze the characterization of the silica sand samples that had been taken. The quality of silica sand as a raw material for solar panels should contain grade of Silicon Dioxide content of more than 99.7%, Iron Oxide less than 85ppm, Titanium Dioxide less than 140ppm, Aluminum Oxide less than 500ppm. From the test results, the quality of silica sand in the research area has a Silicon Dioxide content of 95.78%, Iron Oxide 17,200ppm, Titanium Dioxide 13,700ppm, and Aluminum Oxide 8,900ppm.', 'Silica Sand, Solar Panels, AAS, Grade, Teluk Rendah Village', 'Muhammad El Hakim', 269, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS228-Muhammad El Hakim.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS228-Muhammad El Hakim.pdf', '2024-10-22 04:40:44', '2024-10-24 04:42:18'),
(229, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Innovation in Domestic Waste Processing Using Rotary Eco Composter Technology in RT 10, Tahtul Yaman Subdistrict, Jambi City  ', 'Oki Alfernando, Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih, Hariestya Viareco, Dila Oktarise Dwina, Rainiyati', 'University of Jambi', 'The accumulation of waste has become a critical environmental issue, contributing to a range of problems such as air pollution, fires, landslides, water contamination, and public health risks. A key factor behind this persistent issue is the low level of public awareness and understanding of proper waste management and disposal practices. This lack of knowledge prevents communities from addressing the problem effectively. To combat this, there is a pressing need for educational movements and community guidance on proper waste management, particularly focusing on organic waste. One practical solution is the introduction of simple composting technology. This approach emphasizes the transformation of organic waste into valuable products, such as compost or liquid organic fertilizer (MOL), which hold commercial potential and economic benefits for local communities. By empowering residents to create and use Rotary Eco Composters, communities can process organic waste efficiently, fostering both environmental sustainability and economic growth at the local level.  ', 'Waste Management, Composter, Community Empowerment', 'Oki Alfernando', 271, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS229-Oki Alfernando.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS229-Oki Alfernando.pdf', '2024-10-22 13:44:34', '2024-10-24 10:11:49'),
(230, 'science', 'oral presentation', 'DEVELOPMENT OF TEXTBOOKS ON FAMILY ENVIRONMENT AND CHILD EDUCATION', 'Akmal Sutja *1), Firman 2), Yantoro3), K.A. Rahman 4), Rully Andi Yaksa 5)', 'Universitas jambi', 'his research is designed to develop the textbook \"Family Environment and Child Education\" aimed at meeting the specific needs of the Guidance and Counseling Study Program. Using the Lee and Owens research and development model, the study involves five key stages: assessment/analysis, design, development, implementation, and evaluation. In order to obtain valid data, instruments such as questionnaires and tests were used, with participation from subject matter experts and students through various trials including individual, small group, and field tests. The research results indicate a high level of alignment between the developed textbook and the applicable curriculum, as well as its effectiveness in enhancing students\' competencies. The significance of this improvement is evident from the increase in the average test scores of students after the implementation of the textbook. Furthermore, data from the Likert Scale obtained from student questionnaires indicate a substantial increase in satisfaction and understanding of the material. In conclusion, this textbook successfully meets the expected quality standards of teaching materials and is effective in enhancing the learning effectiveness in the Family Environment and Child Education course. ', 'Development of textbooks, family environment, child education, guidance and counseling, learning effectiveness', 'Rully Andi Yaksa', 270, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS230-Akmal Sutja.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS230-Akmal Sutja.pdf', '2024-10-22 13:47:26', '2024-10-25 08:48:40');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(231, 'technology', 'oral presentation', 'DEVELOPMENT OF TEXTBOOKS ON FAMILY ENVIRONMENT AND CHILD EDUCATION', 'Akmal Sutja *1), Firman 2), Yantoro3), K.A. Rahman 4), Rully Andi Yaksa 5)', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research is designed to develop the textbook \"Family Environment and Child Education\" aimed at meeting the specific needs of the Guidance and Counseling Study Program. Using the Lee and Owens research and development model, the study involves five key stages: assessment/analysis, design, development, implementation, and evaluation. In order to obtain valid data, instruments such as questionnaires and tests were used, with participation from subject matter experts and students through various trials including individual, small group, and field tests. The research results indicate a high level of alignment between the developed textbook and the applicable curriculum, as well as its effectiveness in enhancing students\' competencies. The significance of this improvement is evident from the increase in the average test scores of students after the implementation of the textbook. Furthermore, data from the Likert Scale obtained from student questionnaires indicate a substantial increase in satisfaction and understanding of the material. In conclusion, this textbook successfully meets the expected quality standards of teaching materials and is effective in enhancing the learning effectiveness in the Family Environment and Child Education course. ', 'Development of textbooks, family environment, child education, guidance and counseling, learning effectiveness', 'Rully Andi Yaksa', 270, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS231-Akmal Sutja.pdf', '2024-10-22 13:57:29', '2024-10-23 11:19:16'),
(232, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'ASSISTANCE OF BIOPORE TECHNOLOGY AS A RAINWATER INFILTRATION METHOD TO REDUCE SURFACE RUNOFF AND FLOODING IN RT.10, SUNGAI DUREN VILLAGE, MUARO JAMBI REGENCY', 'Bagus Adhitya*, Yosa Megasukma, Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa, Aditya Denny Prabawa, Jarot Wiratama, Arini Anisa Putri', 'Teknik Kebumian, Fakulas Sains dan Teknologi, Universitas Jambi', 'Community Service activity takes place in RT.10, Sungai Duren, Muaro Jambi Regency. This area is prone to flooding and is densely populated. When it rains for an extended period flooding frequently occur. Therefore, team took the initiative to raise awareness about flood disaster mitigation by reducing water runoff, helping to prevent floods. The Community Service Team consists of five lecturers from UNJA from various disciplines and five students, each contributing according to their expertise. The method employed involves raising awareness about biopore technology, which increases the soil’s ability to absorb rainwater. This PPM activity is expected to help address the flooding problem in the partner area. Geographically, the PPM partner area is 3 km away from the Batang Hari River, so it is not part of a floodplain. Base on topography, the partner area is located at an elevation of 12.5m-25m with a valley morphology, causing water from higher areas to accumulate in the PPM partner area and its surroundings. Additionally, the existing drainage and canal systems in the area are not functioning properly, contributing to the flooding issue.', 'biopore, water_runoff, flooding', 'Bagus Adhitya', 263, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS232-Bagus Adhitya.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS232-Bagus Adhitya.pdf', '2024-10-23 06:46:02', '2024-10-27 03:14:51'),
(233, 'ethnobiology', 'oral presentation', 'Ethnobotanical Exploration of Multiethnic Medicinal Plants in Jambi Province', 'Revis Asra, Anis Tatik Maryani, Upik Yelianti', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research aims to document local knowledge about medicinal plants from several ethni in Jambi Province. Analyze the diversity of  of medicinal plants, processing processes, methods of use, analyze the value of the cultural index (Index Cultural Significance), the percentage of parts used (Plant Part Value) , knowing the cultivation and conservation of medicinal plants by multiethnic groups in Jambi. This research was conducted in Batang Sangir Village and Kersik Tuo Village, Kayu Aro District, Kerinci Regency for Javanese respondents, Limok Manaih Pulau Tengah Village, Keliling Danau District, Kerinci Regency for Kerinci Tribe respondents, Ulu Gedong Seberang Subdistrict, Jambi City for Malay respondents, Faculty of Science Laboratory and Technology, and Herbarium Anda, Andalas University, Padang. This  research is descriptive in nature using survey and in-depth interview methods. Respondents were determined using purposive sampling and snowball sampling. Based on the research results, it is known that there are 156 species from 61 plant families that are used as medicine. The part of the plant that is most widely used is the leaf part at 49.23% totaling (96), the highest ICS value is 72 in the species Zingiber officinale Roscoe, the lowest ICS value is 3 in the species Saracai indica L. and Salvia rosmarinus Spenn. The percentage value is based on the disease, the most common medicinal plant is diabetes (16.67%), and the percentage of the family most frequently used is the Zingiberaceae.', 'Medicinal plants, multiethnic, Jambi', 'Revis Asra', 275, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS233-Revis Asra.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS233-Revis Asra.pdf', '2024-10-23 07:06:44', '2024-10-24 05:52:27'),
(234, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'OPinfru fwiorgneuegf www', 'oewirugjer  eowrigneutbj ', 'oewinrgg  ', 'opinrewrgj', 'werg', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 197, 'not yet reviewed', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-10-23 07:36:09', '2024-10-23 07:52:57'),
(235, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', '132132', 'ef', 'irnuf', 'oiewfnqfuji', 'eikofff', 'Mukhtada Nasution', 197, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS235-Mukhtada Nasution.pdf', '2024-10-23 08:00:47', '2024-10-23 11:16:46'),
(236, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'DEVELOPMENT OF TEXTBOOKS ON FAMILY ENVIRONMENT AND CHILD EDUCATION', 'Akmal Sutja *1), Firman 2), Yantoro3), K.A. Rahman 4), Rully Andi Yaksa 5)', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research is designed to develop the textbook \"Family Environment and Child Education\" aimed at meeting the specific needs of the Guidance and Counseling Study Program. Using the Lee and Owens research and development model, the study involves five key stages: assessment/analysis, design, development, implementation, and evaluation. In order to obtain valid data, instruments such as questionnaires and tests were used, with participation from subject matter experts and students through various trials including individual, small group, and field tests. The research results indicate a high level of alignment between the developed textbook and the applicable curriculum, as well as its effectiveness in enhancing students\' competencies. The significance of this improvement is evident from the increase in the average test scores of students after the implementation of the textbook. Furthermore, data from the Likert Scale obtained from student questionnaires indicate a substantial increase in satisfaction and understanding of the material. In conclusion, this textbook successfully meets the expected quality standards of teaching materials and is effective in enhancing the learning effectiveness in the Family Environment and Child Education course. ', 'Development of textbooks, family environment, child education, guidance and counseling, learning effectiveness', 'Akmal Sutja', 270, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS236-Akmal Sutja.pdf', '2024-10-23 08:01:20', '2024-10-23 11:19:01'),
(237, 'ethnobiology', 'oral presentation', 'Ethnobotanical Exploration of Multiethnic Medicinal Plants in Jambi Province', 'Revis Asra, Anis Tatik Maryani, Upik Yelianti', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research aims to document local knowledge about medicinal plants from several ethni in Jambi Province. Analyze the diversity of  of medicinal plants, processing processes, methods of use, analyze the value of the cultural index (Index Cultural Significance), the percentage of parts used (Plant Part Value) , knowing the cultivation and conservation of medicinal plants by multiethnic groups in Jambi. This research was conducted in Batang Sangir Village and Kersik Tuo Village, Kayu Aro District, Kerinci Regency for Javanese respondents, Limok Manaih Pulau Tengah Village, Keliling Danau District, Kerinci Regency for Kerinci Tribe respondents, Ulu Gedong Seberang Subdistrict, Jambi City for Malay respondents, Faculty of Science Laboratory and Technology, and Herbarium Anda, Andalas University, Padang. This  research is descriptive in nature using survey and in-depth interview methods. Respondents were determined using purposive sampling and snowball sampling. Based on the research results, it is known that there are 156 species from 61 plant families that are used as medicine. The part of the plant that is most widely used is the leaf part at 49.23% totaling (96), the highest ICS value is 72 in the species Zingiber officinale Roscoe, the lowest ICS value is 3 in the species Saracai indica L. and Salvia rosmarinus Spenn. The percentage value is based on the disease, the most common medicinal plant is diabetes (16.67%), and the percentage of the family most frequently used is the Zingiberaceae.', 'Medicinal Plants, Multiethnic, Jambi', 'Revis Asra', 275, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS237-Revis Asra.pdf', '2024-10-23 08:20:37', '2024-10-23 11:18:12'),
(238, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Implementation of Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) to analyze factors that influence the learning motivation of students at the Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi ', 'Sarmada 1), Sherli Yurinanda *2), Syamsyida Rozi3),', '1)Mathematics Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universita Jambi, Indonesia\n2)Mathematics Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universita Jambi, Indonesia\n3)Mathematics Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universita Jambi, Indonesia', 'Learning motivation is an effort that drives learning activities to ensure the continuity of learning activities, so that the desired goals can be achieved. Thus, it is necessary to analyze the factors that influence students\' learning motivation. The number of samples in this study was 341 FST UNJA students from the 2021-2024 intake who were actively attending lectures. The research method used was a quantitative survey method where data collection was carried out using a questionnaire with a Likert scale. The data analysis method used was descriptive analysis and Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) analysis assisted by the AMOS 2.2 program. In this study, there were three exogenous variables used, namely learning methods, learning facilities and learning environment. While the endogenous variable used was learning motivation. The results of the study showed that the learning method variable had the greatest contribution, then the second variable that contributed to increasing learning motivation was the learning environment and finally the learning facilities had the smallest effect on learning motivation. So, all latent variables of learning methods, learning facilities and learning environment have a positive effect on learning motivation', 'Learning motivation, Structural Equation Modeling, Students', 'Sherli Yurinanda', 276, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS238-Sherli Yurinanda.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS238-Sherli Yurinanda.pdf', '2024-10-23 08:45:34', '2024-10-26 01:03:15'),
(239, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'EDUKASI DINI SAMPAH ORGANIK DAN ANORGANIK  PADA ANAK PAUD TK AL-AQSHA KEC. TELANAIPURA KOTA JAMBI', 'Fetty Febriasti Bahar, Ira Galih Prabasari,  Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung,  Dyah Kumalasari,  Zuli Rodhiyah', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Anak dengan usia 1 – 3 tahun merupakan masa dimana edukasi dan disiplin perlu ditanamkan dengan cara yang menarik. Memberikan edukasi terkait sampah organik dan anorganik pada anak anak tersebut akan memberi hal positif berupa kesadaran lingkungan yang bermanfaat tidak saja bagi diri sendiri namun juga bagi lingkungan sekitar. Dengan melakukan pemilahan sampah berarti anak anak tersebut telah melakukan pengelolaan sampah awal yang akan memberi dampak yang baik bagi pengelolaan sampah selanjutnya. Tujuan dari pengabdian ini adalah untuk meningkatkan kesadaran akan kebersihan lingkungan dengan jalan memberikan edukasi dini tentang sampah organik dan anorganik pada anak PAUD TK Al-Aqsha Kecamatan Telanaipura Kota Jambi. Luaran yang ditargetkan dari pengabdian ini adalah publikasi pada jurnal nasional, pada media massa (cetak atau online), serta video kegiatan yang akan diunggah di channel youtube LPPM.', 'sampah; usia dini; edukasi ', 'Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung', 278, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS239-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS239-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', '2024-10-23 23:08:42', '2024-10-24 10:13:02'),
(240, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Perancangan Detail Engineering Design Rencana Pembangunan Tempat Pengolahan Sampah Terpadu  Universitas Jambi', 'Revis Asra, Hariestya Viareco, Oki Alfernando, Winny Laura, Dila Oktarise, Ade Nurdin, Fetty Febriasti, Dyah Kumalasari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Sampah merupakan bahan yang terbuang atau dibuang dari suatu sumber hasil dari aktivitas manusia maupun proses-proses alami yang tidak mempunyai nilai ekonomi, bahkan dapat bernilai negatif akibat pengelolaannya baik itu dalam hal pembuangannya maupun pembersihannya dimana membutuhkan biaya yang lumayan besar. Universitas Jambi yang berkembang cukup pesat, terutama dalam hal fisik dan jumlah mahasiswa berimbas pada peningkatan volume timbulan sampah yang dihasilkan dimana nantinya dapat menimbulkan masalah jika tidak dikelola dengan baik. Oleh karena itu perlu adanya pembangunan Tempat Pengolahan Sampah Terpadu (TPST) di Universitas Jambi yang bisa menjadi salah satu Solusi dalam mengatasi permasalahan sampah yang muncul. Dalam Pembangunan TPST dibutuhkan dokumen awal yang disebut Detail Engineering Design (DED) sebagai acuan dalam Pembangunan TPST tersebut dan dokumen resmi yang wajib dimiliki untuk membangun suatu konstruksi bangunan. Melalui penelitian ini diharapakan nanti diperoleh dokumen Detail Engineering Design (DED) yang lengkap dimana terdiri dari gambar detail dan gambar rencana, rencana kerja dan syarat-syarat (RKS) dan rencana anggaran biaya (RAB). ', 'TPST; DED; gambar; RKS; RAB ', 'Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung', 278, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS240-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS240-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', '2024-10-23 23:24:55', '2024-10-25 02:52:15'),
(241, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Peningkatan Indeks Kepuasan Masyarakat: Strategi Inovatif untuk Meningkatkan Kualitas dan Layanan Pendidikan di Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi Universitas Jambi', 'Bambang Hariyadi, Ira Galih, Winny Laura, Ade Adriadi, Hisni Rahmi, Andre Rabiula', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Melalui indeks kepuasan Masyarakat, Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi (FST) akan mampu mengukur peranannya di dalam Masyarakat. Pengukuran indeks kepuasan Masyarakat dilakukan melalui penyebaran kuesioner indeks kepuasan Masyarakat kepada masyarakat yang memiliki dampak langsung kepada Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi seperti dosen, tenaga kependidikan, mahasiswa, alumni, dan mitra/pengguna lulusan. Tujuan dari penelitian ini adalah untuk mengetahui tingkat keberhasilan kinerja layanan di FST sebagai bahan untuk upaya peningkatan di tahun yang akan datang. Dengan menggunakan skala likert 1 – 4, dimana skala 4 untuk skala paling baik dan skala 1 untuk skala paling buruk, maka indeks kepuasan diharapkan berada pada skala sangat baik sampai baik dengan indeks 4 – 3. Indeks kepuasan kepada Masyarakat juga dapat berperan untuk pengembangan bagi layanan Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi. Adanya indeks skala 2 dan 1 pada penilaian kuesioner menjadi cermin bagi Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi untuk melakukan perbaikan. Luaran yang ditargetkan dari penelitian ini adalah terbitnya kuesioner indeks kepuasan masyarakat terhadap layanan Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi.', 'kepuasan masyarakat; layanan pendidikan, kuesioner', 'Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung', 278, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS241-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS241-Winny Laura Christina Hutagalung.pdf', '2024-10-23 23:33:00', '2024-10-24 10:12:31'),
(242, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Early Bankruptcy Detection Analysis of Manufacturing Companies Using Machine Learning Algorithms', 'Tri Suratno*1), Zainil Abidin 2), Mutia Fadhila Putri 3)', 'Fakultas Sains & Teknologi \nUniversitas Jambi', 'Bankruptcy prediction is a critical aspect for stakeholders in assessing the financial health of companies, particularly in the manufacturing sector, which plays a vital role in Indonesia\'s economy. This study aims to develop an early bankruptcy detection model using machine learning algorithms to assist in predicting the risk of bankruptcy in manufacturing companies. Financial data from 164 manufacturing companies listed on the Indonesia Stock Exchange (IDX) were collected and analyzed, covering variables such as Working Capital, Total Assets, Retained Earnings, EBIT, Market Value of Equity, Total Liabilities, and Sales. The Altman Z-Score was used to categorize companies into bankrupt, high-risk, and healthy. Four machine learning algorithms—K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN), Naive Bayes Gaussian, Support Vector Classifier (SVC), and Decision Tree—were implemented to evaluate prediction accuracy. The results showed that SVC had the highest accuracy of 0.97, followed by Naive Bayes Gaussian (0.95), K-Nearest Neighbor (KNN) (0.94), and Decision Tree (0.79). These findings highlight the effectiveness of machine learning in early bankruptcy detection and can provide valuable insights for investors, regulators, and company management in making informed decisions.', 'bankruptcy, machine learning, financial health, Altman Z-Score, manufacturing companies', 'Tri Suratno, Zainil Abidin', 279, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS242-Tri Suratno.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS242-Tri Suratno.pdf', '2024-10-24 01:46:36', '2024-10-24 02:32:18'),
(243, 'advanced_material', 'oral presentation', 'Enhanced Mechanical Stabilization of Peat Soils Using Geopolymerization of Palm Oil Fuel Ash', 'Oki Alfernando, Dila Oktarise Dwina, Nurza Purwa Abiyoga', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Peat soils are characterized by high compressibility and low shear strength, which present significant construction and infrastructure development challenges. In Jambi, Indonesia, extensive peatland areas require innovative stabilization methods to enhance their mechanical properties for sustainable land use. This study investigates using palm oil fuel ash (POFA) as a base material for geopolymerization to stabilize peat soils. POFA, an industrial by-product, is abundant in the region and offers a sustainable alternative to traditional stabilizers. The research methodology involved the preparation of geopolymer-stabilized peat samples with 0%, 10%, 15%, and 20% POFA concentrations, followed by Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS) tests to assess the improvement in soil strength. The results of the UCS tests indicate a positive trend in enhancing the peat’s mechanical properties, with increasing compressive strength values as the geopolymer content increases to 15%. The average UCS results for the consecutive amount of POFA addition are 15.10 kPa, 30.05 kPa, 37.90 kPa, and 9.71 kPa. In conclusion, using POFA-based geopolymer shows potential as an effective and sustainable solution for improving the strength characteristics of peat soils with a notable maximum amount of addition at 15%. This stabilization technique could provide a viable method for infrastructure development in peatland areas', 'geopolymer, unconfined compression strength, palm oil fuel ash, peat', 'Nurza Purwa Abiyoga', 272, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS243-Dila Oktarise Dwina.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS243-Dila Oktarise Dwina.pdf', '2024-10-24 02:37:18', '2024-10-25 02:53:56'),
(244, 'technopreneurship', 'oral presentation', 'Transformation of Economic Independence: Development of Tangkit Eco-Edu-Tourism Village Through Empowerment of Natural Resources Based on Family Medicinal Plants in RT 26, Tangkit Village, Sungai Gelam District, Muaro Jambi Regency', 'Ade Adriadi, Revis Asra, Oki Alfernando, Dila Oktarise Dwina, Dyah Kumalasari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Brick-making is the main livelihood of the residents of RT 26 in Tangkit Village. However, the depletion of raw material sources has led to increased production costs, which in turn has reduced profits. This has impacted the community’s income. Therefore, it is necessary to develop alternative income sources to improve the community’s economy. One of the steps that can be taken is to develop Tangkit Village into an Eco-Edu-Tourism village based on medicinal family plants, utilizing the wealth of natural resources available in the village. Through this initiative, it is hoped that Tangkit Village can become a model village where the community can manage the village independently, both in terms of technology and economy, thereby supporting its economic sustainability. Additionally, it is expected that this initiative will generate new sources of income from the empowerment of the Eco-Edu-Tourism village, as well as foster a skilled, productive, and economically independent community.', 'Eco-Edu-Tourism, Community, Economic, sustainability', 'Ade Adriadi ', 272, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS244-Dila Oktarise Dwina.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS244-Dila Oktarise Dwina.pdf', '2024-10-24 02:53:11', '2024-10-25 02:53:19'),
(245, 'technopreneurship', 'oral presentation', 'Penerapan Sistem Audit Search Engine Optimization (SEO) Berbasis Perangkat Lunak Open Source', 'Daniel Arsa\nMutia Fadhila Putri\nMuhammad Razi A.\nKelvin Adinata', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Dalam tiga puluh tahun terakhir, perkembangan internet telah mengalami kemajuan pesat dari website sederhana menjadi platform yang ramah seluler. Dengan jumlah website yang semakin banyak, pengguna sering mengandalkan mesin pencari untuk mendapatkan informasi yang mereka cari. Untuk mendapatkan eksposur yang optimal, website perlu dioptimalkan untuk dapat muncul pada peringkat utama di mesin pencari seperti Google melalui praktik yang dikenal sebagai Search Engine Optimization (SEO). Penelitian ini berfokus pada pengembangan alat audit SEO yang tersedia secara gratis dan berbasis Open Source, yang bertujuan untuk membantu pemilik website meningkatkan peringkat mereka dalam hasil pencarian. Diharapkan dapat dilakukan analisis terhadap source code halaman web yang dimiliki, sekaligus menganalisis website pesaing untuk memberikan rekomendasi perbaikan yang diperlukan.  Inovasi ini diharapkan akan memberikan manfaat bagi pemilik website, digital marketer, dan pakar SEO tanpa memandang tingkat pengalaman mereka. Pengembangan alat SEO yang gratis ini diharapkan dapat mempermudah proses audit SEO, khususnya bagi organisasi nirlaba dan pemula yang mungkin tidak mampu membeli perangkat lunak SEO berbayar. Pada penelitian ini, sistem akan diuji pada website Universitas Jambi dengan keyword pencarian dan kompetitor yang sesuai.', 'SEO, Audit, Open Source, Search Engine, Website', 'Daniel Arsa', 281, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS245-Daniel Arsa.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS245-Daniel Arsa.pdf', '2024-10-24 03:03:53', '2024-10-24 04:36:27'),
(246, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Improving the Learning Outcomes of the Environmental Impact Analysis Course through Building Design Project Assignments in the Odd Semester of the Academic Year 2024/2025', '1. Shally Yanova\n2. Jalius', 'Jambi University', 'The project-based learning model is one of the learning systems that can be applied to the Environmental Impact Assessment course. This model is suitable for the concept of educational innovation in the field of environmental engineering, especially for students in achieving the learning outcomes of this course. The outcome of the Environmental Impact Assessment course is that students are able to plan construction projects and prepare environmental documents. Based on the results of the midterm exam of Jambi University Environmental Engineering students for the academic year 2024/2025, an average of 82.36 was obtained from 42 students. This result is higher than the midterm exam results for the 2023/2024 academic year with an average score of 80.84 from 35 students. In addition, based on the survey results, 95.24% of the students thought that the project assignments given in this course were able to improve the students\' understanding in this Environmental Impact Analysis course. Thus, it can be concluded that the implementation of building design project assignments and environmental documents is able to improve students\' understanding and fulfill the outcomes of the Environmental Impact Analysis course.', 'PjBL, EIA, Environmental Engineering, Jambi University, Academic Year 2024/2025', 'Shally Yanova', 268, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS246-Shally Yanova.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS246-Shally Yanova.pdf', '2024-10-24 03:12:57', '2024-10-25 04:01:56'),
(247, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Network Pharmacology Analysis of Secondary Metabolites of Sungkai (Peronema Canescens Jack) as Anticancer', 'Ratih Dyah Puspitasari *1), Indra Lasmana Tarigan 2), Ahmad Sazali3) ', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Cervical cancer is a malignant cervix tumor caused by Human papillomavirus infection. Cancer drugs on the market, such as SAHA and Varinostat, are reported to have side effects, so there is still a need to develop new drugs. The drug discovery process requires quite expensive costs and a relatively long time. Aside from synthesis results, medicinal cancer therapy products also come from natural ingredients containing secondary metabolites. Sungkai plants contain secondary metabolites of alkaloids, terpenoids, steroids, flavonoids, phenolics, and saponins. The compounds are β-sitosterol, phytol, β-amyrin, Peronemin A1, A3, B1, B2, B3, C1, and D1, Peronemin A3 and C1. This research used the ADMET, QSAR, and network analysis study methods.  Extract Sungkai bioactives were predicted to act as an anticancer by targeting proteins associated with cervical cancer. STAT3 is one of the potential bioactive targets of Sungkai leaf extract because it correlates with the development of cervical cancer—regulation of gene expression, proliferation, inflammatory response, and regulation of apoptosis influenced by Sungkai leaf extract.', 'extract, sungkai, network analysis, anticancer', 'Ratih Dyah Puspitasari', 284, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS247-Ratih Dyah Puspitasari.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS247-Ratih Dyah Puspitasari.pdf', '2024-10-24 15:04:39', '2024-10-26 00:06:29'),
(248, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Formulation and Evaluation of Sunscreen Cream Containing Jernang Resin (Daemonorops draco (Willd.) Blume)', 'Revis Asra, Uce Lestari, Fitra Wahyuni, Tiara Nasela, Ajeng Aprina Azzahra', 'Jambi University', 'The type of dragon blood discovered in Jambi Province is jernang rambai (Daemonorops draco\n(Willd.) Blume), which can produce a higher quantity of resin with better quality compared to\nother types of jernang. Pharmacological research shows that jernang resin contains flavonoid,\npolyphenol, and triterpenoid compounds, which function to counteract free radicals, thus\nprotecting facial skin from sun exposure and skin damage such as sunburn and aging. Based\non this, jernang resin has the potential to serve as a sunscreen in the cosmetics industry. The\naim of this research is to formulate and evaluate a sunscreen cream made from the active\ningredient of jernang resin. The sunscreen cream was formulated using three different\nconcentrations of jernang resin (10%, 20%, and 30%) and underwent psychochemical\nparameter testing (colour, odour, homogeneity, spread ability and adhesion, pH, and emulsion\ntype). The results of this study indicate that all jernang rambai formulations have a pH range\nthat is suitable for the skin (5.8-6.25) and exhibit good adhesion, spread ability, homogeneity,\nconsistency, and have a m/a emulsion type. The conclusion of the research is that the sunscreen\ncream at concentrations of 10%, 20%, and 30% of jernang rambai meets the psychochemical\nparameter requirements.', 'Daemonorops draco (Willd.) Blume, psychochemical parameter, sunscreen formulation', 'Fitra Wahyuni', 282, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS248-Fitra.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS248-Fitra.pdf', '2024-10-24 17:54:04', '2024-10-25 04:02:27'),
(249, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'Community Service: Training on Creating Dry Herbarium as A Learning Media for Senior High School Students ', 'Revis Asra, Ade Adriadi, Dawam Suprayogi, Hasna Ul Maritsa, Fitra Wahyuni', 'Jambi University', 'The use of learning media can make the learning process more engaging, with teaching materials that are more tangible in their application, making it easier for students to understand and enhancing creativity as students engage in learning activities such as observing and demonstrating, rather than just listening to the teacher. One essential learning media in the biology teaching process is an herbarium. An herbarium is a collection of dried/preserved specimens, usually organized based on a classification system. Herbarium serves as an appropriate learning media because it can be taken anywhere, both in the classroom and in the laboratory. Therefore, to enhance the use of herbarium learning media in high schools, the biology study program at Jambi University conducted community service aimed at training students and teachers in creating simple herbariums as an environmentally based biology learning media. The methods used were tutorials and group mentoring for students and teachers. The results of this community service activity were very positive, as evidenced by the high enthusiasm of students and teachers in participating in the herbarium training. This community service increased knowledge and new skills about herbariums and their simple creation techniques. During this activity, five plant species were identified with their scientific names, and herbariums were created from the school environment, which can be used as teaching tools to support the achievement of learning objectives.', 'herbarium, learning media, senior high school, training', 'Fitra Wahyuni', 236, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS249-Revis Asra.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS249-Revis Asra.pdf', '2024-10-24 19:11:55', '2024-10-25 04:00:42'),
(250, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'DETERMINING THE CALENDAR OF PLANTING PERIOD BASED ON RAINFALL PREDICTION USING THE SARIMA METHOD', 'Mohammad Ilhami, Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Ulfa Khaira, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Rainfall data has been a benchmark that is often used in determining the start of rice planting using the Oldeman method. When rainfall in a particular month reaches 200 mm, it will be designated as the start of rice planting. With this method, the determination of the start of planting can change according to changes in climatic conditions. An early planting season approach using rainfall data will generally be suitable for areas with the main water source being rainwater. One of the climate elements most widely used in research is rainfall, which is closely related to determining the planting calendar. So, forecasting rainfall conditions will be very useful for the agricultural sector in determining the planting calendar. Therefore, considering the importance of rainwater for plants, especially rice, in this research it is proposed to apply rainfall predictions using the Seasonal ARIMA method which is a form of the ARIMA method that takes into account seasonal patterns.  The ARIMA method was chosen because it is able to accommodate extreme values, in this case rainfall which is usually seasonal, as well as the availability of rainfall data in monthly form', 'SARIMA, Forecasting, Oldeman, Rainfall, rice', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS250-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS250-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2024-10-25 00:40:38', '2024-10-26 11:41:41'),
(251, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Evaluation of the Implementation BISMA Application through Black Box Testing approach', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Jodion Siburian, Dawam Suprayogi', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study evaluates the BISMA application, recently implemented at Universitas Jambi for managing research and community service, through black-box testing. Despite the system\'s initial launch, it is essential to assess how effectively BISMA improves user confidence and decision-making processes within academic settings. This research aims to measure user trust in BISMA, analyze its role in decision support for research and community service, and identify factors influencing user satisfaction. The study employs descriptive quantitative methods, gathering primary data through surveys and interviews with system users, including university administrators and faculty members. Key findings will inform further development of the BISMA application to enhance functionality, accuracy, and efficiency, ultimately leading to better support for decision-making and user satisfaction. The anticipated outcomes include actionable recommendations for system improvements, the creation of a real-time data dashboard, and increased user confidence in BISMA\'s capabilities.', 'BISMA, BlackBox, testing, user, satisfaction', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS251-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS251-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2024-10-25 00:48:33', '2024-10-26 11:42:14'),
(252, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Pemanfaatan Teknologi Informasi Pada Praktek Dokter Bersama Patimura Untuk Meningkatkan Produktivitas Kerja', 'Tri Suratno, Reni Aryani, Zainil Abidin, Yolla Noverina, Mohamad Ilhami', 'Fakultas Sains &Teknologi Universitas Jambi', 'Klinik Praktek Dokter Bersama Patimura menghadapi beberapa kendala dalam operasionalnya, terutama terkait dengan penggunaan teknologi informasi. Aktivitas bisnis di klinik masih dilakukan secara manual dan konvensional. Berkas administrasi klinik, termasuk data pasien dan rekam medis, belum terintegrasi dalam sistem komputer, sehingga menimbulkan inefisiensi dalam pengarsipan dan pengelolaan data. Selain itu, keterampilan pegawai dalam bidang teknologi informasi dan komunikasi (TIK) masih terbatas, yang menghambat produktivitas kerja. Berdasarkan analisis situasi ini, solusi yang ditawarkan melalui program pengabdian kepada masyarakat adalah memberikan pendampingan dan pelatihan intensif kepada pegawai klinik untuk meningkatkan keterampilan mereka dalam penggunaan TIK. Pelatihan ini dilakukan dalam dua sesi utama: (1) sosialisasi mengenai peran penting TIK dalam kegiatan bisnis sehari-hari dan manfaat penggunaannya, dan (2) pendampingan dan pelatihan teknis menggunakan aplikasi Microsoft Word, Excel, serta pemanfaatan internet secara optimal. Hasil dari kegiatan ini diharapkan dapat meningkatkan keterampilan pegawai dalam menggunakan TIK, sehingga proses administrasi di klinik menjadi lebih efisien dan produktivitas kerja meningkat. Melalui kegiatan ini, diharapkan terjadi peningkatan keterampilan pegawai dalam mengoperasikan teknologi informasi sehingga produktivitas kerja di Klinik Praktek Dokter Bersama Patimura dapat meningkat secara signifikan', 'Keterampilan computer, Pemanfaatan teknologi, informasi, komunikasi', 'Tri Suratno, Reni Aryani, Zainil Abidin, Yolla Noverina', 279, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS252-Tri Suratno.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS252-Tri Suratno.pdf', '2024-10-25 01:30:34', '2024-10-25 04:01:25'),
(253, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'MOUNTAIN CLIMBING SUPPORT SYSTEM FOR GUNUNG KERINCI: Technological Solutions Through Virtual Reality, Path Tracking, and Warning System ', 'Edi Saputra, Reni Aryani, Mutia Fadhila Putri, Jefri Marzal', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research aims to improve the safety and monitoring of Mount Kerinci climbing through the development of a web-based integrated information system. As the highest volcano in Southeast Asia, Mount Kerinci attracts many climbers, but the absence of a system to monitor climbers\' positions and volcanic status in real-time increases the risk of accidents. As a solution, a web-based Integrated Information System was developed to support the management of Kerinci Seblat National Park in monitoring climbing activities. The development of this system uses the Scrum method, which allows flexible development through continuous iteration. The system has five main features: 1) Climber Monitoring Dashboard to track active climbers and their positions; 2) Mountain Monitoring Dashboard to provide mountain status and weather information; 3) Climber Tracking that visualizes the climbing path that has been traversed; 4) Climber Registration to manage climber data; and 5) Virtual Reality (VR) that provides a simulation of the climbing path. This research makes an important contribution in supporting technology-based adventure tourism management, as well as improving climbing safety on Mount Kerinci.', 'hiking safety, Kerinci Mount, ecotourism technology', 'Reni Aryani', 265, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS253-Reni Aryani.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS253-Reni Aryani.pdf', '2024-10-25 01:44:33', '2024-10-26 03:07:08'),
(254, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Digital Literacy Training for IT Ash-Shiddiqi Middle School Students to be Wise in Using Digital Technology', 'Reni Aryani, Muhammad Razi. A, Tri Suratno, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat, Rizqa Raaiqa Bintana', 'Universitas Jambi', 'In the ever-evolving digital era, ensuring that young people have comprehensive digital literacy skills is essential. This activity focuses on a training program implemented at SMP IT Ash-Shiddiiqi to address students’ lack of understanding of digital literacy. Although students tend to be adept at using digital technology, they often lack adequate knowledge about how to safely and responsibly interact in the digital world. The program is designed in two phases: the first phase focuses on basic knowledge about the benefits and risks of digital technology, while the second phase provides practical training to strengthen digital security practices. This initiative aims to equip students with the necessary competencies to participate safely and responsibly on digital platforms. The outcome of this activity was a significant increase in digital awareness and security practices among participants, and supports the broader educational goal of producing digitally competent and responsible learners.', 'digital literacy, junior high school students, digital media literacy', 'Reni Aryani', 265, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS254-Reni Aryani.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS254-Reni Aryani.pdf', '2024-10-25 02:05:41', '2024-10-26 03:07:37'),
(255, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Modeling Morbidity Table for Chronic Diseases Based on Prevalence Rates', 'Khairul Alim, Gusmi Kholijah, Meinarisa', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Morbidity tables are essential tools in healthcare and actuarial science that provide an overview of disease prevalence within a population at a given time. These tables aid in comparing disease rates between populations, assessing the success of health programs, and predicting future healthcare needs. In health insurance, morbidity tables play a key role in determining fair premium rates for both the public and insurance providers. However, constructing such tables in Indonesia poses challenges, particularly due to its vast geographical landscape. To address this, the study uses disease prevalence rates for modeling, as they are easier to obtain in regions with smaller populations. This research focuses on developing morbidity table for chronic diseases, modeled using a multi-state approach that includes the statuses of healthy, sick, and deceased. The transition from healthy to sick is modeled using a piecewise constant function, while mortality risk is modeled using the Gompertz-Makeham model.', 'morbidity table, prevalence, chronic diseases, Gompertz-Makeham', 'Khairul Alim', 232, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS255-Khairul Alim.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS255-Khairul Alim.pdf', '2024-10-25 03:17:46', '2024-10-26 02:15:17'),
(256, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Utilization of Organic Waste at Three Senior High School of Jambi as an Eco-Friendly Multipurpose Liquid', 'Mahya Ihsan, Ade Adriadi, Tia Wulandari, Ahmad Sazali, Fitra Wahyuni', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Household waste can be utilized for useful products such as eco-enzyme and liquid organic fertilizer. Due to this, the community services team from university of jambi organized an training activity on making liquid organic fertilizer and eco-enzyme for students at SMAN 3 Muara Jambi. the results obtained are products innovative that can be utilized as products for entreprenuership  for students.', 'Liquid organic fertilizer, eco-enzyme, community services', 'Ade Adriadi', 234, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS256-Ade Adriadi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS256-Ade Adriadi.pdf', '2024-10-25 09:15:08', '2024-10-25 09:32:32'),
(257, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Design and implementation of integrity zone in Faculty of Science and Technology, Jambi University', 'Dr. Madyawati Latief, S.P.,\r M.Si.\r\nAlrizaf, S.Pd., M.Si.\r\nFernando Mersa Putra,\r S.T., M.Sc.\r\nSherli Yurinanda, S.Pd.,\r M,Si.', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'This research aims to design and implement a Integrity Zone (ZI) model at the Faculty of Science and Technology, Jambi University (FST UNJA) using a mixed-method, combining qualitative and quantitative methods, for a comprehensive understanding. The study begins with literature review and policy analysis to fully understand the concepts of ZI, bureaucratic reform, and good governance in higher education level (university), alongside reviewing related national and institutional policies that are related to ZI. \nThis research also involves benchmarking to BPMP Jambi, to identify best practices that they have developed, problems and also challenges that they faced in their journey to achieve WBK zone award.\nA conceptual ZI model is then developed and validated through expert involvement. The research also includes designing instruments like questionnaires, interview guidelines, and observation rubrics, tested for validity and reliability before it Will be used as a baseline for data collection. \nSurveys and internal audits will be done to measure academic community understanding and perceptions of ZI while assessing existing systems and procedures in FST UNJA. The ZI implementation model starts with a pilot project in selected units at FST UNJA, accompanied by regular monitoring and evaluation. Collected data are analyzed using descriptive and inferential methods for quantitative data, and thematic analysis for qualitative data. Pilot project evaluation results then Will be used to refine the ZI model, formulate recommendations policy and also implementation strategy for FST UNJA. Finally, research findings are disseminated through reports, scientific articles, and presentations to stakeholders and the academic community.', 'Integrity zone, Implementation, Design', 'Fernando Mersa Putra', 285, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS257-Fernando Mersa Putra.pdf', '2024-10-25 10:56:05', '2024-10-25 23:40:25'),
(258, 'socio_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Design and Implementation of Integrity Zone in Faculty of Science and Technology, Jambi University', 'Dr. Madyawati Latief, S.P.,\nM.Si.\nAlrizaf, S.Pd., M.Si.\nFernando Mersa Putra,\nS.T., M.Sc.\nSherli Yurinanda, S.Pd.,\nM,Si.\nFitra Wahyuni, S.Si., M.Si.\nDaniel Arsa, S.Kom., M.S.I.\nMuhammad El Hakim, S.T., M.T.\nFebri Berthalita Pujaningsih, S.Si., M.Si', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research aims to design and implement a Integrity Zone (ZI) model at the Faculty of Science and Technology, Jambi University (FST UNJA) using a mixed-method, combining qualitative and quantitative methods, for a comprehensive understanding. The study begins with literature review and policy analysis to fully understand the concepts of ZI, bureaucratic reform, and good governance in higher education level (university), alongside reviewing related national and institutional policies that are related to ZI. \nThis research also involves benchmarking to BPMP Jambi, to identify best practices that they have developed, problems and also challenges that they faced in their journey to achieve WBK zone award.\nA conceptual ZI model is then developed and validated through expert involvement. The research also includes designing instruments like questionnaires, interview guidelines, and observation rubrics, tested for validity and reliability before it Will be used as a baseline for data collection. \nSurveys and internal audits will be done to measure academic community understanding and perceptions of ZI while assessing existing systems and procedures in FST UNJA. The ZI implementation model starts with a pilot project in selected units at FST UNJA, accompanied by regular monitoring and evaluation. Collected data are analyzed using descriptive and inferential methods for quantitative data, and thematic analysis for qualitative data. Pilot project evaluation results then Will be used to refine the ZI model, formulate recommendations policy and also implementation strategy for FST UNJA. Finally, research findings are disseminated through reports, scientific articles, and presentations to stakeholders and the academic community.', 'Integrity zone, Design, Implementation', 'Fernando Mersa Putra', 285, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS258-Fernando Mersa Putra.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS258-Fernando Mersa Putra.pdf', '2024-10-25 11:17:25', '2024-10-26 10:48:42'),
(259, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Laboratory Security Transformation: Utilizing IoT Technology for Toxic Gas Detection in the Faculty of Science and Technology Laboratories', 'Zainil Abidin\nAndre Rabiula\nMuhammad Razi A.\nDhian Eka Wijaya', 'Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi, Universitas Jambi, Jambi', 'This study aims to enhance laboratory security through an innovative system that leverages Internet of Things (IoT) technology for toxic gas detection within the Faculty of Science and Technology laboratories. Academic laboratories, particularly in higher education institutions, are often at high risk for hazardous gas leaks, which pose significant dangers to users and the surrounding environment. By integrating IoT, this research seeks to create a toxic gas detection system that connects directly to the network, enabling real-time monitoring and rapid alerts via an Android application. The continuous data collection from this system allows for analyzing laboratory safety trends, identifying leakage patterns, and providing recommendations for preventive measures. The research methodology encompasses the design and development of both hardware and software components, trial runs in a simulated laboratory environment, and subsequent implementation and evaluation within the Faculty of Science and Technology laboratories. The current phase has successfully completed device construction and installation. The findings of this research are expected to significantly improve laboratory safety, reduce accident risks, and optimize toxic gas risk management within academic settings. Moreover, this research can serve as a foundation for developing similar technologies in industrial and other environmental sectors.', 'laboratory security, IoT, toxic gas detection, real-time monitoring, risk management', 'Zainil Abidin', 286, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS259-Zainil Abidin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS259-Zainil Abidin.pdf', '2024-10-25 13:02:42', '2024-10-26 01:00:26');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(260, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Implementation of Project-Based Learning (PjBL) for Effective Learning in Clean Water Production Technology Using Portable Purification Technology from Biomass Waste', 'Oki Alfernando, Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih, Ra\'ida Raudhatussya\'rifah', 'Department of Chemical Engineering, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia', 'This research explores the effectiveness of Project-Based Learning (PjBL) in the course \"Clean Water Production Technology,\" which addresses the global issue of clean water scarcity and the need for sustainable solutions. Integrating a portable purification prototype based on biomass waste, the study aims to enhance students\' understanding, practical skills, and innovation in developing eco-friendly water treatment solutions. Key objectives include examining how PjBL improves learning outcomes and practical skills in clean water production, particularly through projects utilizing portable purification technology. The research also seeks to identify challenges students may face in applying PjBL to biomass-based purification technology and to propose solutions to address these issues. Findings from this study are anticipated to highlight the tangible benefits of PjBL in building student competency, critical thinking, and readiness to contribute to sustainable water treatment advancements in both industrial and community contexts.', 'Project-Based Learning, Clean Water Production Technology, Biomass Waste, Portable Purification Technology, Sustainable Water Treatment.', 'Oki Alfernando', 289, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS260-Oki Alfernando.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS260-Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih.pdf', '2024-10-25 23:38:02', '2024-10-26 00:11:52'),
(261, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', '123', '123', '123', '123', '123', '123', 290, 'not yet reviewed', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2024-10-26 00:32:32', '2024-10-26 00:32:32'),
(262, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', ' Application of palm oil fuel ash to reduce cobalt contamination in gold mining wastewater, incorporating isotherm modeling, thermodynamics, and kinetics', 'Rainiyati, Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih, Ahmad Riduan, Trias Novita', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The contamination of cobalt heavy metals in gold mine waste poses significant environmental problems due to its toxicity to ecosystems. This research uses palm oil fuel ash (POFA) as an adsorbent to reduce cobalt levels in gold mining waste. POFA was characterized using the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method to measure the specific surface area. The analysis of cobalt concentration was conducted using Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry. (ICP-MS). The adsorption process is evaluated through variations in contact duration and temperature. The data were analyzed using the Langmuir, Freundlich, and Temkin isotherm models. The analysis results indicate that the Temkin model exhibits the best fit, with an R² value of 0.9324. Thermodynamic parameters such as ΔG°, ΔH°, and ΔS° were calculated, indicating that the adsorption process is spontaneous and endothermic. The kinetics were comprehensively analyzed using first-order, pseudo-second-order, and intraparticle diffusion kinetic models, revealing a complex adsorption mechanism with moderate adsorption rates and slower kinetics, indicating that adsorption may not involve chemical solid bonding. Research shows that POFA is an effective and economical adsorbent for the remediation of gold mining wastewater contaminated with heavy metal cobalt.', 'adsorption, cobalt removal, gold mining wastewater, isotherm, kinetic, palm oil fuel ash, thermodynamic', 'Rainiyati', 288, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS262-Rainiyati.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS262-Rainiyati.pdf', '2024-10-26 02:25:43', '2024-10-26 02:31:47'),
(263, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Development of a Portable Drinking Water Treatment Prototype with Palm Oil Fuel Ash - Eggshell Powder Membrane Innovation.', 'Oki Alfernando, Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih, Rainiyati', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Clean and safe drinking water has become one of the biggest challenges worldwide. There are demands, especially in remote areas and regions destroyed by disasters, to have access to clean and safe drinking water. These researches are to develop a prototype of a portable drinking water treatment device with an innovative membrane made from a mixture of Palm Oil Fuel Ash (POFA) and eggshell powder. POFA and eggshell powder have been selected for this study because of their abundant availability and high adsorptive properties toward contaminants in water.\nThis prototype device is fabricated with a composite membrane filtration system of POFA-eggshell powder, which can facilitate the filtration of fine particles, reduction in turbidity, and removal of heavy metals and microorganisms. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller Analysis (BET) characterized membranes to determine their specific surface area and porosity. Results: Testing the device\'s efficiency requires that water samples be treated for different contamination levels. In contrast, subsequent water quality analysis uses universal physical parameters such as pH, TDS, and turbidity. Test results showed that this new membrane could reduce contaminant levels to below drinking water quality standards. This prototype portable drinking water treatment device provides a practical and low-cost means of supplying clean water to areas unreached by conventional drinking water infrastructure. The innovation of how waste is utilized creates value addition towards such wastes, besides POFA and eggshell, being supportive enough in environmental sustainability practices. The field test results showed that this prototype could remove most water pollutants.', 'Clean Water Production, Drinking Water, Eggshell Powder, Membrane Technology, Palm Oil Fuel Ash', 'Oki Alfernando', 288, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS263-Oki Alfernando.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS263-Oki Alfernando.pdf', '2024-10-26 02:52:29', '2024-10-26 02:56:39'),
(264, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'OPTIMIZATION OF WASTE MANAGEMENT IN THE AL ARIEF PRIVATE ISLAMIC HIGH SCHOOL (SMAS), MESTONG MUARO DISTRICT, JAMBI', 'Febri Juita Anggraini\nWinny Laura Christina H.\nZuli Rodhiyah\nHariestya Viareco\nTri Syukria Putra ', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Optimal waste management is crucial for maintaining a clean school environment. Al Arief Islamic Private Senior High School (SMAS), located in Mestong District, Muaro Jambi, faces challenges in effective and sustainable waste management. Current practices involve open burning or disposal without further processing, which negatively impacts the school and surrounding environment. This community service aims to optimize waste management at SMAS Islam Al Arief by identifying issues, establishing management strategies, implementing solutions, and evaluating their effectiveness, to address the school’s waste management challenges sustainably. Participants are 40 students from the 12th grade. A preliminary survey showed that domestic organic waste at the school had not been processed and was discarded as is. To address this, the service team conducted a session on waste types and a workshop on organic waste processing through composting with a bag composter. They also provided the school with a set of bag composters. Survey results before and after the session revealed improved participant knowledge regarding organic waste processing and types of waste.', 'composter, organic waste, solid waste treatment', 'Febri Juita Anggraini', 203, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS264-Febri Juita Anggraini.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS264-Febri Juita Anggraini.pdf', '2024-10-26 03:20:08', '2024-10-27 15:09:53'),
(265, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'A Project-Based Learning Approach for Chemical Engineering Students: Waste Cooking Oil Biodiesel Production for Portable Stove Applications', 'Rahma Amalia, Rosmawati Sipayung, Nazarudin*', 'University of Jambi', 'Modern engineering education emphasizes bridging theoretical knowledge with practical application, aligning with industry needs. This study explores the impact of project-based learning in a bioenergy engineering course at the University of Jambi, focused on producing biodiesel from waste cooking oil for portable stove applications. Conducted with 17 chemical engineering students over the 2024/2025 academic year, this project-based learning initiative aimed to develop both subject-specific and transferable skills, such as problem-solving, teamwork, and adaptability. The project involved hands-on laboratory work, emphasizing critical steps in biodiesel production, including transesterification and product purification using both homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts. Students demonstrated notable improvement in technical competencies and soft skills, with over 90% indicating enhanced understanding and application of biodiesel production processes. Despite challenges with equipment limitations and occasional reaction failures, students showed resilience and resourcefulness by consulting with lecturers and documenting successful procedures. Mixed responses were observed regarding time allocation, highlighting areas for potential refinement in workload management. Ultimately, the project fostered an appreciation for sustainable practices and deepened students\' understanding of renewable energy solutions. The findings affirm that project-based learning effectively supports holistic skill development, preparing students for real-world engineering challenges and reinforcing the value of sustainable engineering practices.', 'Project-Based Learning, Biodiesel Production, Engineering Skills', 'Rahma Amalia', 287, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS265-Rahma Amalia.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS265-Rahma Amalia.pdf', '2024-10-26 09:29:59', '2024-10-27 04:39:39'),
(266, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'Near Field and Static Corrections of CSAMT Data Obtain in Volcanic and Geothermal Area', 'Husnul Hamdi, Doddy Sutarno, Wahyu Srigutomo, Enjang Jaenal Mustopa', 'Universitas Jambi\nInstitut Teknologi Bandung', 'Controlled source audio-frequency magnetotellurics (CSAMT) is one of electromagnetic sounding method with frequency domain. CSAMT is a frequency-domain electromagnetic sounding technique which uses a fixed grounded dipole as an artificial signal source. Based on the measurement distance variation the transmitter with receiver on CSAMT, there are three zones, near field, transition, and far field. In the far field zone the plane wave assumption already admitted so that the equation for calculating the value of the resistivity can be applied together with the MT method using Cagniard equation. In the area of near field, source effects can still be captured directly by the receiver and the assumption of a plane wave can not be applied so that the data must be corrected. Correction data is also performed at the transition area. The static effects could result for two values of resistivity which can cause large errors in depth estimation and interpretation of the subsurface structure becomes complex. Static correction is done by averaging the resistivity curve in the measured region. Static correction can also be done by comparing the data obtained with the data TEM (Transient Electromagnetic)', 'CSAMT, Volcanic, Geothermal', 'Husnul Hamdi', 249, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS266-Husnul Hamdi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS266-Husnul Hamdi.pdf', '2024-10-26 15:02:04', '2024-10-27 03:13:58'),
(267, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Design and Construction of Android-Based Virtual Reality Multimedia Applications for Muaro Jambi Temple on Local History and Culture Materials for Elementary Schools', 'Dedy Setiawan, Muhammad Razi A, Dewi Lestari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to develop an interactive multimedia application based on Virtual Reality (VR) on local history\nand culture using the Android-based VR application of Muaro Jambi Temple, specifically for local history and\nculture materials at the Elementary School level. In addition, this study also aims to evaluate the quality of\neducational games developed based on functionality suitability, performance efficiency, compatibility, and\nusability. Data collection was carried out through literature studies and questionnaire distribution. The literature\nstudy aims to obtain references from books related to local history and culture as well as journals relevant to this\nsystem\'s development. Meanwhile, the questionnaire will be distributed in the final stage after the application is\ncompleted and implemented to evaluate the system\'s functionality and usability. Programmers and information\nsystems carried out data analysis, and lecturers tested the functionality of each software function. Furthermore,\nperformance efficiency, compatibility, and usability were tested using a questionnaire filled out by 26 sixth-grade\nstudents at SDIT Nurul Ilmi Jambi as users of the VR application of Muaro Jambi Temple. After the data is\npresented as a percentage, the results will be analyzed to determine the value of the application\'s suitability based\non the percentage assessment scale. The results of this study are expected to provide insight into the\nimplementation of VR-based applications using Unity in building interactive multimedia applications of Muaro\nJambi Temple. This application is expected to make it easier for parents and teachers to introduce local history\nand culture to children while also functioning as a means of entertainment and learning media. In addition, this\napplication is also expected to play a role in building positive character in children through moral values and\ncharacter education contained in local history and culture', 'Virtual Reality, Muaro Jambi Temple, Elementary School, Game, Education', 'Muhammad Razi A', 223, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS267-Dedy Setiawan.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS267-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', '2024-10-26 22:25:30', '2024-10-27 01:55:30'),
(268, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'Hydrothermal impregnation of Nickel metal into activated carbon from palm fiber and its application as a catalyst for the cracking of used cooking oil into biofuel', 'Nazarudin, Ulyarti , Hadistya Suryadri ', 'Chemical Engineering Department, University of Jambi', 'Researchers have been conducting cracking experiments to produce green fuel, testing the cracking of vacuum gas oil (VGO), crude palm oil (CPO), used cooking oil, and plastic waste using various metal catalysts based on natural zeolite, carbon, silica, aluminosilicate, and aluminophosphate (1-21). A review of previous research and literature concluded that cracking used cooking oil with a carbon catalyst derived from palm fiber is the most promising method for further development. Compared to aluminosilicate and aluminophosphate-based catalysts, which show good conversion rates but have high production costs, the use of these materials makes biofuel production from used cooking oil expensive. Previous research has shown that cracking reactions using powdered catalysts are practically less effective, as the powder is often carried away by the flow of gas/fluid from the reactant or carrier gas stream. Additionally, the use of traditional impregnation and ion-exchange methods with standard stirring has been found to introduce only small amounts of nickel into the support material (5, 8, 9, 15, 20, 21). According to literature studies, these issues can be addressed by using pellet-shaped catalysts (22-28).\n\nNickel was chosen as the catalyst due to its excellent performance in cracking reactions and its ability to prevent coke formation. In this study, the hydrothermal method will be tested, as preliminary and literature studies indicate it is more effective at incorporating metal catalysts into carbon (29-31) or other supports (32-40). The objective of the study is to determine the effect of the hydrothermal impregnation method, nickel concentration, and pellet size on catalyst characterization and the conversion of used cooking oil into biofuel, as well as the quality of the resulting biofuel. The study will involve hydrothermal impregnation of nickel at three different concentrations (0.5%, 1%, 1.5%) into carbon derived from palm fiber. Hydrothermal impregnation of nickel will be carried out on powdered carbon, which will then be shaped into pellets (Method A), and on activated carbon that has already been pelletized (Method B). The pellets will be formed into cylinders of three sizes: d8xt10, d10xt12, and d12xt14 mm (d=diameter, t=height).\n\nKeywords: Used Cooking Oil, Hydrothermal, Cracking, Ni-Activated Carbon, Pellet \n', 'Used Cooking Oil, Hydrothermal, Cracking, Ni-Activated Carbon, Pellet ', 'Nazarudin', 294, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS268-Nazarudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS268-Nazarudin.pdf', '2024-10-27 01:36:40', '2024-10-27 06:12:16'),
(269, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Model of intention in Using Mobile Banking of Bank Syariah Indonesia in Jambi Province: Integrated TPB-TAM-Religiosity and Trust Approach', 'Lucky Enggrani Fitri, Dedy Setiawan, Faulina Lubis, Aditya Pratama', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to develop an innovative model that integrates the Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB), Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), religiosity dimensions, and trust in the context of Muslim customers\' interest in using Bank Syariah Indonesia (BSI) Mobile Banking services in Jambi Province. The model, known as the Integrated Model TPB-TAM-Religiosity-Trust, offers a comprehensive perspective on customer behavior and serves as a reference framework for similar studies in the future. By identifying various factors that influence the use of Mobile Banking, this model is expected to improve the quality and efficiency of BSI services. The research sample was selected using the probability sampling method with a purposive sampling technique, involving 190 Muslim respondents in Jambi Province who had used BSI Mobile Banking services. Data analysis was carried out using the Partial Least Square Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM) method with the help of SmartPLS version 3 software. The study results showed that out of 11 proposed hypotheses, two were rejected: (1) the effect of perceived behavioral control on behavioral intention to use and (2) the effect of religiosity on behavioral intention to use. These findings indicate that personal control and religiosity alone are insufficient to influence people\'s interest in using BSI services. Therefore, researchers recommend considering other factors, such as perceived service quality, ease of access, promotion, and social and economic aspects, which may be more relevant in influencing usage intentions.', 'TPB; TAM; Religiosity; Mobile Banking; Trust', 'Lucky Enggrani Fitri', 223, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS269-Lucky Enggrani Fitri.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS269-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', '2024-10-27 02:30:26', '2024-10-27 02:33:17'),
(270, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'Application of Ethnoscience in the Application of Ber-ikan in developing the Batanghari River Fish Dataset', 'Tedjo Sukmono, Teja Kaswari, Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Tia Wulandari, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat', 'Universitas Jambi', 'In social life, there are cultural elements that play an important role as guidelines for social order. Culture applied in society is not limited to objects, but also behavior, ways of behaving, responding to nature, and even managing nature itself. One of the abundant natural resources in Jambi fish biodiversity. The Batanghari River has a big influence on fish diversity and culture. The culture of fish is still embedded in people\'s lives to this day. Ethnoscience is not just knowledge about culture, far from it, fish ethnoscience is an intangible heritage owned by Jambi. Therefore, ethnoscience and fish biodiversity are interrelated things. They have now been combined in the same information system, namely the Ber-Ikan Jambi. This information system is an integrated information system that contains the existence of fish that can be found in the Batanghari River. The socialization using the application were given to the community in the Muaro Jambi Temple area, aimed at providing knowledge and understanding regarding the Provide Jambi information system in supporting ethnoscience studies of the community in the area, so that it can make it easier for the community to get information about local folklore against certain fish', 'Ber-ikan, Etnosains, Biodiversity, Information, System', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS270-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS270-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2024-10-27 03:21:28', '2024-10-27 14:46:06'),
(271, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Project Based Learning (PjBL) Method in the Plant Genetic Resource Conservation Course through a Case Study Project on Plant Genetic Resource Conservation in Muaro Jambi.', 'Rainiyati\nEliyanti\nAhmad Riduan\nSarah Fiebrina Heraningsih', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Project-based learning (PjBL) is an approach that leads to learning by doing, where students engage in projects directly related to real-world experiences. This research aims to evaluate the effectiveness of applying PjBL in the course on the Conservation of Plasma Nutfah through a case study project in Muaro Jambi. The focus is on enhancing students\' theoretical knowledge and practical skills related to the Conservation of Plasma Nutfah, including critical thinking, problem-solving, and collaborative abilities. The research method follows a quantitative descriptive design, with data gathered through questionnaires to capture students\' perceptions of the project\'s benefits and effectiveness. The participants in the study were students enrolled in the Conservation of Plasma Nutfah course and actively involved in the field project in Muaro Jambi. The analysis measured both the level of satisfaction and the achievement of learning objectives, particularly in applying conservation theory in practical, real-world contexts. The findings revealed that implementing PjBL significantly improved students\' understanding of Conservation of Plasma Nutfah concepts and boosted their learning motivation.\nFurthermore, students indicated that the case study project offered significant practical experience in autonomous and collaborative problem-solving with genetic resource conservation concerns. Nonetheless, specific issues were observed, especially in project teams\' time management and task coordination. The study concludes that PjBL effectively enhances academic competence and practical skills. ', ' Case Study, Muaro Jambi, Project-Based Learning, PjBL, Plasma Nutfah Conservation, Project-Based Education.', 'Rainiyati', 289, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS271-Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS271-Sarah Fiebrina Heraningsih.pdf', '2024-10-27 07:01:15', '2024-10-27 07:05:36'),
(272, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Estimation Of Parameters on The Number of Malaria Patients in Indonesia Using the Poisson Regression Method', 'Gusmi Kholijah, Khairul Alim, Uni Baroroh Husnudin, M. Rafly Maretha', 'Mathematics Study Programme', 'Health is an aspect of concern, because health is a benchmark in the welfare of society. Health means that health in all forms is a concern, especially endemic diseases. Currently, one of the endemic diseases in Indonesia is malaria. This disease also puts Indonesia in second place as a country with malaria in Southeast Asia. Malaria is still widely spread in Indonesia. Malaria is still widely found in various regions in Indonesia. This malaria disease that attacks the sufferer is caused by various influencing factors, namely education, community income, place of defecation, sewerage, drinking water source, poverty level and knowledge possessed by the community about malaria. These variables are said to be predictor variables that cause the number of malaria sufferers to occur, which is said to be the response variable. Then the number of cases of malaria in an area follows a Poisson distribution which is influenced by several predictor variables. The observation area of interest in the number of malaria sufferers in Indonesia consists of 34 provinces. This parameter estimation will produce a model of the number of malaria sufferers in Indonesia. The variables formed are used as a consideration in the number of malaria sufferers.', 'Malaria; Regresi Poisson', 'M. Rafly Maretha', 293, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS272-Gusmi Kholijah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS272-Gusmi Kholijah.pdf', '2024-10-27 10:54:07', '2024-10-27 15:00:50'),
(273, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Training on Poisson RegressionAnalysis for Final Year Students at the Faculty of Health Sciences, Adiwangsa University Jambi to Improve the Quality of Thesis Writing ', 'Gusmi Kholijah, Corry Sormin, Gusmanely. Z, Niken Rarasti, Khairul Alim, Ario Surya Trinata, Candra Rijaya, M. Rizki Albaihaqi, Olivia Cantica, Riska Fitriani', 'Mathematics Study Programme, Universitas Jambi', 'Graduates from higher education institutions are expected to become agents of change and make tangible contributions to society. Universities, as providers of higher education, have an obligation to maintain the quality of their graduates. In accreditation assessment criteria, factors such as scientific publications, products/services, and the outputs of student research or community service also play a role in evaluating the quality of education and graduates from an institution. Therefore, the obligation to conduct research and community service lies not only with faculty but also with students. This requirement demonstrates that the research and community service carried out by students must utilize appropriate methods, data analysis, and decision-making. This issue is also faced by students at the Faculty of Health Sciences at Universitas Adiwangsa Jambi. Therefore, a training session on Poisson Regression Analysis was organized for final-year students at the Faculty of Health Sciences to improve the quality of their thesis writing as a form of community service by faculty from Universitas Jambi. The activities included workshops and hands-on analysis practice using R software. Based on the evaluation results, the activity received a score of 3.64 on a 4-point scale, indicating that the community service activity was conducted very well.', 'community service, poisson regression analysis, software R', 'Ario Surya Trinata', 293, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS273-Gusmi Kholijah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS273-Gusmi Kholijah.pdf', '2024-10-27 10:56:47', '2024-10-27 15:01:32'),
(274, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Jambi Kritis Air Tahun 2033', 'Harmes, Muhammad Ikhsan', 'Universitas Jamvi', 'KOTA JAMBI KRISIS AIR TAHUN 2033\nHarmes*1, Muhammad Ikhsan2\n1 Program Study Teknik Sipil Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi, Universitas Jambi\n2 Program Study Teknik Lingkungan Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi, Universitas Jambi\n*)Corresponding author: harmes@unja.ac.id\n\nABSTRACT\n\nNational policy in spatial planning has been implemented massively since Law No.26/2007 was enacted. One of the functions the spatial plan is directing ground water management, especially water catchment areas.  The implementation of the Jambi city spatial plan, shows that there are inconsistencies in several landuses that are also water catchment areas. Anticipating the possibility deviation of landuses is important to maintain the availability of ground water for the society in the future. The purpose of this study was to determine the spatial distribution of potential of water catchment areas and its relationship to the Jambi City spatial plan. The research method used scoring and spatial weighted overlay techniques. Geopatial data that use as input  are the land use, slope, soil type and rainfall, the spatial pattern plan of protected and cultivated areas. Stage of the overlay process uses geographic information system ie. geospatial data compilation, processing and preparation, scoring and weighting, spatial and statistical analysis, discussion. This research shows that the condition of natural normal water catchment area is 164.79 ha or 1.12% located in the southern and east part of the city.  The critical is 235 ha or 1.60%, scattered in Alam Barajo, Pelayangan and Danau Teluk sub-districts. Moderately critical condition is 3,548 ha or 24.06%, spread across the southern part of the city in Kota Baru and Alam Barajo. Very critical area of 8,132.28 ha or 55.14%, covering most of Jambi City from the western part of Paal Merah, South Jambi, Jelutung, Pasar Jambi, Danau Sipin and Telanai Pura and Alam Barajo. The potential of water catchment areas in Jambi City is dominant very critical.\n\nKeywords: Critical Water catchment area, Jambi, spatial pattern, overlay, Spatial analysis.\n\nABSTRAK\nKebijakan nasional penataan ruang berupa yang diwujudkan dalam bentuk rencana tata ruang wilayah diimplementasikan secara massif sejak UU No.26/2007 ditetapkan. Salah satu fungsi ruang wilayah yang atur adalah tata ruang air, khususnya daerah resapan air pada bermacam bentuk peruntukan ruang.  Pelaksanaan perda RTRW No. 09/2013 Kota Jambi yang dilakukan monitoring pemanfaatan ruangnya menunjukkan terjadi inkonsistensi pemanfaatan ruang pada beberapa kawasan yang sekaligus sebagai daerah resapan air. Kawasan bervegetasi dengan fungsi sebagai pengatur tata air berubah ke penggunaan lain yang lebih intensif. Antisipasi adanya kemungkinan simpangan pemanfaatan ruang penting dilakukan untuk menjaga ketersediaan sumber air bagi penduduk pada masa yang akan datang. Penelitian ini ditujukan untuk menganalisis daerah resapan air dalam rangka perwujudan tata ruang wilayah Kota Jambi tahun 2033. Metode penelitian menggunakan teknik scoring dan spatial weighted overlay. Variabel penelitian meliputi penggunaan lahan aktual, kemiringan lahan, jenis tanah dan curah hujan, pola ruang kawasan lindung dan budidaya. Proses overlay menggunakan sistem informasi geografis. Tahapan riset meliputi kompilasi data geospasial, pengolahan dan penyiapan data geospasial, skoring dan pembobotan, analisis spasial dan statistik, pengambilan kesimpulan. Penelitian ini menunjukkan kondisi daerah resapan air normal alami seluas 164,79 ha atau 1,12% berlokasi di wilayah selatan kota dan sebagian kecil di bagian timur.  Kondisi mulai kritis seluas 235 ha atau 1,60%, tersebar di kecamatan Alam Barajo, Pelayangan dan Danau Teluk. Kondisi agak kritis seluas 3.548 atau 24,06%, tersebar disebagian Selatan kota di kecamatan Kota Baru dan Alam Barajo. Kondisi sangat kritis seluas 8.132,28 ha atau 55,14%, meliputi sebagian besar Kota Jambi mulai dari bagian barat kecamatan Paal Merah, Jambi Selatan, Jelutung, Pasar Jambi, Danau Sipin dan Telanai Pura serta sebagian besar Alam Barajo. Potensi daerah resapan air di Kota Jambi dominan sangat kritis. Temuan ini menjelaskan bahwa krisis air di Kota Jambi akan terjadi pada tahun 2033 sebagai akibat dari rencana tata ruang yang sekarang diimplementasikan atau dapat dikatakan bahwa penyebab krisis air adalah perda tentang rencana tata ruang kota Jambi tahun 2013-2033.\n\nKeywords: Daerah resapan air, pola ruang, spasial analisis, krisis air, Jambi.\n', 'Critical Water catchment area, Jambi, spatial pattern, overlay, Spatial analysis.', 'Harmes', 255, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS274-Harmes.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS274-Harmes.pdf', '2024-10-27 12:44:53', '2024-10-27 14:56:43'),
(275, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Helium-Like Energy on the Hylleraas Function with the Variational Optimization Method Fminunc', 'Sri Purwaningsih', 'Universitas Jambi', 'A study has been conducted on the calculation of helium-like energy using the variational method with Fminunc optimization. Optimization is a term often used to refer to minimizing or maximizing a function. In terms of optimization engineering, it is closely related to the design of the function F(x), which is called the objective function. In this study, the variational method with Fminunc optimization was used to determine the minimum of an infinite multivariable function, where x was a variable, and f(x) was the function minimized. The function minimized was the expectation value equation of the helium-like system as the objective function to determine helium-like energy. Based on the results, helium-like energy could be obtained in accordance with the literature.', 'Optimization, Fminunc, Helium-Like ', 'Sri Purwaningsih', 283, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS275-Sri Purwaningsih.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS275-Sri Purwaningsih.pdf', '2024-10-27 13:56:42', '2024-10-28 01:23:14'),
(276, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'MT', 'ade nurdin', 'universitas jambi', 'In 2021, Jambi Province recorded a significant rice production, reaching 298,149 tons of dry milled rice. The production comes from 64,412 hectares of rice fields. In Jambi City, as many as 1,368 tons of rice are produced, mainly from the Orak Kemang area which has 103.07 hectares of rice fields, or about 7.5% of the city\'s total rice field area. The city is located in a lowland north of the Batanghari River, with a lake connected to the river at high tide. However, challenges arose when the irrigation gate in Kaligawe Kanan collapsed due to theft, which interfered with water management in the rice fields. As a result, the process of drying rice fields is highly dependent on water absorption, especially in soils with low permeability. To overcome this problem, Olak Kemang village plans to optimize rice field productivity through a wind power irrigation system. This initiative aims to develop more efficient irrigation systems, introduce new technologies, and reduce costs. with eco-friendly solutions. The participatory action research approach encourages community involvement, so that researchers and residents can learn and collaborate in dealing with drought problems. The program not only focuses on technical solutions, but also strengthens community capacity to address environmental issues. With structured stages from preparation to evaluation, this project is expected to have a sustainable positive impact on the local community and the surrounding environment', 'irrigation, wind power, Olak Kemang', 'ade nurdin', 298, 'rejected', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, NULL, '2024-10-27 15:50:30', '2024-10-27 23:25:20'),
(277, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'STUDI PEMANFAATAN LIMBAH POME PADA CAMPURAN ASPAL PANAS DAN DINGIN TERHADAP KARAKTERISTIK MARSHALL', 'ade nurdin', 'universitas jambi', 'Palm Oil Mill Effluent (POME) adalah limbah cair yang dihasilkan dari proses pengolahan kelapa sawit di pabrik kelapa sawit. Palm Oil Mill Effluent (POME) mengandung mengandung 95-96% air, berbagai bahan organik, zat terlarut, dan padatan yang berasal dari hasil samping proses pengolahan tandan buah segar (TBS) kelapa sawit untuk menghasilkan Crude Palm Oil (CPO). Proses pengolahan kelapa sawit menjadi minyak kelapa sawit (CPO) akan menghasilkan limbah cair dalam jumlah yang cukup besar (Kimia et al., 2004). \nSalah satu upaya yang dapat di lakukan untuk  memanfaatan kan limbah POME adalah dengan menjadikan limbah POME sebagai salah satu campuran aspal.  Tujuan dari penambahan POME terhadap aspal adalah untuk mengetahui   pengaruh Palm Oil Mill Effluent (POME) terhadap aspala pen 60/70 di tinjau dari Uji Marshall. kadar aspal optimum (KAO) yang di gunakan adalah 5,8 %. Berdasarkan hasil pengujian sifat fisik dengan menggunakan uji Marshal, Nilai stabilitas yang di peroleh dari kadar aspal 1142, 5 kg, VIM 3,7 % kelelhan 2,7 mm dan VMA 16,049 % \n', 'Palm Oil Mill Effluent,Uji Marshall ', 'ade nurdin', 298, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS277-ade nurdin.pdf', '2024-10-27 23:31:14', '2024-10-28 01:22:00'),
(278, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Penerapan PjBL pada Perkuliahan Metodologi Penelitian untuk Merancang Proposal Tugas Akhir Mahasiswa Matematika Berbasis Technopreneurship', 'Wardi Syafmen; Bunga Mardhotillah; Cut Multahadah; Gusmanely Z;', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The Department of Mathematics has achieved specific learning outcomes in the Research Methodology course, including the ability to explain fundamental research principles, formulate research problems, objectives, and structures, conduct literature reviews and synthesize relevant research cases, apply appropriate research methods and analysis techniques, and effectively prepare research proposals and reports. The research framework guiding faculty and student projects in the Mathematics Study Program at FST Unja focuses on utilizing and developing mathematics and its applications to support the conservation of local natural resources and environmental sustainability, based on science and technopreneurship.To align course content with research methodology, Research Methodology instructors face significant challenges, requiring effective learning innovations. This is because students often encounter various issues in research methodology courses that may impact their understanding and research skills. One effective innovation for instructors is to implement Project-Based Learning (PjBL). This study examines the application of PjBL in the Research Methodology course to support Mathematics students in designing technopreneurship-based final project proposals. The study sample consisted of 29 students, with data collected through observation sheets and tests. Data analysis techniques included identifying course needs and objectives, designing projects, developing syllabi and lesson plans, preparing assessment instruments, implementation and monitoring, evaluation and reflection, as well as documentation and publication. The results indicate that applying PjBL in the Research Methodology course enhances students\' ability to design technopreneurship-based final project proposals and increases student engagement in class.', 'PjBL, Research Methodology, Final Project Proposal, Technopreneurship', 'Wardi Syafmen', 300, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS278-Wardi Syamen.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS278-Wardi.pdf', '2024-10-28 02:28:17', '2024-10-28 02:50:53'),
(279, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Optimizing Rice Field Productivity in Olak Kemang Village through the Implementation of Wind Power Irrigation System ', 'adenurdin', 'universitas jambi', 'In 2021, Jambi Province recorded a significant rice production, reaching 298,149 tons of dry milled rice. The production comes from 64,412 hectares of rice fields. In Jambi City, as many as 1,368 tons of rice are produced, mainly from the Orak Kemang area which has 103.07 hectares of rice fields, or about 7.5% of the city\'s total rice field area. The city is located in a lowland north of the Batanghari River, with a lake connected to the river at high tide. However, challenges arose when the irrigation gate in Kaligawe Kanan collapsed due to theft, which interfered with water management in the rice fields. As a result, the process of drying rice fields is highly dependent on water absorption, especially in soils with low permeability. To overcome this problem, Olak Kemang village plans to optimize rice field productivity through a wind power irrigation system. This initiative aims to develop more efficient irrigation systems, introduce new technologies, and reduce costs. with eco-friendly solutions. The participatory action research approach encourages community involvement, so that researchers and residents can learn and collaborate in dealing with drought problems. The program not only focuses on technical solutions, but also strengthens community capacity to address environmental issues. With structured stages from preparation to evaluation, this project is expected to have a sustainable positive impact on the local community and the surrounding environment', 'irrigation, wind power, Olak Kemang', 'ade nurdin', 298, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, 'invoice/Invoice-ABS279-ade nurdin.pdf', '2024-10-28 02:37:26', '2024-10-28 02:46:15'),
(280, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Isolation, Screening and Characterization of Potential Carbonatogenic Bacteria as Concrete Biofiller', 'Didik yulianto1), Nadya Bestnissa 2), Dharma Satria3), Hafiz Muchti Kurniawan*4), Soni Pratomo5), Harlia Febrianti6)', 'Adiwangsa Jambi University', 'Eight isolates of carbonatogenic bacteria were successfully isolated from the Bukit Bulan area in Sarolangun Regency, Jambi Province. These bacteria grew well on Calcium Carbonate Precipitation Agar (CCPA) medium and were capable of precipitating CaCO3, with indications of urease enzyme production. Qualitative selection results showed that several isolates formed visible CaCO3 crystals around their colonies, while quantitative selection revealed that all eight isolates could precipitate calcium carbonate in NB U/Ca medium. The BB3 isolate produced the highest amount of CaCO3 at 135 mg/30 ml, while the lowest was 102 mg/30 ml. Gram staining characterized six isolates as gram-positive (BB1, BB2, BB3, BB4, BB5, BB7) and two as gram-negative (BB6, BB8). The urease test indicated that the BB3 isolate exhibited the highest urease activity. All isolates tested positive for oxidase production, and the BB3 isolate demonstrated significantly higher catalase activity than the others. Four isolates (BB1, BB2, BB3, BB4) were motile and had endospores, while BB5, BB6, and BB7 were non-motile. X-ray Diffraction (XRD) analysis showed that the calcium carbonate crystals were in the calcite phase, suggesting that the BB3 isolate holds the highest potential for use in concrete biofilling.', 'carbonatogenic bacteria, Calcium Carbonate Precipitation, Isolation, , concrete biofiller', 'Hafiz Muchti Kurniawan', 244, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS280-Hafiz Muchti Kurniawan.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS280-Hafiz Muchti Kurniawan.pdf', '2024-10-28 04:04:55', '2024-10-28 11:59:27'),
(281, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of Students\' Initial Abilities in Stochastic Process Lectures Based on Learning Results in Statistical Methods, Statistical mathematics, and Mathematics Preneurship', 'Bunga Mardhotillah; Kamid; Nizlel Huda; Zurweni', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Stochastic process is a collection of random variables representing the evolution of some system of random values over time. In simple terms, it\'s a way to model systems or processes that change in a random manner. These processes are used in various fields. Stochastic process discuss about several important things such as random variables, state space, markov property, and ergodicity. Students\' initial ability in lectures of stochastics process  refers to the level of knowledge, skills, and competencies that students possess when they first enter a class of this lectures.  This can include their understanding of basic concepts in a subject, their critical thinking skills, and even their attitudes towards learning. This research aims to analyze students’ initial abilities in stochastics process lectures based on learning results in statistical methods, statistical mathematics, and mathematics preneurship. In this research, urgent aspects that should be evaluated are baseline knowledge, skills, and competencies. Understanding students\' initial ability is crucial for creating effective and personalized educational experiences that promote growth and success in stochastic process lectures.\n\n', 'students\' initial abilities; stochastics process, learning result; baseline knowledge', 'Bunga Mardhotillah', 301, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS281-Bunga Mardhotillah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS281-Bunga Mardhotillah.pdf', '2024-10-28 09:18:54', '2024-10-28 09:45:14'),
(282, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of Students\' Initial Abilities in Stochastic Process Lectures Based on Learning Results in Statistical Methods, Statistical mathematics, and Mathematics Preneurship', 'Bunga mardhotillah; Kamid; Nizlel Huda; Zurweni', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Stochastic process is a collection of random variables representing the evolution of some system of random values over time. In simple terms, it\'s a way to model systems or processes that change in a random manner. These processes are used in various fields. Stochastic process discuss about several important things such as random variables, state space, markov property, and ergodicity. Students\' initial ability in lectures of stochastics process  refers to the level of knowledge, skills, and competencies that students possess when they first enter a class of this lectures.  This can include their understanding of basic concepts in a subject, their critical thinking skills, and even their attitudes towards learning. This research aims to analyze students’ initial abilities in stochastics process lectures based on learning results in statistical methods, statistical mathematics, and mathematics preneurship. In this research, urgent aspects that should be evaluated are baseline knowledge, skills, and competencies. Understanding students\' initial ability is crucial for creating effective and personalized educational experiences that promote growth and success in stochastic process lectures.', 'students\' initial abilities; stochastics process, learning result; baseline knowledge', 'Bunga Mardhotillah', 301, 'rejected', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, NULL, '2024-10-28 09:35:19', '2024-10-28 10:35:24'),
(283, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'asd', 'asd', 'asd', 'asdasd', 'asd', 'asdasd', 304, 'not yet reviewed', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2025-01-02 09:37:39', '2025-01-02 09:37:51'),
(284, 'socio_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'asd', 'asd', 'asda', 'asd', 'asd', 'kingpopaos', 306, 'not yet reviewed', NULL, NULL, NULL, '2025-07-18 22:13:04', '2025-07-18 22:13:04'),
(290, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Design and Implementation of an IoT-Based Real-Time Monitoring System for Optimizing the Growth of Chili Plants (Capsicum annuum L.)', 'Andre Rabiula, Edi Saputra, Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat, Mahya Ihsan', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Chili (Capsicum annuum L.) is one of the most important horticultural commodities in the world; however, its production is often constrained by unpredictable environmental conditions and suboptimal management practices. Precision agriculture based on the Internet of Things (IoT) offers a potential solution to address these challenges. This study aims to design, develop, and test a real-time monitoring system for essential growth parameters of chili plants. The system integrates a series of sensors to measure soil moisture, air temperature, air humidity, and light intensity. Data collected by the microcontroller are processed and wirelessly transmitted to a cloud platform for storage and analysis. An interactive web dashboard was developed to visualize both historical and real-time data, enabling users to perform remote monitoring. The testing results indicate that the system is capable of acquiring and transmitting data accurately and reliably with minimal latency. This system holds great potential to assist farmers in making data-driven decisions, optimizing the use of resources such as water and fertilizers, and ultimately improving the yield and quality of chili production.', 'Chili, Internet of Things (IoT), Precision Agriculture, Monitoring System, Smart Farming', 'Ulfa Khaira', 216, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS290-Ulfa Khaira.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS290-Ulfa Khaira.pdf', '2025-10-07 09:41:59', '2025-11-22 03:36:44');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(291, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'ANALYZING THE FACTORS INFLUENCING GENERATION Z STUDENTS’ ENTREPRENEURIAL INTEREST AT JAMBI UNIVERSITY USING SEM-PLS', 'Sarmada   1*, Gusmanely.Z   2, Yuliana Safitri   3, Cut Multahadah   4, Nabilah Shahadah Putri   ', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Unemployment is one of the major issues faced by Indonesia and must be addressed immediately, as it directly affects public welfare and economic growth. This issue also exists in Jambi Province, which faces similar challenges in creating adequate employment opportunities for its population. One of the solutions to reduce unemployment is by promoting entrepreneurship among Generation Z—encouraging them to be creative, innovative, and capable of utilizing digital technology to create new business opportunities. This study aims to analyze the factors influencing the entrepreneurial interest of Jambi University students using the Structural Equation Modeling–Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS) approach. A quantitative survey method was employed, with data collected through a Likert-scale questionnaire. The exogenous variables tested include social and family environment, innovation and creativity, and technology, with entrepreneurial interest as the endogenous variable. The analysis results indicate that all exogenous variables have a significant effect on entrepreneurial interest, with an R² value of 0.59, meaning that 59% of the variation in entrepreneurial interest can be explained by the model. Among these, the factor of innovation and creativity shows the most dominant influence, with a parameter value of 0.352. These findings highlight the importance of strengthening innovation and creativity in fostering students’ entrepreneurial interest.', 'SEM-PLS, SEM-PLS; Entrepreneurial Interest; Social and Family Environment; Innovation and Creativity; Technology.', 'Cut Multahadah', 314, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS291-Cut Multahadah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS291-Cut Multahadah.pdf', '2025-10-09 07:28:02', '2025-10-09 12:50:58'),
(295, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Development of a Prototype Herbal Fish Medicine from Sappan Wood Extract (Caesalpinia sappan L.): An Antimicrobial Innovation to Reduce Mortality Rates in Aquaculture', 'Ahmad Sazali, Ade Adriadi, Hasna Ul Maritsa, Ashif Irvan Yusuf', 'Biology Studi Program, Jambi University', 'Kegagalan panen dalam budidaya ikan, khususnya di Provinsi Jambi, sering disebabkan oleh infeksi bakteri patogen seperti Aeromonas hydrophila, A. Sobria, E. ictaluri, dan Streptococcus iniae. Penggunaan antibiotik sintetis secara berlebihan telah menimbulkan resistensi mikroba serta berdampak negatif terhadap lingkungan dan kesehatan manusia. Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk mengembangkan prototipe obat herbal ikan berbasis ekstrak kayu secang (Caesalpinia sappan L.) dalam bentuk larutan perendaman yang memiliki aktivitas antibakteri tinggi terhadap bakteri penyebab penyakit ikan. Metode penelitian meliputi ekstraksi kayu secang dengan pelarut etanol 96%, formulasi tiga jenis larutan perendaman menggunakan kombinasi bahan tambahan (Tween 80, PEG 400, dan gliserin), uji aktivitas antibakteri secara in vitro terhadap empat bakteri patogen ikan. Luaran penelitian yang ditargetkan meliputi satu prototipe obat herbal ikan siap pakai, publikasi pada jurnal nasional terakreditasi Sinta 2, diseminasi kepada pembudidaya ikan, serta peningkatan Tingkat Kesiapterapan Teknologi (TKT) dari level 4 menjadi level 6. Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa Formula 3 (mengandung gliserin sebagai bahan tambahan) menghasilkan zona hambat terbesar terhadap keempat bakteri uji, menunjukkan aktivitas antibakteri paling tinggi dan stabilitas larutan yang baik. Formula 3 memiliki zona hambat rata-rata sebesar 17 cm untuk keempat bakteri Aeromonas hydrophila, A. Sobria, E. ictaluri, dan Streptococcus iniae dengan katergori kuat. Dengan demikian, Formula 3 ditetapkan sebagai formulasi terbaik untuk dikembangkan menjadi prototipe obat herbal ikan yang efektif dan aman digunakan dalam sistem budidaya perikanan.', 'Obat Ikan, Caesalpinia sappan, Antimikroba, Patogen Ikan, Budidaya Perikanan', 'Ahmad Sazali', 227, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS295-Ahmad Sazali.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS295-Ahmad Sazali.pdf', '2025-10-09 12:41:07', '2025-10-09 12:51:27'),
(296, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'SUPERGENE ALTERATION AND COPPER MINERALIZATION ANOMALY IN PERALUMINOUS (S-TYPE) GRANITOIDS AT TELUK TILAN SITE, MERANGIN GEOPARK, JAMBI', 'Yudi Arista Yulanda, Jarot Wiratama, Muhammad El Hakim, and Muhammad Faisal Seprizal', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to identify geochemical and mineralogical anomalies within the Tantan Granite Formation at the Teluk Tilan site (Sample TL) in Merangin Geopark, Jambi, to understand the potential for metal enrichment. Petrographic, mineragraphic, and X-Ray Fluorescence (XRF) analyses were conducted on a granitoid sample from this specific location. Geochemically, Sample TL is classified as a Peraluminous (S-type) Granite, characterized by a molar A/CNK value of 1.282. This finding is considered anomalous because the rock exhibits the presence of Copper (Cu) mineralization with a concentration 0.1%. Mineragraphic analysis confirmed an association of metallic minerals, including chalcopyrite (CuFeS2), pyrite (FeS2), and most significantly, the presence of the secondary minerals Covellite (CuS) and Goethite (FeO(OH)). The occurrence of covellite indicates that this granitoid has undergone a post-magmatic supergene alteration process. This supergene process (weathering) triggers the leaching of primary copper and the re-precipitation of secondary copper, potentially leading to higher grades. The results support the interpretation that the Tantan Granite Formation records a complex magmatic evolution and holds indicators of a prospective base metal mineralization system within the Merangin Geopark area.', 'Tantan Granite; Copper; Supergene Alteration; Covellite; Merangin Geopark', 'Yudi Arista Yulanda', 256, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS296-Yudi Arista Yulanda.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS296-Yudi Arista Yulanda.pdf', '2025-10-09 13:19:40', '2025-11-22 13:04:56'),
(297, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'CONSERVATION STATUS AND HABITAT ZONATION OF FRESHWATER FISH IN MERANGIN GEOPARK JAMBI INDONESIA', '\nTedjo Sukmono1,Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Yoppie Wulanda, Lauura Hermala Yunita, Febrina Rolin, Rizky Janatul Magwa, Yusyam Leni, Musadat.', '1Biology Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Jambi, l. Jambi Muaro Bulian Km.15 - Mendalo Darat, Jambi, Indonesia\n2Information Systems Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Jambi, l. Jambi Muaro Bulian Km.15 - Mendalo Darat, Jambi, Indonesia\n3Fishery Product Technology Study Program, Faculty of Animal Husbandry, University of Jambi, l. Jambi Muaro Bulian Km.15 - Mendalo Darat, Jambi, Indonesia\n4Fishery Resource Utilization Study Program, Faculty of Animal Husbandry, University of Jambi, l. Jambi Muaro Bulian Km.15 - Mendalo Darat, Jambi, Indonesia\n5PT REKI Hutan Harapan, Indonesia', 'The Merangin Geopark, located in Merangin Regency, Jambi Province, is now part of the Global Geopark Network (GGN) under the auspices of UNESCO (Mei 24, 2023) and plays an important role in preserving biodiversity, particularly within aquatic ecosystems. One of its key components, the Batang Merangin River, serves as a natural habitat for various freshwater fish species and contributes significantly to maintaining ecological balance. However, anthropogenic activities, especially illegal gold mining (locally known as PETI), have altered the aquatic environment, posing serious threats to the sustainability of endemic species. This study aimed to identify the conservation status and map the habitat zonation of conservation-valuable fish species in the Batang Merangin River within the Merangin Geopark area. Data collection was conducted in September 2025 at four sampling stations (Air Batu Village, Fosil Kayu, Muara Karing, and Teluk Wong) using a field survey approach. The results identified 26 fish species, including two categorized as Vulnerable (Bagarius yareli and Lobocheilos falcifer) and four as Data Deficient (Glyptothorax formelicus, Mystus sabanus, Osteochilus festivus, and Tor tambra) based on the IUCN Red List. Spatial mapping revealed that the Fosil Kayu and Muara Karing stations are priority conservation zones, while Teluk Wong functions as a natural spawning habitat. In conclusion, the Batang Merangin River hosts several fish species at high risk of extinction according to the IUCN status, emphasizing the need for targeted conservation efforts. The spatial analysis highlights critical zones that should be prioritized for ecosystem-based conservation management to sustain fish biodiversity within the Merangin Geopark.', 'Bagarius yareli; Conservation; IUCN; Lobocheilos falcifer; The Merangin Geopark;', 'Tedjo Sukmono', 247, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS297-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS297-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', '2025-10-09 15:26:46', '2025-10-09 16:13:35'),
(298, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Development of Kombucang (Secang Wood Kombucha) as a Functional Herbal Drink: Antioxidant and Antihyperuricemic Activities', 'Ahmad Sazali, Ashif Irvan Yusuf, Hasna Ul Maritsa, Fitra Wahyuni', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'Kombucha is a fermented beverage known for its various biological activities, including antioxidant and uric acid-lowering effects. Caesalpinia sappan L. (sappan wood) contains active compounds such as brazilin and flavonoids, which have potential as natural antihyperuricemic agents. This study aimed to evaluate the antihyperuricemic activity of Kombucang (Kombucha of Sappan Wood) on mice (Mus musculus) induced with potassium oxonate, assess the in vitro antioxidant activity of Kombucang using the DPPH method, and examine its feasibility as a functional herbal beverage from pharmacological and organoleptic aspects. The research was conducted using a completely randomized design (CRD) with six treatment groups: a normal control without treatment (K), a positive control induced with potassium oxonate (KP), a negative control treated with potassium oxonate and allopurinol (KN), and three Kombucang-treated groups with different doses: 0.26 ml/20 g body weight (P1), 0.52 ml/20 g body weight (P2), and 0.78 ml/20 g body weight (P3). Each treatment consisted of five replications and was administered for 14 days. The results showed that administration of Kombucang at a dose of 0.52 ml/20 g body weight (P2) significantly reduced uric acid levels in mice induced with potassium oxonate compared to the positive control group. The DPPH antioxidant assay demonstrated that Kombucang possessed strong antioxidant activity, supporting its potential as a functional herbal beverage with beneficial pharmacological effects and favorable organoleptic properties.', 'Kombucha; Kombucang; Sappanwood; Antihyperglycemic; Caesalpinia sappan', 'Ashif Irvan Yusuf ', 228, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS298-Ashif Irvan Yusuf .pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS298-Ashif Irvan Yusuf .pdf', '2025-10-09 16:00:54', '2025-11-27 10:58:48'),
(299, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Implementation of a Web and Mobile-Based Digital Attendance System to Improve Employee Management Effectiveness at Praktek Dokter Pattimura, Jambi City', 'Muhammad Razi A., Tri Suratno, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat, Dedy Setiawan, Dewi Lestari, Yolla Noverina, Willy Bima Alfajri', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The main problem faced by Praktek Dokter Pattimura in Jambi City is the employee attendance system, which still relies on manual recording and standalone fingerprint machines that are not connected to the internet. This condition causes delays in attendance recording, data inaccuracy, and a high risk of manipulation. This Community Service Program (PPM) aims to assist the partner institution in developing and implementing a digital attendance system based on web and mobile technology, integrated with face recognition and Global Positioning System (GPS). The implementation method uses a Prototype model consisting of several stages: system design, application development, testing, implementation, and user assistance. The results show that the digital attendance system improves administrative efficiency, data accuracy, and employee discipline. User satisfaction reached 88.09% for the mobile application and 85.82% for the web application, indicating that the system was well accepted by users. Therefore, this digital attendance system is proven to be effective and can serve as a model for adopting digital technology in healthcare service management.', 'Digital Attendance, Face Recognition, GPS, Clinic, Human Resource Management', 'Muhammad Razi A.', 315, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS299-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS299-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', '2025-10-09 17:29:11', '2025-11-22 03:40:16'),
(300, 'ethnobiology', 'oral presentation', 'Ethnobotany of Medicinal Plants among the Anak Dalam Tribe in Nyogan Village, Jambi, Indonesia: Cultural Knowledge and Potential for Herbal-Medicine Development', 'Revis Asra; Ade Adriadi; Fitra Wahyuni; Yogi Irawan Pandiangan; Febriyanti Munthe; Defrizal; Izu Andry Fijridiyanto', 'Universitas Jambi; Bappeda Provinsi Jambi; Pusat Riset Biosistematika Dan Evolusi - BRIN', 'This collaborative ethnobotanical study documents and analyzes the traditional knowledge of medicinal plant utilization among the Anak Dalam (SAD) community permanently settled in Nebang Parah and Nyogan Hamlets, Nyogan Village, Muaro Jambi Regency, Indonesia. Fieldwork employed a descriptive qualitative approach through direct observation, in-depth interviews with 54 informants (including 1 tribal healer), participatory observation, and herbarium collection for species identification. Twenty-one plant species belonging to 18 families were recorded, predominantly from wild habitats. Frequently used taxa included Annona muricata, Cratoxylum arborescens, Melastoma malabathricum, Curcuma longa, and Tinospora crispa, whose active compounds (flavonoids, xanthones, curcuminoids) demonstrate proven anti-inflammatory, antipyretic, and antioxidant properties. Quantitative indices: Cultural Significance Index (ICS) and Plant Part Value (PPV), revealed that leaves constituted the most-used organ (48%), while species with high ICS values reflected strong ritual and healing importance. The findings show that SAD’s indigenous pharmacopoeia represents an invaluable biocultural asset currently endangered by rapid socio-cultural change. This research provides a scientific foundation for the preservation of traditional medicinal knowledge and identifies several high-value species for further phytochemical and pharmacological exploration toward standardized Jambi-based herbal products. The study contributes to sustainable utilization of biodiversity and strengthens community-based health resilience through local herbal innovation.', 'ethnobotany; medicinal plants; Anak Dalam tribe; traditional knowledge; herbal medicine', 'Revis Asra', 236, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS300-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS300-Revis Asra.pdf', '2025-10-09 17:31:50', '2025-11-07 12:16:22'),
(301, 'technopreneurship', 'oral presentation', 'Empowering Local Entrepreneurs Through Digital Marketing and Pineapple Product Innovation in Tangkit Baru Village to Support the National “Proudly Made in Indonesia” Movement', 'Muhammad Razi A., Ulfa Khaira, Willy Bima Alfajri, Zainil Abidin, Yosi Riduas Hais, Mutia Fadhila Putri, Dedy Setiawan, Reni Aryani', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This community service program was carried out in Tangkit Baru Village, Sungai Gelam District, Muaro Jambi Regency, which is known as one of the largest pineapple production centers in Jambi Province. The main problems faced by the community are the lack of innovation in pineapple derivative products and limited ability to market products digitally. The purpose of this activity is to enhance the capacity of local entrepreneurs through training on pineapple-based product innovation and the introduction of e-commerce platforms to expand market reach. The implementation method used participatory approaches, training, mentoring, and practical sessions. The results of the activity showed an increase in the knowledge and skills of entrepreneurs in product packaging, branding, and the use of digital media for product promotion. Additionally, new business groups were formed that utilize digital technology as a marketing tool. Therefore, this program contributes to strengthening the local economy and supports the national movement of \'Proudly Made in Indonesia.\'', 'Empowerment, Digitalization, E-Commerce, Product Innovation, Pineapple', 'Muhammad Razi A.', 315, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS301-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS301-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', '2025-10-09 17:42:06', '2025-11-22 03:41:18'),
(303, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'COMMUNITY ECO-ENZYME FERMENTATION FOR HOUSEHOLD ORGANIC WASTE', 'Ade Adriadi1, Revis Asra2, Mahya Ihsan3, Tia Wulandari4, Ahmad Sazali5 and Fitra Wahyuni6', 'Department of Biology, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi', 'This community engagement program addressed unmanaged household organic waste among Malay mothers in Ulu Gedong, Jambi, by transferring a low-cost, science-based fermentation technology to produce multi-purpose eco-enzyme. Objectives were to (i) improve knowledge and skills in source-level waste valorization, (ii) generate usable products (liquid eco-enzyme and compostable residues), and (iii) catalyze a resident-led recycling group. Methods combined participatory briefing, hands-on training, and a 30-day anaerobic fermentation using a 1:3:10 sugar:biowaste:water formulation, EM4 bio-activator, and a simple water seal air lock; monitoring was conducted through on site visits and messaging. Results included ~10 L matured eco-enzyme (golden-brown, pleasant acidic aroma) per batch, immediate household use as natural cleaner and plant tonic, and formation of the Ulu Gedong Recycling Mothers group. A post-activity survey (n=16) showed very high satisfaction and intent to continue practice (overwhelming “Strongly Agree/Agree”), indicating effective knowledge transfer and social acceptance. Novelty lies in a rapid, community owned, low resource anaerobic protocol that shortens maturation from ~90 to ~30 days while embedding behavior change and micro enterprise opportunities. Implications include measurable source reduction of organics, reduced chemical cleaner purchases, and a scalable green economy model for similar peri urban communities.', 'eco-enzyme; household organic waste; community engagement; anaerobic fermentation; green economy', 'Ade Adriadi', 236, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS303-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS303-Revis Asra.pdf', '2025-10-09 19:24:34', '2025-11-07 12:15:53'),
(304, 'food_science_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Cysteine-capped silver nanoparticles as a probe for colorimetric sensing of 3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol', 'Martina Asti Rahayu, Arum Widia Putri, Dhian Eka Wijaya, Ayu Nadhila Safitri, M. Irhash Shalihin', 'Universitas Jambi', '3-Monochloropropane-1,2-diol (3-MCPD) is a recognized food contaminant formed during the thermal processing of fats and oils, posing significant health concerns due to its potential toxicity. In this study, we present a rapid and sensitive colorimetric assay based on cysteine-modified silver nanoparticles (Cys-AgNPs) for the detection of 3-MCPD. The Cys-AgNPs were synthesized via chemical reduction and characterized by UV–Vis spectrophotometry. Upon interaction to 3-MCPD, the solution color changed visibly from yellow to pink within five minutes at 80 °C and pH 9.3, corresponding to a shift in the surface plasmon resonance (SPR) band. This phenomenon is attributed to the reaction between the amino group of cysteine and 3-MCPD, forming N-(2,3-dihydroxypropyl)-amino acid moieties grafted onto the nanoparticle surface (3-MCPD–Cys–AgNPs). Quantitative analysis was achieved using UV–Vis measurements, yielding a linear response over the concentration range of 1–5 μg mL⁻¹, with a calculated detection limit of 0.126 μg mL⁻¹. The pronounced color transition enables unassisted visual detection of 3-MCPD even at 1 μg mL⁻¹. Overall, this Cys–AgNP colorimetric probe provides a simple, rapid, and cost-effective approach for monitoring 3-MCPD in food samples, offering potential for on-site quality and safety assessment.', '3-MCPD, silver nanoparticles, colorimetric detection, food contaminant, rapid assay.', 'Martina Asti Rahayu', 246, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS304-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS304-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', '2025-10-10 01:26:52', '2025-11-22 03:34:47'),
(305, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Development of an Android-Based Educational Game for the Preservation of Jambi Folklore: A Case Study of the Legend of the \'Orang Kayo Hitam\'', 'Dedy Setiawan, Tri Suratno, Muhammad Razi A., Dewi Lestari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This research aligns with the University of Jambi\'s Strategic Plan (Renstra UNJA) for 2024–2028, which emphasizes the use of digital technology and the preservation of local wisdom through research-based innovation.\n\nOne implementation is the development of educational media that can introduce local cultural values ​​in an engaging and contextual manner. In this context, the development of an Android-based educational game based on the folktale \"Orang Kayo Hitam\" is a response to the increasing need for effective digital learning media to instill cultural values ​​and moral character in children. With the advancement of digital technology, game-based media has proven to be an innovative solution for increasing children\'s interest in learning and understanding of culturally based materials. This research aims to design and develop an educational game application using Unity as a development platform, using a Multimedia Development Life Cycle (MDLC) software engineering approach. In addition to design, this study also evaluates the application\'s quality based on four software quality standards: functionality suitability, performance efficiency, compatibility, and usability. Data collection was conducted through a literature review to obtain a theoretical foundation and a questionnaire was distributed to users after the application was completed. The analysis involved students from the Ash-Shiddiiqi IT Elementary School in Jambi City to test the application\'s functionality and performance efficiency. Quantitative evaluation of usability aspects was presented as a percentage as the basis for assessing the application\'s feasibility. The outputs of this research include:\n(1) the creation of interactive media based on local culture; (2) a scientific article for publication in the Business Information Systems Journal (JSINBIS) - https://ejournal.undip.ac.id/index.php/jsinbis or other SINTA 2 journals; and (3) a presentation of the research results at the 2025 JICEST Seminar at the Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Jambi.', 'Game; Android; MDLC; Folklore; Jambi', 'Dedy Setiawan', 315, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS305-Muhammad Razi A..pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS305-Dedy Setiawan.pdf', '2025-10-10 02:25:52', '2025-11-22 03:41:47'),
(306, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'DESIGN AND DEVELOPMENT OF A WEBSITE-BASED 360° VIRTUAL TOUR FOR  THE AGROTOURISM OF TELUK VILLAGE', 'Yolla Noverina, Muhammad Razi A, Dewi Lestari, Mohammad Ilhami, Reni Aryani, Hasby Kuswanto, Lina Kartika Paramita', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to develop a digital promotional medium based on a 360° Virtual Tour to introduce the agrotourism potential of Teluk Village, Pemayung District, Batanghari Regency, Jambi Province. The development process employed the Multimedia Development Life Cycle (MDLC) method consisting of six stages: Concept, Design, Material Collecting, Assembly, Testing, and Distribution. At the design stage, an interactive user interface and 360° panorama navigation flow were created and integrated with multimedia content obtained using a VR camera. The implementation utilized the 3DVista Virtual Tour platform, enabling users to explore panoramas, information hotspots, and location maps interactively. System testing using the Black Box Testing method showed that all main functions performed properly, including panorama navigation, information display, and multimedia playback without technical issues. The final product is a functional 360° Virtual Tour website accessible at panorama-desa-teluk.fst.unja.ac.id, serving as a digital, educational, and promotional tool for supporting community-based agrotourism management. The novelty of this research lies in its contextual application of immersive 360° virtual tour technology to promote the agrotourism potential of Teluk Village, which possesses substantial tourism assets but previously lacked digital promotional media, and in providing a structured MDLC-based development model that can be replicated for rural tourism digitalization initiatives in Indonesia.', 'Virtual Tour 3600; MDLC; Agrotourism; Website; Village Digitalization.', 'Yolla Noverina', 316, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS306-Yolla Noverina.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS306-Yolla Noverina.pdf', '2025-10-10 05:03:15', '2025-11-28 01:37:04'),
(307, 'advanced_material', 'oral presentation', 'Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) Spectroscopic Analysis of Hydroxyapatite Scaffolds Obtained from Channa striata and Scomberomorus commersoni Fish Bones ', 'Rista Mutia Anggraini\nRia Anjelina\nFebri Berthalita Pujaningsih\nMuhammad Ficky Afrianto\nSamsidar\nIchy Lucya Resta\nLucky Zaehir Maulana\nFrastica Deswardani ', 'Department of Physics, Universitas Jambi, Jambi 36361, Indonesia', 'Hydroxyapatite (HAp) has been successfully obtained from Channa striata and Scomberomorus commersoni bones. HAp scaffolds are potential materials for bone tissue engineering due to their adjustable characteristics suitable for specific biomedical applications. The freeze-drying method was employed in scaffold fabrication because it minimizes contamination and prevents carbon formation that may result from combustion, as the process operates at very low temperatures. A natural polymer derived from honeycomb was used to enhance the mechanical properties of the scaffold. Variations in the addition of honeycomb biopolymer were applied during the process. One of the characterizations performed was FTIR analysis, which was conducted to identify the functional groups formed. Variations in honeycomb concentration were found to influence the increase in FTIR absorption intensity. The functional groups identified include PO₄³⁻ and CO₃²⁻.', 'scaffold, hydroxyapatite, fish bone, freeze-drying, honeycomb', 'Rista Mutia Anggraini', 220, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS307-Rista Mutia Anggraini.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS307-Rista Mutia Anggraini.pdf', '2025-10-10 07:42:45', '2025-11-26 08:56:27'),
(308, 'advanced_material', 'oral presentation', 'A NOVEL SYNTHESIS ROUTE AND FUNCTIONAL GROUP ANALYSIS BY FTIR SPECTROSCOPY OF GRAPHENE OXIDE FROM INDIGENOUS COAL AND GRAPHITE', 'Lucky Zaehir Maulana, Valda Ayu Nanda, Rista Mutia Anggraini, Muhammad Ficky Afrianto, Fiqri Al Faruqi, Febri Berthalita Pujaningsih, Alrizal, Willy Bima Alfajri dan Frastica Deswardani', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study reports a novel, cost-effective synthesis route for preparing Graphene Oxide (GO) using readily available indigenous coal from Jambi and natural graphite as dual carbon precursors. Graphene Oxide is a crucial intermediate material for producing graphene-based composites, and utilizing local, low-cost raw materials offers significant economic and scalability advantages over relying solely on commercial-grade graphite. The synthesis was successfully performed via a modified Hummers\' method optimized for the unique chemical structure of the indigenous coal. The primary objective of this research was to confirm the successful oxidation and characterize the resulting oxygen-containing groups in the synthesized GO sheets. Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) Spectroscopy was employed as the key characterization technique. The FTIR analysis of the synthesized GO from both coal and graphite exhibited characteristic stretching vibrations, confirming the extensive oxidation of the carbon lattice.  The FTIR results demonstrate that the novel synthesis method effectively introduces a high density of functional groups onto the carbon surface, which is essential for GO\'s applications in composites and chemical sensing. This work confirms the potential of local coal as a viable, alternative feedstock for high-quality GO production.', 'Graphene, nanomaterial, FTIR', 'Lucky Zaehir Maulana', 212, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS308-Lucky Zaehir Maulana.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS308-Lucky Zaehir Maulana.pdf', '2025-10-10 07:44:00', '2025-11-26 09:00:50'),
(309, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'FLOOD DISCHARGE SIMULATION IN  KENALI KECIL RIVER BASIN USING SWAT AND SWMM', 'Freddy Ilfan, Hairul Mursalin, Lailal Gusri, Taufiq Ihsan', 'Jambi University, Andalas University', 'Land use changes in the Kenali Kecil Watershed, particularly in Kembar Lestari 1 Housing Complex, Jambi City, have increased surface runoff, resulting in the inability of the drainage system to accommodate peak discharge during heavy rainfall. This study aims to evaluate the capacity of the existing drainage system through hydrological and hydraulic analysis using the Soil Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) and Storm Water Management Model (SWMM). The data used include 10 years of rainfall records, climatological data, and drainage channel geometry. The analysis shows that the peak discharge of 12.683 m³/s exceeds the capacity of the existing 3.6 m × 1.8 m drainage channel. SWMM simulation indicates flow inefficiency due to sediment accumulation and waste blockage. Recommended solutions include increasing the channel dimensions to 3.6 m × 3 m, or constructing infiltration wells and floodgates. The study concludes that redesigning the drainage system is necessary to mitigate flooding in the area.', 'Drainage; Flood; Soil Water Assessment Tool (SWAT); Storm Water Management Model (SWMM)', 'Freddy Ilfan', 317, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS309-Freddy Ilfan.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS309-Freddy Ilfan.pdf', '2025-10-10 10:43:03', '2025-10-10 15:13:53'),
(310, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'Green Synthesis of Ag/Tio₂ Composite Using Pineapple Peel Extract for Degradation of Organic Pollutants', 'Nurul Pratiwi, Muhammad Irhash Shalihin, Munifilia Ekasari and Fiqri Al Faruqi', 'Chemistry, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia', 'This study aims to develop a green synthesis method for Ag/TiO₂ composites using pineapple peel waste as an eco-friendly bioreductor for organic pollutant treatment applications. The utilization of agricultural waste as a natural reducing agent is expected to replace hazardous chemicals in the synthesis process while supporting the principles of sustainable chemistry. The synthesis was carried out using a hydrothermal approach with variations in pineapple peel extract concentration to obtain composites with optimal morphology and photocatalytic activity. Characterization was performed using XRD, UV-Vis DRS, SEM-EDX, and FTIR to determine crystal structure, composition, and functional group changes. Photocatalytic activity was evaluated through the degradation of an organic pollutant model, methylene blue, under visible light irradiation. The results are expected to show that the presence of silver nanoparticles enhances light absorption and TiO₂ photocatalytic performance through the formation of surface plasmon resonance (SPR). This research is anticipated to produce an effective and environmentally friendly photocatalyst while adding value to pineapple peel waste as a source of functional biomaterials. Moreover, the outcomes are expected to serve as a foundation for further applied research in wastewater treatment and green material technology development.', 'Green synthesis; Ag/TiO₂ nanocomposite; Pineapple peel waste; Bioreduction; Photocatalytic degradation', 'Nurul Pratiwi', 231, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS310-Nurul Pratiwi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS310-Nurul Pratiwi.pdf', '2025-10-10 11:22:04', '2025-10-10 15:13:29'),
(311, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Adaptive Practical Scheduling System Based on Genetic Algorithm with Multi-Objective Optimization Approach', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat, Niken Rarasati', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Practical scheduling is a crucial challenge in higher education, particularly in the Faculty of Science and Technology (FST) of Jambi University, which has numerous study programs, laboratory-based courses, and limited resources. The manual or semi-digital scheduling process often results in scheduling conflicts, inefficient space utilization, and inconsistencies with lecturer and student preferences. This research develops SmartLab Scheduler, an adaptive practical scheduling system based on a genetic algorithm with a multi-objective optimization approach. This system is designed to generate optimal schedules by considering various objectives simultaneously, such as minimizing scheduling conflicts, optimizing space utilization, and accommodating instructor preferences. The genetic algorithm is implemented with a chromosome representation containing important parameters such as days, hours, laboratory space, courses, and faculty. A fitness function is designed to evaluate solutions based on the level of violation of mandatory constraints (hard constraints) and optional preferences (soft constraints). This system is integrated into a user-friendly web/desktop-based information platform, so it can be used directly by academic administrators. This research produces a reliable, adaptive, and replicable system on a wider scale. The main outputs are a ready-to-use SmartLab Scheduler system, scientific publications in accredited journals (SINTA 2), conference/seminar proceedings with ISBNs, and intellectual property rights.', 'scheduling, genetic, practical, optimation', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS311-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS311-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2025-10-10 22:28:27', '2025-11-24 17:20:09'),
(312, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'AUTOMATION OF EMPLOYEE ATTENDANCE SYSTEM USING CONVOLUTIONAL NEURAL NETWORK (CNN) AND YOLO ALGORITHM FOR FACE RECOGNITION WITH DEEP LEARNING APPROACH', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo, Edi Saputra, Mohammad Ilhami, Benedika Ferdian Hutabarat, Ulfa Khaira', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Deep learning is widely used in computer vision, a branch of machine learning that aims to teach systems to understand and interpret images and videos. Object detection is a computer-assisted task related to detecting examples of visual objects of a specific class. The goal of object detection is to develop computational models and techniques. Deep learning algorithms are widely used for various innovations in computer vision, one of which is the automated attendance system. This study aims to create an innovation in the form of an automated attendance system, namely an automatic attendance recording system that utilizes CCTV to capture images/videos of employees entering the office and performs facial detection and recognition processes by applying deep learning algorithms and then records the presence of recognized employees automatically. In this study, OpenCV is used as a facial detection algorithm from input images/video streams that will produce input images for the facial recognition process. Facial recognition and classification are made using the YOLO and CNN algorithms. The training process for the formation of object recognition models will utilize a dataset in the form of a collection of facial images/photos that have been collected so far through a web-based facial attendance application.', 'cnn;yolo;face recognition,attendance', 'Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo', 248, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS312-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS312-Pradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo.pdf', '2025-10-10 22:46:44', '2025-11-24 17:19:35'),
(313, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'AUTO SWITCHING WIRELESS NETWORK ALGORITHM FOR SINGLE-INTERFACE ARMBIAN DEVICES', 'Khairul Alim, Niken Rarasati, Yuliana Safitri, Muhammad Razi A.', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The development of information technology has increased the demand for devices capable of connecting to the internet flexibly, including in mobile environments. Single Board Computers (SBCs) such as Raspberry Pi and Orange Pi running lightweight operating systems like Armbian are widely used in embedded systems due to their stability and efficiency on ARM-based devices. However, devices with a single WiFi interface often face difficulties when switching networks automatically, which becomes a challenge in Internet of Things (IoT), robotics, and edge computing systems that require stable and continuous connections. This study aims to design an automatic WiFi network switching algorithm for Armbian devices with a single wireless interface. The proposed method involves designing an algorithm that monitors signal strength (RSSI), speed, and connection stability, which is then implemented as a bash script that runs automatically in the system background. The results show that the developed algorithm can perform network switching automatically with satisfactory transition time and in accordance with the defined parameters. Therefore, this solution enhances network reliability on Armbian devices without adding significant system overhead.', 'Armbian, WiFi auto switching, single board computer, bash script, network reliability', 'Khairul Alim', 232, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS313-Khairul Alim.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS313-Khairul Alim.pdf', '2025-10-11 11:58:18', '2025-10-11 15:40:08'),
(314, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Empowering Science Practicum through Smartphone-Based Digital Image Analysis: A Contextual Learning Approach', 'Martina Asti Rahayu, Arum Widia Putri, Dina Erliana, Indra Lasmana Tarigan, Dhian Eka Wijaya, Ayu Nadhila Safitri \n', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Science practicum plays a vital role in strengthening students’ conceptual understanding; however, limitations in laboratory facilities often hinder its optimal implementation. To address this challenge, this community service program was designed to enhance practical science learning through digital innovation using smartphones and ImageJ software. The program was conducted at SMA Negeri 13 Jambi as an example of contextual and technology-based practicum enhancement.\n\nThe main objective of this program was to empower teachers and students to utilize smartphone cameras and digital image analysis for the detection of water pollution, serving as a real-world application of analytical chemistry concepts. The implementation included workshops, demonstrations, and guided practice sessions on capturing water sample images using smartphones and processing them with ImageJ. Through this approach, students learned to analyze RGB intensity variations to determine differences in water turbidity and color, which represent the presence of pollutants. The results indicated a significant improvement in students’ engagement, scientific reasoning, and digital literacy. Teachers also reported that the method provided a feasible and low-cost alternative to conventional laboratory instruments while maintaining analytical relevance. In conclusion, this program successfully strengthened science practicum implementation through the integration of smartphone-based digital tools and contextual environmental analysis, promoting a more interactive, accessible, and technology-oriented learning experience in secondary education.', 'contextual practicum, smartphone-based innovation, ImageJ, digital image analysis, science education', 'Martina Asti Rahayu', 246, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS314-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS314-Martina Asti Rahayu.pdf', '2025-10-11 13:23:01', '2025-10-11 15:39:12'),
(315, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Portable Gram-Stain Kit: Enabling Inquiry-Based Microbiology in High Schools', 'Fitra Wahyuni1, Bambang Hariyadi2, Tedjo Sukmono3, Mahya Ihsan4, Anggit Prima Nugraha5, Uni Baroroh Husnudin6, Andini Vermita Bestari7 and Ashif Irvan Yusuf8', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Many Indonesian senior high schools lack functional biology laboratories, limiting experiential microbiology learning. We developed a portable Gram-stain kit plus guided training to enable hands-on practicals aligned with inquiry-based curricula. Using a five-stage community-engagement model (co-planning; kit prototyping/validation; teacher–student workshops; monitoring/evaluation; dissemination), we delivered 30 ready-to-use kits, and a concise step-by-step module to SMAN 3 Muaro Jambi. Students (small groups) completed the full Gram-stain workflow (smear, heat fixation, crystal violet, iodine, alcohol decolorization, safranin), followed by microscopy and interpretation. Learning outcomes were assessed with paired pre/post multiple-choice tests (10 items), procedural checklists, and user feedback. Paired analyses used Shapiro–Wilk for normality and Wilcoxon signed-rank when appropriate (α=0.05). All groups produced interpretable slides (purple Gram-positive; pink Gram-negative), demonstrating feasibility and face validity of the kit. Aggregate knowledge gains were moderate (post-test > pre-test by ~6–10 points on a 0–100 scale) but not statistically significant (Wilcoxon, p>0.05), constrained by ceiling effects (high baseline scores). Qualitative data showed increased confidence, strong engagement, and teacher readiness to replicate the practical independently using remaining kits. The portable Gram-stain kit is a fit-for-purpose, scalable, and low-cost educational technology that enables authentic microbiology experiences in resource-constrained schools. While aggregate cognitive gains were limited by high baselines, procedural and affective outcomes and technology transfer to teachers were substantial. Future work will extend to additional low-cost microbiology kits, strengthen longitudinal monitoring, and scale dissemination via district partnerships.', 'Gram stain; portable lab kit; inquiry-based learning; experiential science practicals; resource-constrained high schools (Indonesia).', 'Fitra Wahyuni', 236, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS315-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS315-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', '2025-10-11 23:13:19', '2025-11-22 03:33:25'),
(316, 'advanced_material', 'oral presentation', 'Synthesis and Characterization of Mg-Al-Ni  Layered Double Oxide (LDO) Catalyst', 'Nazarudin (1,2) , Intan Lestari (3), Hari Wiki Utama (4), Ulyarti (2,4), Sal Prima Yudha S (5),  Vicka Andini (5), Fathan Bahfie (6)', '\n1 Chemical Engineering Department, Faculty of Sciences and Engineering, University of Jambi,Indonesia \n2 Centre of Excellence  on Bio-Geo Material and Energy, University of Jambi, Indonesia\n3Chemistry Department, University of Jambi, Indonesia\n4Geological Engineering, Jambi University, Jambi, Indonesia\n4  Acricultural Technology Department, Faculty of Agriculture, University of Jambi,Indonesia\n5Chemistry Department, University of Bengkulu, Indonesia\n6 Research Center of Mineral Technology, National Research and Innovation Agency of Indonesia, Lampung, Indonesia\n', 'ABSTRACT\nThis study presents the synthesis and characterization of Mg–Al–Ni Layered Double Hydroxide (LDH) and its derived Layered Double Oxide (LDO) catalysts prepared with varying nickel loadings (0.25–1.5 wt%) through a co-precipitation and intercalation approach. The catalysts were characterized using X-ray diffraction (XRD), X-ray fluorescence (XRF), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) to investigate the structural, compositional, and morphological features. XRD analysis confirmed the successful formation of a hydrotalcite-like structure, with basal reflections corresponding to (003), (006), (012), (015), (018), and (110) planes, indicating well-ordered layered crystallinity. Incorporation of Ni²⁺ into the Mg–Al lattice up to approximately 1 wt% preserved high crystallinity and homogeneous substitution, whereas excessive loading (≥1.25 wt%) led to peak broadening and reduced intensity, signifying partial loss of structural order and possible NiO segregation. Elemental analysis revealed a steady increase in Ni content proportional to the nominal loading, supporting effective metal incorporation within the LDH framework. SEM images showed that moderate Ni addition enhanced surface roughness and porosity, while excessive Ni promoted particle agglomeration. These results demonstrate that controlled nickel doping optimizes both the structural integrity and active-site distribution of Mg–Al–Ni LDO catalysts. The resulting material shows strong potential for application in catalytic cracking of used cooking oil and other biomass-derived feedstocks for biofuel production due to its synergistic acid–base and redox properties.\n\nKeywords: Catalyst, Layered Double Hydroxide, Mg-Al LDH, Mg-Al-Ni LDH, Biofuel\n', 'Catalyst, Layered Double Hydroxide, Mg-Al LDH, Mg-Al-Ni LDH, Biofuel', 'Nazarudin', 294, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS316-Nazarudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS316-Nazarudin.pdf', '2025-10-12 04:57:31', '2025-10-12 06:07:12'),
(317, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'Application of Ni-Carbon Catalyst in the Conversion of Used Cooking Oil from Instant Noodle Frying Processes into Biofuel', 'Nazarudin (1,2), Ulyarti (2,3), Mursalin (2,3), Hadistya Suryadri (1,2)', '1 Chemical Engineering Department, Faculty of Sciences and Engineering, University of Jambi,Indonesia \n2 Centre of Excellence  on Bio-Geo Material and Energy, University of Jambi, Indonesia\n3. Acricultural Technology Department, Faculty of Agriculture, University of Jambi,Indonesia\n\n', 'A review of previous research studies (1-21) shows that Nickel metal as a catalyst performs well for cracking reactions and can prevent coke formation. Previous studies have shown that the method of developing Nickel onto carbon by impregnation and ion-exchange is known to have a small amount of Ni entering the carrier (5, 8, 9, 11, 15, 20, 21). The use of the hydrothermal method shows a significant increase in the amount of Nickel carried in carbon, with the increase in the amount of Nickel in carbon resulting in an increase in the conversion of used cooking oil into biofuel (22). However, a review of previous studies shows that used cooking oil sourced from households/restaurants contains various types of chemicals originating from the extraction of fried food ingredients so that when cracked it produces biofuel containing several undesirable chemical compounds such as acetic acid and carboxylic acid (7, 14, 22, 27). In this study, the results of previous innovations on the synthesis of Ni-Carbon pellet catalysts will be developed in the cracking of used cooking oil using used cooking oil samples/feeds that only come from used frying instant noodles, to ensure that chemical impurities are only extracted from the ingredients for making instant noodles. Another reason, based on data from the Central Statistics Agency in 2024, the average consumption of instant noodles in Indonesia per capita/week is 1 pack (80 grams or total production per year of 20 billion packs (28) which of course produces used cooking oil that has the potential to be processed into biofuel. In this study, the synthesis and characterization of Ni-carbon Pellet catalysts will be carried out using methods that have been discovered previously, making instant noodles where the remaining cooking oil is used as a sample of used cooking oil which is then cracked using a Ni-carbon Pellet catalyst to become biofuel.', 'Instant Noodle, Cracking, Ni-Activated Carbon, Pellet,Used Cooking Oil', 'Nazarudin', 294, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS317-Nazarudin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS317-Nazarudin.pdf', '2025-10-12 05:11:11', '2025-10-12 06:07:41');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(318, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Multi-Model Hybrid Framework for Automated Malaria Detection and Species Classification', 'Akhiyar Waladi, Nindy Raisa Hanum, Yogi Perdana, Hasanatul Iftitah, Fitra Wahyuni, Rahmad Ashar\n', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Malaria remains a critical global health challenge with over 200 million annual cases, yet traditional microscopic diagnosis is time-consuming and requires expert pathologists. This study proposes a multi-model hybrid framework combining YOLO (v10-v12) for detection and six CNN architectures (DenseNet121, EfficientNet-B0/B1/B2, ResNet50/101) for classification, validated on two public MP-IDB datasets comprising 418 images across 8 classes (4 Plasmodium species and 4 lifecycle stages). The proposed shared classification architecture generates ground truth crops once and reuses them across all detection methods, enabling efficient model training. YOLOv11 achieves 93.10% mAP@50 with 92.26% recall on species detection and 92.90% mAP@50 with 90.37% recall on lifecycle stages. For classification, EfficientNet-B1 reaches 98.80% accuracy (93.18% balanced accuracy) on species despite 45:1 class imbalance, while EfficientNet-B0 achieves 94.31% accuracy (69.21% balanced accuracy) on lifecycle stages with 54:1 imbalance. A notable finding is that smaller EfficientNet models (5.3-7.8M parameters) outperform larger ResNet variants (25.6-44.5M parameters), with EfficientNet-B1 achieving 10.5 percentage points higher balanced accuracy than ResNet101 (93.18% vs 82.73%) despite 5.7× fewer parameters, challenging the \"deeper is better\" paradigm. The system addresses extreme class imbalance through Focal Loss (α=0.25, γ=2.0), achieving minority class F1-scores of 76.9% on P. ovale with perfect recall and 92.3% on schizont, though trophozoite (51.6%) and gametocyte (57.1%) remain challenging. Implemented on consumer-grade GPU hardware, the framework demonstrates practical feasibility for deployment in resource-constrained settings, though clinical use requires validation on larger datasets and field-collected samples', 'Malaria detection, Deep learning, YOLO, Class imbalance, Medical imaging, Plasmodium species, Lifecycle stages', 'Akhiyar Waladi', 308, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS318-Akhiyar Waladi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS318-Akhiyar Waladi.pdf', '2025-10-12 09:26:31', '2025-11-07 11:42:32'),
(319, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Phytochemical Profile and Antioxidant Activity of Daemonorops draco (Willd.) Blume Resin Extract from Jambi, Indonesia', 'Fitra Wahyuni1, Ade Adriadi2, Anggari Linda Destiana3, Ashif Irvan Yusuf4, Revis Asra5', 'Department of Biology, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to characterize the phytochemical constituents and evaluate the antioxidant activity of Daemonorops draco (Willd.) Blume resin, locally known as jernang, a traditional medicine widely used for wound healing in Sumatra, Indonesia. Resin samples were macerated using 96% ethanol and screened qualitatively for major secondary metabolites. Phytochemical profiling revealed the presence of flavonoids, phenolics, and triterpenoids, while alkaloids and saponins were absent. Antioxidant capacity was determined through the DPPH radical scavenging assay. The extract exhibited a concentration-dependent inhibition of DPPH radicals (R² = 0.96) with an estimated IC₅₀ of 160.4 µg/mL, indicating moderate antioxidant potential. This activity correlates with the abundance of flavonoid compounds, known to neutralize reactive oxygen species and support tissue repair. The findings scientifically confirm the ethnopharmacological use of jernang resin as a natural antioxidant source. These data provide a foundation for subsequent studies involving FRAP and HRBC assays, as well as in vivo wound-healing models to explore its anti-inflammatory and regenerative mechanisms.', 'Daemonorops draco; jernang; phytochemical screening; antioxidant activity; DPPH assay', 'Fitra Wahyuni', 236, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS319-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS319-Fitra Wahyuni.pdf', '2025-10-12 09:46:46', '2025-11-07 12:16:50'),
(321, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'A Web-Based Information System for Digitalizing Administrative Permit Services at University Y', 'Mochammad Arief Hermawan Sutoyo, Renaldi Yulvianda, and Willy Bima Alfajri', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The Indonesian government’s budget efficiency policy has compelled University Y to reduce its operational expenditures. This financial constraint has created an urgent need to modernize the university’s conventional administrative processes, particularly the permit management procedures, which remain dependent on paper-based documentation, limited human resource capacity, and manual archiving systems that are prone to errors. To address these challenges, digitalizing the process must be done, this study proposes the design of an integrated web-based permit information system. The system development adopts the prototyping methodology, and its effectiveness is evaluated using the System Usability Scale (SUS) to ensure the system operates properly and enhances user convenience. The evaluation results indicate a score of 83, suggesting that the prototype meets user expectations and is ready for implementation.', 'Software Enginering, HCI, Administrative System, Digitalization', 'Mochammad Arief Hermawan Sutoyo', 323, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS321-Mochammad Arief Hermawan Sutoyo.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS321-Mochammad Arief Hermawan Sutoyo.pdf', '2025-10-19 10:17:20', '2025-11-07 11:51:31'),
(324, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'Horizontal Axis Multi-Blade Wind Power Plant: A Case Study in Talang, Solok Regency', 'Fiqri Al Faruqi, Andre Rabiula, Dasrinal Tessal, Akhiyar Waladi, Lailal Gusri, Ali Satria Wijaya, Imas Ning Zhafarina, Aldony Reco Putra', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study investigates the design and performance of a multi-blade horizontal axis wind turbine as an alternative energy source in Talang, Solok Regency. The main objective is to develop a wind power generator capable of operating efficiently under the low wind speed conditions typical of the Talang area, while contributing to local electricity supply. The research methodology includes six months of field wind speed measurements at multiple hub heights to establish the wind profile, analysis of regional meteorological data, prototype design with considerations of blade number, pitch angle, and rotor diameter, and evaluation of turbine performance in terms of electrical output, power coefficient, start-up (cut-in) speed, and operational stability under varying wind and load conditions. The results show that with an optimized multi-blade configuration, the turbine achieves cut in at approximately 4-5 m/s, delivering average electrical output within ±10% of the predicted values. Efficiency levels are deemed satisfactory for local deployment, with the turbine maintaining stable operation across wind speed and load variations. Innovations include the adaptation of blade geometry suitable for low-speed wind, use of cost-effective materials, and design for low maintenance. This case study demonstrates that multi-blade horizontal axis wind turbines are viable for regions like Talang with modest wind resources. Recommendations highlight further refinement of blade design and integration of control systems for enhanced consistency and scalability in rural or remote settings.', 'Multi-Blade Wind Turbine, Horizontal Axis, Low Wind Speed, Cut-in Speed, Talang Solok, Renewable Energy', 'Fiqri Al Faruqi', 319, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS324-Fiqri Al Faruqi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS324-Fiqri Al Faruqi.pdf', '2025-10-21 02:21:43', '2025-11-07 12:00:01'),
(325, 'climate_change', 'oral presentation', 'HUMIC ACID COATED BIOCHAR IMPROVES RETENTION IN AND REDUCES NITROGEN EMISSION FROM FERTILIZED SOILS OF PALM OIL PLANTATIONS', 'Damris Muhammad\nEdwin Permana\nDiah Riski Gusti\nIntan Lestari\nMartina Asti Rahayu', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Biochar, a carbon-rich and porous material produced from organic waste biomass, has become a new trend in overcoming various environmental isues. However, direct virgin biochar application has limited capacity. This research aims to synthesis humic acid (HA) coated biochar and to evaluate HA-coated biochar in retaining N in and reducing N emission from soil of palm oil plantations. HA coated-biochar was obtained by autoclaving HA solutions (0.25% and 1.0%) for 30 minutes and then biochar was added with stirring. The mixture was then dropped into 1% and 5% CaCl2 solutions for cross-linking formation. The HA-coated biochar (0.00, 0.75, 1.50, and 3.00% (w/w) were mixed with 500g soil and incubation was carried out for 60 days. Factions of soil-HA-coated biochar and gas from the bootle head space were collected 4 times at the day 0, 20, 40, and 60. The solid was treated with water and 0.1M HCl at a ratio of 1:5 for the determination of N retention and the gas was analyzed with GC for the determination of N2O gas emission. HA-coated biochar amendment significantly increased N retention (p<0.05) and significantly reduced N2O emission from the soil (p<0.05). The increase in retention and the decrease in emission of N from the soils simultantly reduced N loss more than 40% compared to the soil without HA-coated biochar amendment.', 'humic acid; biochar; nitrogen; soil; incubation', 'Damris Muhammad', 313, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS325-Damris Muhammad.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS325-Damris Muhammad.pdf', '2025-10-21 05:09:53', '2025-10-22 04:24:46'),
(326, 'climate_change', 'oral presentation', 'ESTIMATION MODEL OF MANGROVE FOREST BIOMASS USING SATELLITE IMAGERIES IN JAMBI PROVINCE, INDONESIA ', 'Eva Achmad, Hamzah, Fazriyas', 'Department of Forestry, Jambi University, Faperta Building, Jambi University, Jambi, Indonesia', 'Mangrove forests are having quite high biomass content. Currently, data regarding biomass content stored in mangrove forests, especially in Jambi Province, is still limited. Carbon calculations using field surveys are a very good method, but calculations using this method are considered less efficient in terms of time and cost, especially if the area to be studied has a large area. An alternative method for calculating biomass can be done by utilizing remote sensing technology, namely with Landsat 8 satellite imagery. Estimating biomass stores using satellite imagery can be done using a vegetation index approach and model making, where in this research the vegetation index used is NDVI and the model chosen is the model quadratic with the model equation, namely Y = -1315.754 + 9452.41 NDVI2 with an R2 value of 65.5%. Model selection is based on the highest score value which considers SA, SR, RMSE, Bias, R2, and s. The mangrove forest biomass stock map is divided into several classes, namely biomass storage <1 ton/ha, then classes 1-100 (ton/ha), 100-200 (ton/ha), 200-300 (ton/ha), 300- 400 (ton/ha), >400 (ton/ha), where the class with a value range of >400 (ton/ha) dominates, reaching 3015, 98 ha or 30.04% of the mangrove area in Jambi Province.', 'estimation model; forest biomass; Landsat imagery; mangrove forest; NDVI', 'Eva Achmad', 325, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS326-Eva Achmad.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS326-Eva Achmad.pdf', '2025-10-21 15:03:59', '2025-11-07 11:58:24'),
(327, 'food_science_technology', 'oral presentation', 'CHEMICAL INTERESTERIFICATION OF MIXTURE OF RBDPO AND SOFT-PMF OLEIN FRACTIONS FOR SYNTHESIZING  COCOA BUTTER EQUIVALENTS', 'Mursalin Mursalin, Fitry Tafzi, Yernisa Yernisa', 'Department of Agricultural Technology, Faculty of  Agriculture, Jambi University', 'Synthesis of cocoa butter equivalent (CBE) from a mixture of RBDPO olein fraction (refined olein, ROL) and soft-PMF olein fraction (mid olein, MOL) at 5 weight ratios (70/30); (60/40); (50/50); (40/60); (30/70), catalyzed by 0.2% w/w sodium methoxide, have been studied.  Characterization was carried out before and after interesterification, including triacylglycerol composition (AOCS Method Ce 5b-89, 2003), Solid Fat Content (AOCS Method Cd 16b - 93:2017), and Slip Melting Point (AOCS Method Cc 3-25, 2005). Chemical interesterification caused some TAGs were increased, some were decreased, and several new TAGs were formed. Synthesis of target TAGs (POS, SOS) increased as the proportion of MOL in the substrate were increased. ROL/MOL mixture at a weight ratio of 50/50; 40/60; and 30/70 have characteristics similar to cocoabutter (CB) and could be considered as CBE. CBE with TAG markers of more than 70% and CBE index of more than 60% produced from a mixture formulation ROL/MOL at a ratio of 30/70.  This finding confirmed that CBE could be produced from mid olein (a by-product of hard PMF production) and RBD olein. Based on this finding, the economic value of ROL and MOL in the future may be increased and their usage map may also be changed.', 'chemically interesterification; cocoa butter equivalents; palm oi; triacylglycerol', 'Mursalin', 326, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS327-Mursalin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS327-Mursalin.pdf', '2025-10-21 15:26:04', '2025-11-07 12:14:31'),
(328, 'food_science_technology', 'oral presentation', 'TECHNIQUE FOR PRODUCING LOW CHOLESTEROL SPICED MAYONNAISE WITH ADDITION OF MONO-DIASIL GLYCEROL (MDAG) COEMULSIFIER', 'Mursalin Mursalin, Fitry Tafzi, Tasya Dwi Safitri', 'Department of Agricultural Technology, Faculty of  Agriculture, Jambi University, Jalan Raya Jambi-Muara Bulian Km.15 Mendalo Darat, Jambi, Indonesia', 'Synthesis of cocoa butter equivalent (CBE) from a mixture of RBDPO olein fraction (refined olein, ROL) and soft-PMF olein fraction (mid olein, MOL) at 5 weight ratios (70/30); (60/40); (50/50); (40/60); (30/70), catalyzed by 0.2% w/w sodium methoxide, have been studied.  Characterization was carried out before and after interesterification, including triacylglycerol composition (AOCS Method Ce 5b-89, 2003), Solid Fat Content (AOCS Method Cd 16b - 93:2017), and Slip Melting Point (AOCS Method Cc 3-25, 2005). Chemical interesterification caused some TAGs were increased, some were decreased, and several new TAGs were formed. Synthesis of target TAGs (POS, SOS) increased as the proportion of MOL in the substrate were increased. ROL/MOL mixture at a weight ratio of 50/50; 40/60; and 30/70 have characteristics similar to cocoabutter (CB) and could be considered as CBE. CBE with TAG markers of more than 70% and CBE index of more than 60% produced from a mixture formulation ROL/MOL at a ratio of 30/70.  This finding confirmed that CBE could be produced from mid olein (a by-product of hard PMF production) and RBD olein. Based on this finding, the economic value of ROL and MOL in the future may be increased and their usage map may also be changed.', 'low cholesterol mayonnaise; MDAG; palm-based product; seasoning', 'Mursalin', 326, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS328-Mursalin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS328-Mursalin.pdf', '2025-10-22 04:51:01', '2025-11-07 12:15:12'),
(329, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'THE EFFECT OF PH AND BIOCHAR MASS VARIATIONS ON THE EFFICIENCY OF COD AND BOD ABSORPTION LEVELS IN WASTEWATER', 'Teguh Setyawan, Damris M, and Ummi Kalsum', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Biochar is a carbon-rich solid material produced by the conversion of organic waste through incomplete combustion or limited oxygen supply (pyrolysis). Wastewater is the waste water that is discharged from households, industry and other public places, and generally contains materials or substances that can be harmful to human health and disrupt the environment. This study aims to determine the greatest absorption efficiency of COD and BOD levels from wastewater that can be reduced through the adsorption process using biochar. The adsorption process was carried out using variations in weight of pH 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 grams to determine the optimum conditions for the greatest absorption efficiency. The analysis results showed the greatest absorption efficiency for COD levels at pH 6 of 96.953% and 1.5 grams of biochar of 96.33% and BOD levels at pH 8 of 95.589% and 2 grams of biochar of 95.41% by wastewater.', 'Biochar, Wastewater, COD, BOD', 'Teguh Setyawan', 324, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS329-Teguh Setyawan.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS329-Teguh Setyawan.pdf', '2025-10-24 14:43:43', '2025-10-29 04:08:27'),
(330, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'FROM LOCAL ADAPTATION TO NATURE-BASED SOLUTIONS IN THE SOCIO-ECOLOGICAL TRANSFORMATION OF SAYUNG, DEMAK', 'Retno Sari Dewi\nElma Mutmainnah\nAkbar Wira\nAyu Aini Rachmawati\nM. Shulhan Jihadi', 'Department of Environmental Engineering Universitas Diponegoro, Semarang, Indonesia\nCenter for Urban and Regional Resilience Research Universitas Diponegoro, Semarang, Indonesia\nDepartment of Urban and Regional Planning Universitas Diponegoro, Semarang, Indonesia\nDepartment of Urban and Regional Planning Universitas Diponegoro, Semarang, Indonesia\nDepartment of Marine Science Universitas Diponegoro, Semarang, Indonesia', 'The northern coastal region of Java Island has undergone rapid socio-ecological transformations due to tidal flooding, coastal abrasion, and land subsidence. This study examines the dynamics of the coastal ecotone in Sayung, Demak Regency, highlighting the shift from local adaptive strategies toward the implementation of Nature-Based Solutions (NbS). Field observations and direct interviews were conducted in two villages—Wonorejo and Timbulsloko—on September 20, 2025. The results indicate significant landscape changes, starting from rice fields that were converted into aquaculture ponds and eventually transformed into mangrove conservation areas. Local communities have developed various adaptation strategies, such as building floating settlements, elevating houses, and conducting mangrove rehabilitation. However, persistent environmental pressures have gradually exceeded the communities’ adaptive capacity, leading to social and economic vulnerabilities. The findings emphasize the importance of integrating local knowledge with the NbS concept to enhance resilience and restore ecological functions in degraded coastal areas. Furthermore, this study contributes to understanding adaptive transformation processes in Indonesian coastal ecotones and supports the development of community-based coastal management frameworks.', 'Coastal Ecotone; Sayung; Community Adaptation; Land Subsidence; Nature-Based Solutions', 'Elma Mutmainnah', 199, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS330-Elma Mutmainnah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS330-Elma Mutmainnah.pdf', '2025-10-27 07:32:41', '2025-11-22 03:38:19'),
(331, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'Arthropoda Community Structure in Sweet Corn-Turmeric Intercropping Agroecosystem', 'Wilyus Wilyus1,*, Dwi Ristyadi1, Asniwita1, Fuad Nurdiansyah1', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Arthropod diversity plays an important role in maintaining the balance and ecological resilience of agroecosystems. Intercropping systems provide more diverse habitats and resources, which can attract various arthropod species and influence community composition. This study aimed to investigate the diversity of arthropods in the sweet corn–turmeric intercropping agroecosystem. The research was conducted at the Experimental Farm of the Faculty of Agriculture, University of Jambi, and arthropod identification was carried out in the Plant Pest Laboratory. The observation variables focused on species diversity, abundance, and ecological functional groups of arthropods. Arthropod samples were collected at the early of the sweet corn vegetative phase using pitfall traps, yellow sticky traps, and sweep nets. Data were analyzed using the Shannon–Wiener diversity index (H′), dominance index (D), Evenness index (E), and true alpha diversity (¹D) = eᴴ′).  The results showed that the values of H′, D, E, and ¹D were 2.64, 0.09, 0.88, and 13,96, respectively. The ecological functional structure of arthropods consisted of herbivores (25.0%), predators (61.1%), parasitoids (2.8%), pollinators (5.6%), and decomposers (2.8%). These results indicate that the sweet corn–turmeric intercropping agroecosystem supports high arthropod diversity, a stable community structure, and provides favorable conditions for the conservation of natural enemies, particularly predators.\n\n', 'Arthropod diversity; intercropping; sweet corn; turmeric; ecological roles', 'Wilyus', 329, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS331-Wilyus.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS331-Wilyus.pdf', '2025-10-29 23:48:16', '2025-11-07 11:44:45'),
(332, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Introduction of Mining Virtual Reality (Mining VR) to Geology and Mining Students of Vocational School Muhammadiyah Jambi City', 'Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa *1), Aditya Denny Prabawa 1), Yudi Arista Yulanda 1), Jarot Wiratama 1), Bagus Adhitya 2), Yosa Megasukma 1), Dimas Agung Permadi 1), Affif Farras Chan1), Siti Sadariah 1),', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The employment of vocational high school graduates in the mining sector remains relatively low compared to other educational levels. With the continued expansion of the mining industry in Indonesia, particularly in Jambi Province, the establishment of vocational high schools with Mining Geology majors in Jambi City is anticipated to contribute to the supply of workers. The scarcity of teachers, particularly those with a mining education background, can be a significant factor in the decline in the quantity and quality of these vocational high school graduates. This program seeks to introduce Mining Virtual Reality (Mining VR) as a contemporary learning methodology for mining. Utilizing this simulation program, students can engage directly with various mining activities within the classroom without incurring substantial financial and time expenditures. Furthermore, they gain access to restricted areas, such as underground mines and blasting zones. The intended outcomes of this program encompass: (1) augmenting student knowledge and comprehension of several mining learning modules, (2) fostering students’ practical skills in the utilization of digital technology through Virtual Reality Simulators, and (3) disseminating activities in the form of articles on social media platforms and comprehensive reports. This program is expected to enhance the quality of graduates of Muhammadiyah Vocational High Schools specializing in Mining Geology.', 'mining virtual reality, learning digitalisation, Vocational High Schools majoring in Mining Geology', 'Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa', 322, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS332-Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS332-Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa.pdf', '2025-10-31 03:48:33', '2025-11-07 11:56:21'),
(333, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'CHARACTERIZATION OF DAILY SOLAR RADIATION INTENSITY BASED ON IN-SITU DATA USING THE BH1750 SENSOR', 'Jesi Pebralia, Yoza Fendriani, M. Ficky Afrianto, Frastica Deswardani, Alrizal', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study aims to characterize the daily solar radiation intensity using in-situ data obtained from the BH1750 sensor. The measurement system was designed using the BH1750 light sensor, with data transmitted wirelessly to the ThingSpeak cloud platform for storage and analysis. Data collection was carried out over two consecutive days in an open area with a recording interval of one hour. The measurement results show that the intensity pattern on the first day reached its peak (approximately 62 kLx) at 12:00 noon. This pattern exhibits a nearly symmetric distribution, representing relatively clear and stable weather conditions around midday. In contrast, the second day’s peak intensity (around 65 kLx) occurred earlier, at 11:00, and rose again at 13:00, indicating sharp intensity fluctuations. These fluctuations resulted in two distinct local peaks before the intensity declined to zero at sunset. The differences in peak timing and daily fluctuation patterns highlight the need for further analysis using machine learning approaches to accurately model the complex and non-linear relationships within solar radiation data.', 'Radiation Intensity; Sensor BH1750; Machine Learning', 'Alrizal', 330, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS333-Alrizal.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS333-Alrizal.pdf', '2025-10-31 04:44:02', '2025-11-22 03:34:05'),
(334, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'MORPHOLOGICAL AND PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES OF Coffea liberica TO NPK AND SWIFTLET MANURE ON ULTISOL', 'Elis Kartika1,3, Lizawati1,3, Adzuan Handi Duansyah1, Yusnaidar2,3, Madyawati Latief2,3*, Samsidar3,4, Indra Lasmana Tarigan2,3', '1Department of Agroecotechnology, Faculty of Agriculture, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n2Department of Chemistry, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n3The University Centre of Excellences, E2-KOLIM, Universitas Jambi\n4Department of Physic, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Indonesia\n', 'Ultisol soils are characterized by low fertility, acidic pH, and limited nutrient availability, which constrain the early growth of Coffea liberica seedlings. This study aimed to evaluate the effects of combining NPK fertilizer and swiftlet manure on the morphological and physiological growth of C. liberica seedlings grown in Ultisol soil. The experiment employed a completely randomized design (CRD) with a single factor consisting of six treatments: (1) control (no fertilizer), (2) 100% recommended NPK dose, and combinations of 50% NPK with swiftlet manure at (3) 150 g, (4) 300 g, (5) 450 g, and (6) 600 g per polybag. Observed parameters included plant height, stem diameter, number of leaves, root and shoot dry weight, total leaf area, chlorophyll content, and N, P, K uptake. Results showed that the combined application of NPK fertilizer and swiftlet manure significantly affected both morphological and physiological growth of C. liberica seedlings. The optimal response was obtained with 300 g of swiftlet manure combined with 50% NPK, producing the highest plant vigor, nutrient uptake efficiency, and biomass accumulation while improving Ultisol soil properties. This combination effectively reduced NPK use by half without compromising growth performance, suggesting a sustainable, cost-efficient, and environmentally friendly fertilization strategy for C. liberica seedling production on acidic soils', 'Coffea liberica; Ultisol; NPK fertilizer; swiftlet manure; seedling growth; nutrient uptake; sustainable fertilization', 'Indra Lasmana Tarigan', 332, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS334-Madyawati Latief.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS334-Madyawati Latief.pdf', '2025-10-31 04:59:37', '2025-10-31 05:49:06'),
(335, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', ' EVALUATION OF LEAK ELEVATION EFFECTS ON AMMONIA STORAGE RELEASE CONSEQUENCES USING ALOHA SIMULATION', 'Aldillah Herlambang, Beatric Erlina Sitohang', 'University of Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia', 'communities due to its toxic and flammable properties. This study investigates the influence of leak hole elevation on Quantitative Risk Assessment (QRA) outcomes for the 5,000 MT Ammonia Storage Tank 4101-F at PT Pupuk Sriwidjaja Palembang, located near residential areas. Simulations were conducted using ALOHA 5.4.7 software to compare two scenarios: low elevation leak (0.5 m above ground) and high elevation leak (10 m above ground), with a 3-inch diameter hole under worst-case meteorological conditions (stability class F, wind speed 2 m/s). Four consequence scenarios were evaluated: toxic vapor cloud, flammable vapor cloud, vapor cloud explosion, and BLEVE. Results demonstrated that low elevation leaks generated significantly higher release rates (1,820 kg/min) compared to high elevation leaks (974 kg/min) due to greater hydrostatic pressure. The toxic vapor cloud ERPG-3 zone extended to 1.4 km for low elevation versus 0.953 km for high elevation. Similarly, flammable vapor cloud LEL zones reached 156 m and 105 m, while vapor cloud explosion overpressure (1 psi) zones extended to 168 m and 114 m, respectively. The findings reveal that leak elevation substantially affects consequence dispersion patterns and hazard zone extents, providing critical insights for emergency response planning and safety system optimization in ammonia storage facilities.', 'Ammonia Storage; Quantitative Risk Assessment; Leak Elevation; ALOHA Simulation; Consequence Analysis', 'Beatric Erlina Sitohang ', 328, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS335-Beatric Erlina Sitohang.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS335-Beatric Erlina Sitohang.pdf', '2025-10-31 07:19:28', '2025-11-22 17:02:52'),
(336, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'IDENTIFICATION OF ACTIVE FAULT TRACES OF “PALEO EARTHQUAKES” USING GRAVITY METHODS IN JANGKAT, MERANGIN REGENCY ', 'Anggi Deliana Siregar\nD. M. Magdalena Ritonga\nSarwo Sucitra Amin\nMaulina Tanjung \nShazkya Annura Rizky\nPius Lauren Datu Bara', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Indonesia is a country with high seismic activity because it is located at the meeting point of four major tectonic plates, resulting in numerous active faults and susceptibility to geological disasters such as earthquakes and landslides. Despite the high risk, data on active faults and geological disasters in Indonesia is still limited. This study focuses on geological mapping to identify Active Fault Traces, specifically “Paleo Earthquakes,” in Jangkat District, Jambi Province. The purpose of this study is to update and verify the position of faults from old geological maps, as well as to interpret new deformation mechanisms resulting from the interaction of geological structures in the field. Through preliminary studies and analysis of field data, as well as secondary geophysical data. Based on the results of geological structure measurements in the field, there are three fault locations consisting of a left-lateral strike-slip fault, a right-lateral strike-slip fault, and a right-lateral strike-slip fault. Analysis of the residual map shows residual gravity anomalies with amplitudes of -9.6 to +11.0 mGal. The dominance of positive anomalies in the southwest is interpreted as an active fault and is significantly consistent with the distribution of active faults identified in the field. A linear residual anomaly pattern with a relatively high northwest-southeast gradient that correlates directly with the fault segment can be seen on the alignment map, which shows the general direction pattern. The results of the research location analysis are traces of active faults from paleo-earthquakes that have been identified through field and gravity data.', 'Active Faults, Geological Disasters, Gravity', 'Anggi Deliana Siregar', 335, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS336-Anggi Deliana Siregar.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS336-Anggi Deliana Siregar.pdf', '2025-10-31 09:51:49', '2025-11-17 13:02:54'),
(337, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'Cyprinidae Diversity and Ecosystem Services in Aceh: A Systematic Study for Sustainable Fisheries Management', 'Andri Yusman Persada, Andhika Puspito Nugroho, and Rury Eprilurahman', 'Faculty of Biology, Universitas Gadjah Mada, Yogyakarta, Indonesia', 'Family Cyprinidae is the largest group of freshwater fish in the world. It plays an important role in maintaining the balance of ecosystems, including nutrient cycles, food webs, and community stability. This study aims to identify the biodiversity, distribution, and conservation status of Cyprinidae in Aceh, as well as their role in supporting ecosystems, communities, and social sustainability. Data were compiled from Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF), Encyclopedia of Life (EOL), FishBase, IUCN Red List, and related scientific articles up to September 2025. A descriptive analysis of geographic distribution, species richness, and conservation status was conducted based on IUCN Criteria. The results show that there are 41 species from 12 Cyprinidae genera in Aceh. These species are dominated by Rasbora, Osteochilus, Tor, and Barbonymus. The spatial distribution shows that the districts and cities in South Aceh and Middle Aceh have the highest biodiversity. Most species are in the Least Concern category, but some genera, such as Barbodes and Tor, are highly threatened due to habitat degradation and human activity. The high diversity of the Cyprinidae family demonstrates its important role in providing ecosystem services, such as a food source, regulating ecosystems, and providing cultural value. This study emphasizes the need for an ecosystem-based approach to fisheries management that integrates biodiversity conservation with socio-economic sustainability. Integrating local knowledge and modern science could produce a Cyprinidae-based bioeconomic model that supports both conservation and community welfare in the inland waters of Aceh', 'Aceh, biodiversity, conservation, cyprinidae, sustainable fisheries', 'Andri Yusman Persada', 311, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS337-Andri Yusman Persada.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS337-Andri Yusman Persada.pdf', '2025-10-31 11:13:16', '2025-11-07 11:48:53'),
(338, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'PERFORMANCE OF ELECTRO-FENTON PROCESS USING IRON–GRAPHITE ELECTRODES FOR 30-MINUTE PALM MILL OIL EFFLUENT TREATMENT', 'Rifan Rizki¹, Perdana Alfarez2, Nita Widyastuti3, Sri Mundarti4, Rara Ayu Lestary5, Ra’ida Raudhatussya’rifah6, Hadistya Suryadri7, and Oki Alfernando8\nCorresponding author email: nitawidyastuti@unja.ac.id\n', 'Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia', 'Palm Oil Mill Effluent (POME) possesses a high organic load and low biodegradability, making direct biological treatment ineffective. Therefore, a pretreatment stage is required to reduce organic pollutants prior to further processes. This study investigated the Electro-Fenton (EF) process as an alternative pretreatment method using iron–graphite electrodes operated at current variations of 0.1, 0.25, 0.4, 0.55, and 0.7 A for 30 minutes. Hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) was introduced to accelerate hydroxyl radical (•OH) formation, promoting oxidation of complex organic compounds in POME. The process performance was evaluated by observing changes in Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD), pH, temperature, electrode dissolution, and effluent characteristics. The results showed that COD removal efficiency increased with current intensity, achieving an optimum of about 55% reduction at 0.4 A. Higher currents (0.55–0.7 A) yielded similar COD reductions but caused greater electrode dissolution. Temperature remained stable (30–37 °C), while pH slightly increased from 3.2 to 3.5. The effluent appearance became clearer and less odorous. The novelty of this study lies in applying the EF process with Fe–graphite electrodes for short-time (30 min) POME pretreatment, highlighting an efficient and rapid oxidation mechanism. This research contributes to applied physics through the analysis of electrochemical parameters, such as current density, redox reactions, and electrode behavior that govern radical generation and pollutant degradation, offering insights for optimizing industrial wastewater treatment systems', 'Electro-Fenton, POME, COD removal, Iron–graphite electrodes, wastewater pretreatment', 'RIFAN RIZKI', 334, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS338-RIFAN RIZKI.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS338-RIFAN RIZKI.pdf', '2025-10-31 12:46:56', '2025-11-26 07:56:01'),
(339, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'DETECTION OF FLUORIDE IONS USING METAL-ORGANIC FRAMEWORKS (MOFs) TYPE UiO-66-NH2@Histidine', 'Muh Rizal B', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Histidine-functionalized UiO-66-NH2 (UiO-66-NH2@Histidine), as a novel “turn-on” fluorescent probe for detecting fluoride ions, has been successfully synthesized using the solvothermal method. The results showed that fluoride ion detection gave the best fluorescence response in water media, and no response was observed in non-polar solvents. Detection at pH 3–10 produces a relatively similar fluorescence response and decreases quite significantly at pH 11–13. UiO-66-NH2@Hst is very selective and stable towards fluoride ions, as evidenced by the appearance of blue luminescence under UV light (λ365 nm) compared to other ions. A possible mechanism for detecting fluoride ions is through the formation of hydrogen bonds, which results in incremental electron transfer from organic ligands to Zr-oxo clusters. The LoD value obtained in this study was 0.013 ppm, which is smaller than the maximum concentration of fluoride ions in drinking water samples (1.5 ppm) set by the World Health Organization (WHO). Therefore, UiO-66-NH2@Hst can be a candidate fluorescence-based sensor for fluoride ions.', 'Fluoride ions, Fluorescent, UiO-66-NH2', 'Muh Rizal B', 336, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS339-Muh Rizal B.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS339-Muh Rizal B.pdf', '2025-10-31 13:38:39', '2025-11-22 03:37:19'),
(340, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'TERMITICIDAL ACTIVITY OF METHANOL EXTRACT OF  TETRACERA INDICA LEAVES AND DETERMINATION  OF CHEMICAL COMPONENTS USING GC-MS', 'Dina Erliana1, Munifilia Ekasari2, Uni Baroroh3, Nurul Pratiwi4, Melyanda5, Sindi Haryani6, Tio Ersa Relyansyah7, Muhammad Irfan Nur widad8', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The purposes of this research to investigate the termiticide activity of methanol extract of Tetracera indica against Coptotermes curvignathus Holmgren using the no choice test method, and to determine the chemical components of extract by using GC-MS. The stages of this research were: sampling, preparation extract of the Tetracera indica plant using the maceration method, phytochemical screening, determination of chemical components using GC-MS, and termiticidal activity testing. Termiticide activity tests showed that the percentage of termite mortality increased with the concentration applied. Concentrations of 20% and 25% resulted in the highest termite mortality, reaching 100%. Phytochemical screening results of flavonoids, saponins, tannins, steroids/terpenoids, and GC-MS analysis showed 26 chemical components, with the main compounds being methyl octadecanoate and methyl palmitate.\n', 'Tetracera indica, GC-MS, Termiticide', 'Sindi Haryani', 337, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS340-Dina Erliana.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS340-Dina Erliana.pdf', '2025-10-31 14:05:52', '2025-11-26 11:53:09'),
(341, 'big_data_analytics', 'oral presentation', 'Heterogeneous Vision-Language Ensemble for Multi-Account Instagram Engagement Prediction', 'Jefri Marzal\nMuhammad Razi\nMiranty Yudistira\nAkhiyar Waladi\nHamzah Alghifari', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Predicting social media engagement remains challenging due to heterogeneous user behaviors and complex multimodal signals. Existing approaches predominantly employ single convolutional neural network (ConvNet) architectures with simple text embeddings, overlooking the semantic understanding capabilities of modern vision-language transformers and the benefits of architectural diversity. We propose a heterogeneous ensemble framework combining three ConvNet architectures (ResNet-50, ConvNeXt, EfficientNet-B0) with two vision-language transformers (CLIP, Vision Transformer) for Instagram engagement prediction. Through systematic ablation studies across 22 experiments on 10,520 posts from 8 Instagram accounts, we demonstrate that architectural heterogeneity yields superior performance compared to homogeneous ensembles. Our analysis reveals that account-specific features provide the largest single improvement (10.2% MAE reduction), while vision-language models contribute complementary semantic information (+0.42% improvement over ConvNet-only ensemble). The framework achieves mean absolute error of 170.77 likes with Ridge meta-learning employing negative ensemble weights for systematic error correction—an advantage unavailable to constrained methods. Notably, we observe 30-fold performance variance across accounts (MAE: 10.18–295.29), indicating highly heterogeneous engagement dynamics. Uncertainty analysis demonstrates well-calibrated ensemble disagreement (r=0.51 with prediction error), enabling confidence-based deployment where low-uncertainty predictions achieve MAE of 19.33. Systematic failure mode analysis identifies posts without faces as exhibiting 46% higher error, providing actionable insights for future feature engineering. Our results establish state-of-the-art performance while maintaining training efficiency at 4.9 minutes on consumer-grade GPUS', 'Vision-language models, multimodal fusion, ensemble learning, Instagram engagement prediction, social media analytics, CLIP, vision transformer, heterogeneous architectures, meta-learning, deep learning', 'Jefri Marzal', 308, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS341-Akhiyar Waladi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS341-Akhiyar Waladi.pdf', '2025-10-31 14:52:58', '2026-09-25 10:37:17'),
(342, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'REVITALIZING FACULTY OPEN SPACE THROUGH LOCAL FLORA AND TECHNOLOGICAL INTEGRATION', 'Mahya Ihsan, Bambang Hariyadi, Fetty Febriasti Bahar, Nurza Purwa Abiyoga, Andre Rabiula', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Green open spaces within university environments play a crucial role in enhancing academic, social, and ecological quality. At the Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi (FST UNJA), the existing park has not yet functioned optimally due to a non-functional landscape layout, unsystematic use of local flora, and the absence of technological integration to support sustainability. These conditions have resulted in limited social interaction, inadequate environmental education functions, and a lack of intelligent systems for efficient green space management. Therefore, revitalizing the faculty’s open space has become an urgent initiative to realize the university’s vision of creating a green, innovative, and competitive academic environment. This study aims to design, implement, and integrate a technology-based green park (smart eco-park) that functions as a communal area, an interdisciplinary learning platform, and a pilot model for sustainable open-space management. Using an applied research design combining qualitative and quantitative methods, the study comprises four stages: pre-design analysis and surveys, landscape and technological system design, physical implementation, and subsequent evaluation. The main innovations include the use of local flora, application of smart irrigation systems, humidity sensors, and plant QR codes to enhance user engagement and data-driven environmental learning. This research contributes to scientific advancement and physics education by demonstrating the practical application of sensor-based monitoring systems, environmental data acquisition, and energy-efficient technologies to support sustainability and interdisciplinary learning within a university setting.', 'Campus Green Open Space; Landscape Revitalization; Local Flora; Smart Eco-Park; Smart Technology', 'Nurza Purwa Abiyoga', 339, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS342-Nurza Purwa Abiyoga.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS342-Nurza Purwa Abiyoga.pdf', '2025-10-31 15:30:16', '2025-11-07 11:48:03'),
(343, 'green_chemistry', 'oral presentation', 'Chelating Zinc(II) in Crude Pheophorbide Gives It CO₂-Reducing Capability', 'Muhammad Irhash Shalihin\nPanji Cakra Buana', 'Universitas Jambi', 'A pheophorbide-based photocatalyst prepared from water hyacinth (Pontederia crassipes) was developed for photocatalytic CO₂ reduction. Crude pheophorbide was prepared from the chlorophyll extract of fresh water hyacinth leaves by a sequential acid–base treatment. The successful conversion of chlorophyll to pheophorbide was indicated by the decreased absorbance intensity and slight red shift of the Qy band from around 662 nm to approximately 667 nm, indicating demetallation at the chlorophyll core as well as dephytylation. Preliminary photocatalytic investigation proved that the presence of a Zn center in pheophorbide resulted in its ability to convert CO₂ into CO and other organic compounds in water as the solvent under light irradiation. CO and organic molecules within a closed reaction system were detected and confirmed photocatalytic reduction of CO₂. However, bubbles formed during the process, probably owing to residual impurities carried in the crude chlorophyll precursor, degraded reactor stability. Additional purification and optimization therefore are required for further stability and effectiveness of the catalyst. Although this research is at the initial stage, this study demonstrates the feasibility of utilizing water hyacinth–derived pheophorbide–Zn complexes as potential organometallic photocatalysts for CO₂ reduction in support of green chemistry principles.', 'CO₂, Chlorophyll, Pheophorbide, Photocatalysis, Zinc', 'Muhammad Irhash Shalihin', 221, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS343-Muhammad Irhash Shalihin.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS343-Muhammad Irhash Shalihin.pdf', '2025-10-31 16:50:12', '2025-11-22 03:35:46'),
(344, 'advanced_material', 'oral presentation', 'Influence of Plasticizer Type on Starch-Based Nanocomposite Films Reinforced with CMC and ZnO', 'Rara Ayu Lestary, Ra\'ida Raudhatussya\'rifah, Rosmawati Sipayung, Sri Mundarti, Defia Indah Permatasari, Chindy Mauliza Duana', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The development of eco-friendly composite films from renewable resources represents a crucial strategy to reduce dependence on petroleum-based plastics. In this study, carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) derived from oil palm empty fruit bunches (OPEFB) was utilized along with starch and zinc oxide nanoparticles (ZnO NPs) to fabricate biodegradable composite films. Two types of plasticizers, glycerol and sorbitol, were incorporated at varying concentrations of 20%, 30%, and 40% to evaluate their influence on the physical properties of the films, including density, thickness, moisture content, water absorption, and tensile strength. The results revealed that both plasticizers significantly affected the structural and physical characteristics of the films. Glycerol addition decreased density from 1.413 g/cm³ to 0.463 g/cm³ and increased moisture content and water absorption, whereas sorbitol addition increased density from 1.330 g/cm³ to 1.559 g/cm³ and reduced moisture content and water absorption. The formulation containing 20% glycerol showed optimal results for higher density and water resistance, while 40% sorbitol provided superior performance with enhanced mechanical integrity and lower water sensitivity. Overall, the type and concentration of plasticizer play a critical role in determining the physical properties of OPEFB-derived CMC/starch/ZnO nanocomposite films, demonstrating their potential as environmentally friendly packaging materials to replace conventional plastics.', 'carboxymethyl cellulose; oil palm empty fruit bunches; sorbitol; glycerol; composite film', 'Rara Ayu Lestary', 327, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS344-Rara Ayu Lestary.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS344-Rara Ayu Lestary.pdf', '2025-11-01 05:53:43', '2025-11-24 17:21:09');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(345, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'ETHNOSCIENCE OF ENAU (ARENGA PINNATA): EXPLORING THE LOCAL KNOWLEDGE OF JAMBI COMMUNITIES FOR CONTEXTUAL SCIENCE LEARNING', 'Bambang Hariyadi, Azizah Turrohmah, Winda D. Kartika, Cut Multahadah, Rahma Dani, Danial Mursyid, Fitri Astriawati, Ine Tentia', 'Biology Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi \nBiology Education Program, Faculty of Education and Techer Training, Universitas Jambi \nMathematics Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi \nPhysics Education Program, Faculty of Education and Techer Training, Universitas Jambi \n', 'Several traditional communities possess rich local knowledge and traditions related to the management of the sugar palm (Arenga pinnata), encompassing ecological, economic, and cultural values. However, much of this knowledge remains undocumented and has not been optimally utilized in science education. This study aims to explore and document local community knowledge about the sugar palm and to examine the ethnoscientific values embedded within it for potential integration into contextual biology learning. The research was conducted in Jambi Tulo Village, Muaro Jambi Regency, using a descriptive qualitative approach. Informants were selected purposively and expanded using snowball sampling, involving sap tappers, palm sugar producers, customary leaders, and community members possessing traditional knowledge related to the sugar palm. Data were collected through participant observation, in-depth interviews, visual documentation, and literature review, and analyzed using thematic and content analysis to identify relevant scientific concepts. The results indicate that the traditional management of the sugar palm reflects an integration between local knowledge and biological concepts such as plant structure and function, physiological processes (osmosis, root pressure), as well as ecological and conservation principles. Integrating these ethnoscientific values into biology learning can enrich learning contexts, strengthen students’ scientific understanding, and foster ecological awareness and appreciation of local wisdom.', 'Arenga pinnata; local knowledge; contextual learning; ethnobotany; ethnoscience', 'Winda Dwi Kartika', 260, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS345-Bambang Hariyadi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS345-Bambang Hariyadi.pdf', '2025-11-01 22:58:55', '2025-11-02 07:36:06'),
(346, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'GROUNDWATER MODELING USING KRIGING INTERPOLATION TECHNIQUE WITH GEOLECTRICAL TEST DATABASE', '1. Muhammad Guntur, M.Si (muhammadguntur@unja.ac.id)\n2. M. Farandika Akbar, M.Si (farandikaakbar@unja.ac.id)\n3. Royhan Himawan Putra, S.T (royhanhimawanputra@gmail.com)', '1. Program Studi Teknik Lingkungan Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi Universitas Jambi\n2. Program Studi Agribisnis Fakultas Pertanian Universitas Jambi\n3. Program Studi Teknik Lingkungan Fakultas Teknik Universitas Batanghari', 'Groundwater availability is one of the primary needs in a particular area nowadays. Nagari Sopan Jaya, where most of the community using groundwater for domestic needs and economic activities, including palm oil cultivation, which requires groundwater to support crop productivity and growth. This study aims to provide information on groundwater availability through contour conditions, depth, and groundwater flow patterns using GIS-based groundwater modeling with kriging interpolation techniques using geolectrical test data. The results of the study show that the contour of groundwater availability is at an elevation of 84 mdpl to 131 mdpl, then the depth of groundwater based on the model results varies from 6 meters to 15 meters from the ground surface with a groundwater flow pattern moving from east to west. In addition, the results of the geoelectric test show that most of the aquifers located along the measurement line are confined aquifers, with the water table being highly dependent on rainfall intensity. From the results of the groundwater modeling, it can be implied that in Nagari Sopan Jaya, with a minimum groundwater depth of 6 meters from the ground surface and a groundwater supply that is highly dependent on rainfall intensity, priority concern is needed considering the dependence on rainfall, which can affect groundwater availability up to 15 meters during the dry season. The results of this study are expected to serve as consideration for relevant parties and the local community in utilizing groundwater for domestic needs and productive activities in a sustainable way.', 'Groundwater Modelling, Interpolation Kriging, Geolectrical Test, GIS', 'M. Farandika Akbar', 341, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS346-M. Farandika Akbar.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS346-M. Farandika Akbar.pdf', '2025-11-08 03:41:57', '2025-11-17 13:04:00'),
(347, 'socio_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'IoT-BASED LOW-COST SENSORS DEVELOPMENT AS AN ALTERNATIVE FOR CITY’S AMBIENT PM2.5 MONITORING IN INDONESIA ', 'Rizki Andre Handika1*, Yosi Riduas Hais1, Damris Muhammad1, Febri Juita Anggraini1, Kemas Rahmat Wiharja2, Iqbal Mutawaqil1, Putri Ayu Risti1, Aida Khairani1, Rahmi Yulia Fitri1, and Tegar Rizki Saputra1', '1 Faculty of science & Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi 36361, Indonesia \n2 School of Computing, Telkom University, Bandung 40257, Indonesia\n', 'Air quality has become a critical environmental problem globally, in which ambient PM2.5 got the most consideration. Since more than 90% of the population were alive in PM2.5 concentrations above the WHO standard, efforts in air pollution monitoring and control have been recognized. The monitoring aspect emerged to be important for mitigation to attain appropriate control strategies based on the problems. However, about 1 billion rupiah is needed for one air quality monitoring station (AQMS), making it still unavailable for each city in Indonesia. Moreover, numerous AQMS is needed to have comprehensive monitoring in Indonesian cities. This paper presents recent progress of an Internet of Things (IoT)-based low-cost sensor (LCS) development that is still studied in Jambi city as an alternative AQMS solution for ambient PM2.5 monitoring. Our two-year work and achievements will be reported, which were started from aurduino-based to ESP32 microcontrollers with some trial LCSs (from GP2Y1010AU, ZH03B, PMS5003, until PMS7003 sensors), while the next work planned will be discussed with focusing on accuration and validation. The proposed system harnesses affordable optical sensors with IoT connectivity, emphasizing robust calibration, real-time data delivery, and cloud-based visualization adaptable to local atmospheric conditions.', 'PM2.5 pollutant, AQMS, low-cost sensor, IoT-based, city levels.', 'Rizki Andre Handika', 321, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS347-Rizki Andre Handika.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS347-Rizki Andre Handika.pdf', '2025-11-11 10:29:28', '2025-11-14 13:57:28'),
(348, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'A REVIEW OF MICROBIAL DEGRADATION OF PLASTIC: ENZYMES AND ORGANISMS', 'Zuli Rodhiyah and Silvia Annur Annisa', 'UNIVERSITAS JAMBI', 'This review synthesizes research on systematic analysis of microbial applications in degrading plastic and microplastic waste, focusing on bacteria, fungi, actinomycetes, and enzymes such as PETase, cutinase, and laccase to address persistent plastic pollution and remediation challenges. The review aimed to evaluate microbial taxa involved in biodegradation, benchmark enzymatic activities, identify consortia strategies, elucidate metabolic pathways, and assess environmental influences on degradation efficiency. Literature was selected based on comprehensive inclusion of experimental, metagenomic, enzymatic, and biotechnological studies spanning global environmental microbiology and synthetic biology, analyzed through comparative and critical frameworks. Findings reveal diverse microbial communities, including key bacterial and fungal genera, with consortia exhibiting synergistic enzymatic cooperation that enhances polymer breakdown. Enzymatic mechanisms of PETase, cutinase, and laccase demonstrate variable catalytic efficiencies, improved by protein engineering and immobilization, though challenges remain for non-PET plastics. Metabolic pathways for monomer assimilation and gene regulation are partially elucidated, with environmental factors such as temperature and pH critically modulating microbial and enzymatic performance. Integration of multi-omics and synthetic biology advances supports enhanced biodegradation but highlights scalability and methodological standardization gaps. These findings collectively underscore the potential of microbial and enzymatic systems for sustainable plastic waste remediation while emphasizing the need for integrative approaches to optimize efficacy and application in diverse environmental contexts. ', 'Plastic Degradation; Microorganisms; Bioremediation; Environmental Microbiology.', 'Zuli Rodhiyah, S.Si., MT', 343, 'rejected', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', NULL, NULL, '2025-11-15 07:18:57', '2025-11-18 17:39:14'),
(349, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'A REVIEW OF MICROBIAL DEGRADATION OF PLASTIC: ENZYMES AND ORGANISMS', 'Zuli Rodhiyah and Silvia Annur Annisa', 'UNIVERSITAS JAMBI', 'This review synthesizes research on systematic analysis of microbial applications in degrading plastic and microplastic waste, focusing on bacteria, fungi, actinomycetes, and enzymes such as PETase, cutinase, and laccase to address persistent plastic pollution and remediation challenges. The review aimed to evaluate microbial taxa involved in biodegradation, benchmark enzymatic activities, identify consortia strategies, elucidate metabolic pathways, and assess environmental influences on degradation efficiency. Literature was selected based on comprehensive inclusion of experimental, metagenomic, enzymatic, and biotechnological studies spanning global environmental microbiology and synthetic biology, analyzed through comparative and critical frameworks. Findings reveal diverse microbial communities, including key bacterial and fungal genera, with consortia exhibiting synergistic enzymatic cooperation that enhances polymer breakdown. Enzymatic mechanisms of PETase, cutinase, and laccase demonstrate variable catalytic efficiencies, improved by protein engineering and immobilization, though challenges remain for non-PET plastics. Metabolic pathways for monomer assimilation and gene regulation are partially elucidated, with environmental factors such as temperature and pH critically modulating microbial and enzymatic performance. Integration of multi-omics and synthetic biology advances supports enhanced biodegradation but highlights scalability and methodological standardization gaps. These findings collectively underscore the potential of microbial and enzymatic systems for sustainable plastic waste remediation while emphasizing the need for integrative approaches to optimize efficacy and application in diverse environmental contexts. ', 'Plastic Degradation; Microorganisms; Bioremediation; Environmental Microbiology.', 'Silvia Annur Annisa', 343, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS349-Silvia Annur Annisa.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS349-Silvia Annur Annisa.pdf', '2025-11-15 07:22:31', '2025-11-22 03:36:13'),
(350, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'IMPLEMENTATION OF HOUSEHOLD ORGANIC WASTE PROCESSOR USING AEROBIC AND ANAEROBIC FERMENTATION METHODS', 'Bagus Adhitya, Muhammad Ikrar Lagowa, Aditya Denny Prabawa, Jarot Wiratama, Yosa Megasukma, and Maulina Tanjung', 'Teknik Kebumian, Fakultas Sains dan Teknologi, Universitas Jambi', 'The problem of household organic waste management has become a serious challenge in many regions of Indonesia, including RT 10 of Sungai Duren Village, Muaro Jambi Regency. Most residents still rely on traditional methods such as burning or disposing of waste in vacant land, which leads to environmental pollution, foul odors, and serves as a breeding ground for disease vectors such as flies and rats. In fact, more than 57% of the total 67.8 million tons of national waste per year consists of organic waste that can actually be processed into useful products. The main obstacles faced by the community are the lack of appropriate waste-processing equipment designs and low literacy in waste management. This community service program aims to design and implement a household-scale organic waste processing device that integrates aerobic and anaerobic fermentation in a single, unified system. The implementation method uses Focus Group Discussion (FGD) with a participatory approach so that the community can be actively involved in all stages of the activity, which include: preparation and initial survey, design and fabrication process, socialization, as well as monitoring and evaluation. Monitoring is carried out using an online survey via Google Forms to assess the effectiveness, ease of use, and sustainability of the device. The results show that the device functions effectively in reducing the volume of household organic waste and produces compost and biogas that can be reused. In addition, there is an increase in community knowledge, awareness, and participation in independent, sustainable, and environmentally friendly waste management.', 'Organic_waste; Composter; Aerobic–anaerobic_fermentation ', 'Bagus Adhitya', 263, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS350-Bagus Adhitya.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS350-Bagus Adhitya.pdf', '2025-11-17 05:11:06', '2025-11-26 11:54:09'),
(351, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'DEVELOPING A FORENSIC ENGINEERING MODEL FOR INVESTIGATION BUILDING CONSTRUCTION FAILURES: A LITERATURE REVIEW', 'Tatang Kukuh Wibawa1, Yusuf Latief2\n1 Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Universitas Indonesia, Depok, Indonesia\n2 Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Universitas Indonesia, Depok, Indonesia\n', 'Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Universitas Indonesia, Depok, Indonesia', 'This study presents a systematic literature review of Scopus-indexed publications (2015–2025) on forensic engineering investigations of building construction failures, conducted under the PRISMA 2020 protocol. From 139 identified articles, 38 were analyzed through bibliometric mapping (VOSviewer) and narrative meta-synthesis. The mapping of methods and failure types indicates that specific failures tend to be examined with particular analytical tools—for example, structural collapses are modeled numerically, while procedural or human-factor failures rely on Root Cause Analysis (RCA), Failure Tree Analysis (FTA), or expert judgment. Findings reveal that research remains predominantly technical, emphasizing structural and material failures analyzed via Finite Element Analysis, load testing, and visual inspection, while managerial, procedural, and legal aspects are underexplored. Three interrelated domains—technical, managerial, and regulative—emerged as key determinants of investigative effectiveness. The study proposes an integrated forensic engineering investigation framework encompassing six sequential phases: Pre-Scene Preparation and Coordination, Scene Examination/Investigation, Laboratory and Testing, Analytical Modelling, Causal Analysis, and Reporting and Knowledge Management. Within technical, investigative, and legal-regulatory domains, ensuring methodological rigor, procedural consistency, and legal defensibility, thereby advancing a standardized, multidisciplinary model for construction failure investigations.', 'Keywords: Building Construction Failures, Forensic Engineering, Failure Analysis,  Integrated Investigation Framework', 'Tatang Kukuh Wibawa', 346, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS351-Tatang Kukuh Wibawa.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS351-Tatang Kukuh Wibawa.pdf', '2025-11-17 12:01:17', '2025-11-19 12:01:53'),
(352, 'climate_change', 'oral presentation', 'CLIMATE CHANGE AND THE FUTURE OF COASTAL FISHERIES: ECOLOGICAL SHIFTS, GEOPOLITICAL IMPLICATIONS, AND PATHWAYS TO SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT', 'Muhammad Irsyad Abiyusfi Ghafari1,2*, Namira Risqi Putri Muquita3*, Djumanto4, Eko Agus Suyono1, and Andhika Puspito Nugroho1', '1 Faculty of Biology, Gadjah Mada University, Indonesia\n2 Life as Naturalist, Indonesia\n3 Department of International Relations, Faculty of Social Science and Political Science, Gadjah Mada University, Indonesia\n4 Department of Fisheries, Faculty of Agriculture, Gadjah Mada University, Indonesia', 'Climate change is reshaping the world’s coastal and marine ecosystems, posing complex challenges for ecological stability, food security, and maritime governance. This review synthesizes current knowledge on the ecological, social, and geopolitical dimensions of climate change impacts on coastal fisheries. Ecologically, ocean warming, sea-level rise, and habitat degradation—particularly in mangrove and coral reef systems—are driving shifts in species distribution and altering ecosystem productivity. These biophysical changes cascade into socio-economic and geopolitical tensions as fish stocks migrate across national boundaries, challenging existing governance regimes and creating new arenas for negotiation and conflict. Drawing upon interdisciplinary literature from marine ecology, political geography, and sustainable development studies, this paper critically examines how ecological transformations intersect with economic dependency and regional power dynamics in coastal states. It further identifies gaps in current adaptation frameworks, highlighting the need for integrated, science-based, and inclusive policy responses that link local resilience with global cooperation. The review concludes by proposing pathways toward sustainable coastal fisheries grounded in ecosystem-based management, maritime diplomacy, and equitable blue economy strategies. By bridging ecological evidence and governance innovation, this study contributes a holistic perspective on how coastal fisheries can adapt to a rapidly changing climate while maintaining ecological integrity and social justice.', 'climate change; ecological shifts; blue economy; maritime governance; sustainable development', 'Namira Risqi Putri Muquita', 349, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS352-Namira Risqi Putri Muquita.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS352-Namira Risqi Putri Muquita.pdf', '2025-11-19 13:26:12', '2025-11-22 03:35:19'),
(353, 'ethnobiology', 'oral presentation', 'HISTOPATHOLOGY OF GILLS AND MUSCLES IN SEVERAL FISH SPECIES CONTAMINATED BY GOLD MINING WASTE IN LEBONG REGENCY', 'Dian Fita Lestari, Vestidhia Yunisya Atmaja, Santi Nurul Kamilah', 'Department of Biology, Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, University of Bengkulu, Bengkulu, Indonesia', 'Lebong Regency is one of the gold mining area in Bengkulu province. Heavy metals are used in gold mining processes to separate gold from rock and sand. The waste is subsequently discharged into the river, affecting the water bodies near the mine in Lebong Regency. Heavy metals can cause organ damage in a variety of ways in fish. Increasing heavy metal contamination can result in morphological, physiological, and biochemical alterations in fish. Heavy metal pollution can cause structural and functional damage to many fish organs. Alterations in histopathological structures can  inform adaptive responses to changing environments or in cases of severely altered conditions. Histopathological examination is one of the examination techniques by studying abnormal changes in cells or tissues which is used to determine the diagnosis of the effects of environmental contaminants on fish. The aim of this research was to examine histopathological of gills and muscles in several types in fish impacted heavy metal from gold mining waste. The result showed that the effects of heavy metals on fish can be seen from the photomicrograph of the histopathological structure of the gills and muscles that showing histological damage to the gills including fusion secondary lamellae, epithelial lifting, hyperplasia and hypertrophy. Additionally, the gills of T.tricopterus are infected with parasites. Meanwhile, photomicrograph of the muscles histology show the muscle atrophy, edema, necrosis, and fatty degeneration are common symptoms of damage fish muscles. Heavy metal waste is one of the pollutant that can causes alteration in fish organs.', 'alteration, fish, gold mining, heavy metals', 'Dian Fita Lestari', 350, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS353-Dian Fita Lestari.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS353-Dian Fita Lestari.pdf', '2025-11-20 00:33:22', '2025-11-20 17:28:16'),
(354, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Phenotypic Spectrum and Disorder Candidate Analysis in Ectrodactyly Cases in South Sulawesi, Indonesia', 'Firdaus', 'Faculty of Biology UGM, Yogyakarta, Indonesia', 'Background: Ectrodactyly is a rare congenital disorder characterized by reduced digits and a deep median cleft in the hands and/or feet. This condition has wide genetic heterogeneity and phenotypic expressivity, and can be either isolated or syndromic. There is no literature that reveals the phenotypic spectrum of ectrodactyly from South Sulawesi. Objective: To characterize the phenotypic spectrum and identify candidate disorders in ectrodactyly cases in South Sulawesi, Indonesia. Methods: Four individuals with ectrodactyly from non-consanguineous families were characterized phenotypically and radiologically. Phenotypic examination included head & neck, chest, skeletal, skin, nails & hair, and supported by plain radiography to assess the bone structure of the hands and feet. Each phenotypic character was annotated using The Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO) and then analyzed with Phenotype Similarity Tools using the Ancestor Information Content (AIC) metric to identify candidate disorders. Results: All individuals showed bilateral involvement with a broad phenotypic spectrum, including tridactyly to hexadactyly with median cleft, syndactyly, camptodactyly, hallux valgus, brachydactyly, hallux duplication, nail dysplasia, phalangeal aplasia, triphalangeal thumb, and metatarsal and metacarpal synostosis. No anomalies were found outside the hands and feet. Candidate disorder analysis identified Split Hand-Foot Malformation 4 (SHFM4; MIM 605289) as the strongest candidate. Conclusion: This study demonstrates a highly variable phenotypic spectrum of ectrodactyly in South Sulawesi, with SHFM4 as a candidate disorder. Molecular confirmation through TP63 gene sequencing is recommended to uncover the underlying genetic mechanism.', 'Ectrodactyly, South Sulawesi, Phenotypic Spectrum, Phenotype Similarity Tools, SHFM4', 'Firdaus', 219, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS354-Firdaus.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS354-Firdaus.pdf', '2025-11-20 03:54:56', '2025-11-24 17:21:35'),
(355, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'EFFECT OF TEA–COFFEE WASTE RATIOS ON THE CALORIFIC VALUE OF TORREFIED BIOBRIQUETTES', 'Aldillah Herlambang1, Dwinka Aldian Nugraha2, Boy Setiawan3, Melani Putri4, Ayu Nadila Safitri Putri5, and Ilham Dwi Arirohman6', '1 Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia\n2 Institut Teknologi Sumatera, Lampung, Indonesia\n', 'Biomass residues such as spent tea leaves and coffee grounds offer significant potential as renewable solid fuels, yet their energy performance varies widely depending on composition and pre-treatment. This study investigates how different mixing ratios of tea and coffee waste influence the calorific value of biobriquettes produced through torrefaction. Five formulations (1:0, 1:1, 2:1, 1:2, and 0:1) were prepared, dried, milled, and subjected to torrefaction at 250 °C. Proximate analysis was performed to determine moisture, volatile matter, ash content, and fixed carbon, while calorific values were quantified using a bomb calorimeter. Torrefaction was found to substantially enhance fuel quality by lowering moisture and increasing fixed carbon. Coffee grounds exhibited superior fuel characteristics, yielding the highest calorific value of 5704.27 cal/g, whereas the 1:2 tea–coffee blend produced a comparable value of 5687.27 cal/g. These results indicate that blended compositions can achieve high energy performance while improving resource flexibility. Overall, the study demonstrates that torrefied mixtures of tea and coffee waste represent a promising pathway for developing sustainable solid fuels within waste-to-energy applications.', 'Biomass, Torrefaction, Calorific Value, Biobriquette, Waste-to-Energy', 'Dwinka Aldian Nugraha', 347, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS355-Dwinka Aldian Nugraha.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS355-Dwinka Aldian Nugraha.pdf', '2025-11-20 13:19:57', '2025-11-26 08:00:39'),
(356, 'bio_medic_technology', 'oral presentation', 'PHYTOCHEMICAL SCREENING AND ANTIOXIDANT ACTIVITIES OF ETHANOLIC EXTRACTS OF Metaxya parkeri (Hook. & Grev.) J.Sm. ', 'Uni Baroroh Husnudin (1), Dina Erliana (2), Munifilia Ekasari (2), Andini Vermita Bestari (1), and Anggari Linda Destiana (1).', '(1) Biology, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia\n(2) Chemistry, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia \n', 'Metaxya parkeri (Hook. & Grev.) J.Sm. is a fern species whose pharmacological potential has not been extensively explored. This study aimed to screen for phytochemical compounds and evaluate the antioxidant activity of ethanolic extracts from the stipes and laminas (leaf blades) of M. parkeri. Extraction was performed using the maceration method with 96% ethanol. Qualitative phytochemical screening was conducted to detect alkaloids, flavonoids, saponins, tannins, and terpenoids. Antioxidant activity was determined using the DPPH (2,2-diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl) method, expressed as the Inhibitory Concentration 50 (IC50). Phytochemical screening revealed that both stipe and lamina extracts tested positive for alkaloids, tannins, saponins, and flavonoids. The antioxidant assay demonstrated that the ethanolic extracts exhibited weak activity, with IC50 values of 160.49 ppm for the stipes and 414.14 ppm for the laminas, compared to ascorbic acid as the positive control (3.39 ppm). These results indicate that M. parkeri contains bioactive compounds. However, their concentration in the crude ethanolic extract may be insufficient to produce strong antioxidant activity, suggesting the need for further extraction or purification methods.', 'Metaxya parkeri; antioxidant; DPPH; phytochemicals; ethanolic extract.', 'Andini Vermita Bestari', 342, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS356-Andini Vermita Bestari.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS356-Andini Vermita Bestari.pdf', '2025-11-21 10:44:55', '2025-11-22 09:18:56'),
(357, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'SYNTHESIS AND APPLICATION OF AGRICULTURAL WASTE SILICA–CELLULOSE ADSORBENT FOR NON-AGITATED FFA ADSORPTION FROM WASTE COOKING OIL', 'Putri Ananda,  Rosmawati Sipayung,  Oki Alfernando, Ira Galih Prabasari, Nazarudin, Rahma Amalia, Rara Ayu Lestary, Nita Widyastuti ', 'Universitas Jambi ', 'Waste cooking oil is a promising and low-cost raw material for biodiesel production; however, its high Free Fatty Acid (FFA) content inhibits the transesterification process and lowers biodiesel yield. This study aims to develop a silica–cellulose composite bioadsorbent synthesized from rice husk ash and corn cob, and to evaluate its effectiveness in reducing FFA under non-agitated conditions. Silica was extracted through calcination and alkaline leaching, while cellulose was isolated via NaOH delignification. Both materials were crosslinked using glutaraldehyde and characterized using FTIR, and XRD to determine their functional groups, structural phases, and surface morphology. Characterization confirmed the integration of silica and cellulose through Si–O–Si, O–H, and C–O–C functional groups, an amorphous semi crystalline hybrid structure, and porous morphology favorable for adsorption. Adsorption experiments with varying dosage, temperature, contact time, and silica–cellulose ratios demonstrated that the highest efficiency occurred at 3–5% adsorbent dosage, 60–90 minutes contact time, and 45°C. Under optimal conditions, the bioadsorbent successfully reduced FFA levels to below 2%, meeting biodiesel pretreatment standards. The novelty of this research lies in the development of a low-cost, agricultural-waste-based composite functioning effectively without agitation, offering energy savings and operational simplicity. The findings support sustainable resource utilization and provide a practical pretreatment approach for improving biodiesel production efficiency.', 'Silica adsorbent, cellulose adsorbent, waste cooking oil, FFA reduction, adsorption process', 'PUTRI ANANDA', 354, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS357-PUTRI ANANDA.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS357-PUTRI ANANDA.pdf', '2025-11-21 13:00:57', '2025-11-25 10:42:55'),
(358, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'ANALYSIS OF AIR QUALITY IN JAMBI CITY BASED ON AIR POLLUTION STANDARD INDEX (ISPU)', 'Tyas Hanifatunnisa, Arifin Abdullah, Bambang Irawan, and Rizki Andre Handika*', 'Jambi University', 'This study aims to analyze air quality in Jambi City utilizing the Air Pollution Standard Index (ISPU), particularly for PM10 and PM2.5, during the 2023–2024 period. The trends were analyzed and their implications for public health and environmental management. A descriptive quantitative approach was employed using secondary data from the Air Quality Monitoring System (AQMS), which were subsequently converted into ISPU values in accordance with Ministry of Environment and Forestry Regulation No. 14/2020. The findings indicate that air quality in 2024 improved compared to 2023, particularly for PM₁₀, which consistently fell within the “good” category. However, PM2.5 still recorded numerous days in the “moderate” category, although it no longer reached the “unhealthy” levels observed during the peak pollution episode in 2023, which was influenced by forest and land fires. The Wilcoxon test confirmed a significant difference in ISPU values between the two years (p < 0.001), suggesting a measurable shift in air quality. The novelty of this study lies in its comparative analysis of two recent years, integrating ISPU conversion with inferential statistical assessment to provide a more comprehensive understanding of particulate pollution patterns in a tropical city highly vulnerable to forest and land fire (karhutla) events. These findings contribute to strengthening the scientific evidence base for pollution-control policymaking and support the development of data-driven early warning systems and mitigation strategies at the regional level.', 'air quality; PM10; PM2,5; ISPU; Jambi City.', 'Tyas Hanifatunnisa', 345, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS358-Tyas Hanifatunnisa.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS358-Tyas Hanifatunnisa.pdf', '2025-11-21 15:39:14', '2025-11-27 15:35:36'),
(359, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'IDENTIFICATION OF AMPHIBIANS OF THE ORDER ANURA IN MUHAMMAD SABKI URBAN FOREST PARK, JAMBI CITY', 'Anggit Prima Nugraha (1), Tia Wulandari (1), Dawam Suprayogi (1), Andini Vermita Bestari (1),  Rizky Desryan Syah (2), Verataty Gultom (2), Louisa Bella Br Tampubolon (2), Despita (2)', '(1) Biology Study Program, Department of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia\n(2) Student of the Biology Study Program, Department of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia\n', 'Green Open Spaces (GOS) play an important role in maintaining urban ecosystems and provide\necological, social, and economic benefits for local communities. The Muhammad Sabki Urban Forest Park (Taman Hutan Kota Muhammad Sabki) in Jambi City is one of the GOS areas that still contains a natural forest ecosystem. The order Anura is one of the components of this ecosystem and also functions as an environmental bioindicator. This study aimed to characterize the morphology and assess the species diversity of the order Anura in Muhammad Sabki Urban Forest Park. The survey method used was Visual Encounter Survey (VES), applied to all habitat types in each zone. Data collection was conducted at 07.00-10.00 and 19.00-22.00 Western Indonesian Time. Observations were carried out in high-use, moderate-use, and low-use zones. Photograph of anuran individuals were taken, and species were identified based on morphological character using field guides. The results consist of morphological\ncharacter description and conservation status. Based on the observations, 40 individuals representing 10 species from five families were recorded. The five families encountered in this survey were Bufonidae, Dicroglossidae, Ranidae, Microhylidae, and Rhacophoridae. The ten species recorded were Hylarana erythraea, Hylarana baramica, Hylarana glandulosa, Limnonectes microdiscus, Limnonectes blythii, Duttaphrynus melanostictus, Polypedates leucomystax, Kurixalus chaseni, Microhyla mantheyi, and Microhyla mukhlesuri.', 'Anura; Bioindicator; Muhammad Sabki Urban Forest Park.', 'Andini Vermita Bestari', 342, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS359-Andini Vermita Bestari.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS359-Andini Vermita Bestari.pdf', '2025-11-22 01:39:05', '2025-11-26 16:40:34'),
(360, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'STIRRING VARIATION EFFECT ON SILICA-CELLULOSE ADSORPTION EFFICIENCY FOR FFA REDUCTION', 'Intan Nandia Sakti1,  Rosmawati Sipayung1, Oki Alfernando1, Ira Galih Prabasari1, Rahma Amalia1, Rara Ayu Lestary1, Nita Widyastuti1, Nazarudin1', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Used cooking oil contains high free fatty acid (FFA) levels that inhibit transesterification and promote saponification, making biodiesel purification difficult; therefore, an effective pretreatment is required to reduce FFA content to meet biodiesel feedstock standards. This study evaluates the adsorption performance of agricultural-waste-based bioadsorbents, namely rice husk ash as a silica source and corn cobs as a cellulose source, under batch conditions with 300 rpm stirring. Operational variations included adsorbent concentrations of 1–5%, temperatures of 30–60 °C, and contact times of 30–90 minutes. The adsorbents were produced through calcination of rice husk and alkaline treatment of corn cobs, followed by FTIR and XRD characterization to identify functional groups and structural phases. The results show that silica-rich rice husk ash exhibits superior performance, achieving an 81.44% FFA reduction at the optimum condition of 5% adsorbent concentration, 60 °C, and 30 minutes, resulting in a final FFA value of 0.20%. In contrast, cellulose-based corn cobs achieved only a 39.02% reduction at 5% concentration, 30 °C, and 90 minutes, with a final FFA level of 0.66%. FTIR confirmed active silanol and hydroxyl groups, while XRD revealed an amorphous silica structure with higher reactivity compared to the semi-crystalline cellulose of corn cobs. These chemical and structural differences explain why stirring more effectively enhances silica adsorption than cellulose. The novelty of this study lies in integrating stirring variation with comparative adsorbent behavior, contributing to applied physics through adsorption dynamics, surface interaction mechanisms, and material utilization. This research provides an environmentally friendly approach to waste valorization and supports physics education by demonstrating the relevance of material properties and mass-transfer phenomena in real-world energy applications', 'adsorbent; biodiesel; corncob; free fatty acid; rice husk', 'INTAN NANDIA SAKTI', 356, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS360-INTAN NANDIA SAKTI.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS360-INTAN NANDIA SAKTI.pdf', '2025-11-22 05:26:18', '2025-11-25 10:42:07'),
(361, 'renewable_energy', 'oral presentation', 'SYNTHESIS OF SO4-Fe2O3-MgO CATALYST USING MICROWAVE IRRADIATION COMPARED TO CONVENTIONAL HEATING', 'Tamami Nukma Mujnadila, Lea Aprilyani, Rahma Amalia, Oki Alfernando, Ira Galih Prabasari, Rosmawati Sipayung, Sri Mundarti, Nita Widyastuti, Rizka Shafira', 'Jambi University', 'The demand for renewable energy has encouraged the development of heterogeneous catalysts to improve methyl ester production from waste cooking oil. Sulfated metal oxide catalysts exhibit strong acidity and good thermal stability, but their physical properties and catalytic activity are strongly affected by the activation method. SO4-Fe2O3-MgO catalysts were synthesized via co-precipitation with activation compared between microwave irradiation and conventional heating. Fe(NO3)3·9H2O and Mg(NO3)2·6H2O precursors were precipitated using a combination of NH4OH and NaOH as precipitating agents to reach pH 9–11, followed by 15 hours of aging, filtration, drying, and calcination at 600 °C. Sulfation was carried out by incipient wetness impregnation using 1 M (NH4)2SO4, then dried and calcined again at 500 °C. Catalyst characterization was limited to XRD and FTIR to determine crystal structure and functional groups. Catalytic activity was tested in the transesterification of waste cooking oil under optimized conditions of 65 °C for 150 minutes with a 1:12 methanol-to-oil ratio, using three systems: 1% homogeneous NaOH catalyst, 5% SO4-Fe2O3-MgO catalyst prepared by conventional heating, and 5% SO4-Fe2O3-MgO catalyst activated by microwave irradiation. Results indicated that microwave-activated catalysts exhibited higher crystallinity, more homogeneous sulfate distribution, and greater methyl ester yield compared to conventional heating and homogeneous NaOH base catalysts. These findings confirm that microwave irradiation is a more efficient method for enhancing the structural and acidic properties of SO4-Fe2O3-MgO catalysts and for improving catalytic performance in methyl ester production from waste cooking oil.', 'SO₄-Fe₂O₃-MgO, sulfation, microwave irradiation, methyl ester, waste cooking oil', 'Tamami Nukma Mujnadila & Lea Aprilyani', 355, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS361-Tamami Nukma Mujnadila.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS361-Tamami Nukma Mujnadila.pdf', '2025-11-22 05:49:38', '2025-11-22 07:55:50'),
(362, 'technopreneurship', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of WhatsApp Business Features on the Effectiveness of Marketing Communications for Craft MSMEs in Jambi City', 'Rudi Nata, Ari Andrianti, Miranty Yudhistira, Rahmad Ashar, and Renaldi Yulvianda', 'University of Jambi', 'This study analyzes the influence of WhatsApp Business features (Broadcast Message, Quick Reply, Catalog, and Labeling) on marketing communication effectiveness for Craft SMEs in Jambi City. Using a mixed-methods approach, quantitative data were collected through structured questionnaires from 385 creative SME respondents, while qualitative data were gathered through in-depth interviews, case studies, and focus group discussions. Multiple linear regression analysis revealed that WhatsApp Business features simultaneously significantly influence marketing communication effectiveness (R²=0.596, p=0.003), explaining 59.6% of the variance. However, partial analysis showed only the Catalog feature had significant positive influence (β=0.903, p=0.034), while Broadcast Message and Labeling showed positive but insignificant effects, and Quick Reply demonstrated negative influence. The novelty lies in focusing specifically on creative SMEs in Jambi with cultural heritage products and comprehensively analyzing four WhatsApp Business features simultaneously. This research contributes to digital marketing literature by demonstrating how digital tools can preserve local cultural values while enhancing business competitiveness. The findings imply that creative SMEs should prioritize optimizing Catalog features with high-quality visual content that showcases cultural values, supported by government digital literacy programs tailored to heritage-based businesses.', 'Features, WhatsApp, Effectiveness, Communication, MSMEs', 'Rudi Nata', 333, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS362-Rudi Nata.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS362-Rudi Nata.pdf', '2025-11-22 06:10:41', '2025-11-26 11:54:38'),
(363, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'ANALYSIS OF SHALLOW GROUNDWATER QUALITY AS AN INDICATOR OF SEAWATER INTRUSION IN THE NORTHERN COAST OF JAVA', 'Merisa R. Mawaddah, Risnaliyah Nuriil Tadersi, Febiyora Chandra Kirana, Andicho Haryus Wirasapta', 'Universitas Jambi', 'This study investigates the quality of shallow groundwater as an indicator of seawater intrusion in Yosorejo Village, Batang Regency, located on the northern coast of Java. This study evaluates physicochemical parameters for Total Dissolved Solids (TDS), Electrical Conductivity (EC), and pH, incorporating geological factors to investigate preliminary indicators of saline intrusion. Groundwater sampling and analysis took place in September-October 2023 using seven domestic wells distributed at various distances from the coastline (0-4 km) and topographic elevations (0-10 meters above sea level). GIS mapping of TDS and Electrical Conductivity revealed peaks for TDS with a maximum reading of 1400 ppm and Electrical Conductivity with a maximum reading of 1970 µS/cm, indicating that saline intrusion is well under way. It also confirmed increasing tendencies towards decreasing elevations nearshore. Groundwater pH varies between 7 to 8.4, within tolerable standards but slightly basic inland along hydrolysis and lithological reactions. Geologically, this site is primarily covered by very porous layers of alluvial deposit silt sand, representing aquifers amenable to saline waters. This work presents its originality by doing integrated spatial hydrogeological analyses to identify saline intrusion affected areas within 0-500 meters onshore. This work contributes to the development of environmental physics and hydrogeological research by confirming empirically for the imbalanced interface between seawater and groundwaters on tropical low-lying areas, pressing for alleviating strategies to secure groundwaters\' sustainability.', 'Groundwater Quality; Coastal Sediment; Seawater Intrussion', 'Andicho Haryus Wirasapta', 359, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS363-Andicho Haryus Wirasapta.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS363-Andicho Haryus Wirasapta.pdf', '2025-11-22 07:49:36', '2025-11-22 09:17:12'),
(364, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'TECHNOLOGICAL EVALUATION OF THE IMPLEMENTATION OF ADVANCED PROCESS CONTROL AT THE RFCC UNIT OF  PT KILANG PERTAMINA INTERNASIONAL', 'Suhar Prianto1*, Andri Wiyo2, and Teguh Ariyanto3', '1Engineer professional Program Study Program, Faculty of Engineering, Gadjah Mada University, Yogyakarta\n2Engineering & Development Department, PT. KPI RU IV, Cilacap\n3Department of Chemical Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Gadjah Mada University, Yogyakarta \n', 'The RFCC (Resid Fluid Catalytic Cracking) unit has a design capacity of 62 MBSD, with raw oil feed coming from Low Sulphur Wax Residue (LSWR) ex CDU II and Vacuum Gas Oil ex-HVU I/II. Raw oil feed will go through the cracking process using a fluidized catalyst into a lighter and more valuable product, including: Propylene products are produced in unit 104, Propylene Recovery as an overhead product C3 Splitter, after going through the advanced treatment process of moisture removal in the propylene drier and COS and metal removal in the propylene treater. RFCC naphtha derived from unit 101/102 removed sulphur contaminants and ditreated external diolefin fraction in unit 105 Gasoline Hydrotreating (Prime-G) produces desulphurized RFCC gasoline. Ex-RFCC gasoline products are high octane blending (HOMC) components, flowed to the RFCC gasoline tank. LPG (mixed C3/C4) products are produced in unit 104, Propylene Recovery after going through the process of treating and separating in unit 102, Gas Concentration and unit 103, LPG Merox. In unit 104, LPG is produced as the bottom product of C3/C4 Splitter and C3 Splitter. Light Cycle Oil (LCO) received from the LCO Product Coalescer at RFCC Unit 101 will be delivered to the existing MFO header or LCO Tank and can also be delivered to the Fuel Oil Tank as a blending component of the refinery fuel oil (RFO). Decant Oil (DCO) is the bottom distillate of RFCC products. Before going to the product tank, the fine catalyst is first separated using an automatic backwash filter. Some DCO can also be sent to the Fuel Oil Tank as a blending component of the refinery fuel oil (RFO). Heavy Cycle Oil (HCO) is an intermediate product that is used to circulate back to the reactor to be used for heat, besides that HCO can also be reused as reactor feed, under certain conditions. The problems that occur in RFCC operations are as follows: Fluctuations in reactor outlet temperature which has an impact on fluctuations in RFCC conversion, Fluctuations in temperature profiles in the main column which have an impact on suboptimal valuable products produced., Tuning operating conditions that take a long time in Unit 104 to obtain high purity propane in conditions while maintaining on-spec propylene quality. There is still room for improvement in the RFCC unit to get more optimal operating conditions to improve the stability of operating conditions at RFCC PT KPI RU IV Cilacap so that it is able to produce products to the maximum, one of the optimization efforts that can be done is to implement Advanced Process Control (APC) in the RFCC unit, whose use has been widely applied to increase profits through the optimization of valuable products,  efficiency, and energy saving.\n\n', ' APC, Advanced Process Control, optimization ', 'Suhar Prianto', 353, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS364-Suhar Prianto.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS364-Suhar Prianto.pdf', '2025-11-22 07:55:39', '2025-11-24 05:21:26'),
(365, 'food_science_technology', 'oral presentation', 'Enhancing the Capacity of Mushroom Farmers through Training on the Production of Oyster Mushroom Soy Sauce and Powder', 'Anggari Linda Destiana, Hasna Ul Maritsa, Putri Dwi Mulyani, Uni Baroroh Husnudin, Fitra Wahyuni, Anggit Prima Nugraha, Mahya Ihsan, and Ahmad Sazali', 'Biology Department, Science and Technology Faculty, Universitas Jambi', 'Oyster mushroom is an edible fungus widely cultivated in Jambi. Based on digital data searches, there are 9 MSMEs (Micro, Small, and Medium Enterprises) engaged in oyster mushroom cultivation in the region. One such MSME collaborating with the community service team from the Biology Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Jambi University, is Ryan Jamur. Ryan Jamur MSME harvests approximately 30-50 kg of fresh oyster mushrooms per harvest cycle, which are then marketed fresh to several traditional markets in Jambi. Fresh oyster mushrooms (Pleurotus ostreatus) have a relatively short shelf life due to their high moisture content (80-90%) and rapid enzymatic activity. Therefore, this community service initiative aims to enhance the capacity of mushroom farmers through training on the production of soy sauce and powder from oyster mushrooms, which have a longer shelf life compared to fresh ones. This service activity consists of three main stages: (1) preparation (initial survey, activity planning, and coordination with partners), (2) implementation of training on the production of mushroom soy sauce and powder, and (3) activity evaluation. Based on the evaluation results, 72.73% of participants assessed that producing oyster mushroom powder and soy sauce is easy to do, indicating that the participants feel capable of producing them independently.', 'oyster mushroom, mushroom soy sauce, mushroom farmers, mushroom powder', 'Anggari Linda Destiana', 338, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS365-Anggari Linda Destiana.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS365-Anggari Linda Destiana.pdf', '2025-11-22 13:29:52', '2025-11-26 08:58:31');
INSERT INTO `upload_abstracts` (`id`, `topic`, `type`, `title`, `authors`, `institutions`, `abstract`, `keywords`, `presenter`, `participant_id`, `status`, `reviewed_by`, `loa`, `invoice`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES
(366, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'Diversity and Bioprospecting Potential of Lignicolous Fungi in the Jambi University Educational Forest', 'Anggari Linda Destiana, Jessica Maranatha Tambun, Elvira Aulia Daryanti, Putri Dwi Mulyani, Hasna Ul Maritsa, Anggit Prima Nugraha, and Tia Wulandari', 'University of Jambi', 'Wild lignicolous fungi are a group of fungi that play a crucial role in wood decomposition and nutrient cycling in forest ecosystems. Furthermore, these lignicolous fungi represent a hidden gem for herbal medicine. This research aims to investigate the diversity and bioprospecting potential of wild lignicolous fungi located at the periphery of the University of Jambi Educational Forest. The study employed a combined exploratory-analytical method and literature review, with stages including fungal sampling and collection, morphological characterization and identification, and a literature-based study on their bioprospecting potential. The results revealed the collection of 17 wood fungus samples with distinct morphological characteristics. Ten of these samples were successfully identified, while seven remain unidentified. All identified samples belong to the Phylum Basidiomycota, Order Agaricomycetes, and are divided into 3 Classes (Agaricales, Polyporales, Auriculariales) and 6 Families (Mycenaceae, Polyporaceae, Fomitopsidaceae, Auriculariaceae, Schizophyllaceae, and Agaricaceae). The fungi from these six families have diverse applications, including use as food sources, herbal medicine ingredients, and decomposers. In conclusion, the wood fungi obtained from the edge of the University of Jambi Educational Forest are diverse and possess significant bioprospecting value worthy of further development.', 'Basidiomycota, Bioprospecting, Fungi, Lignicolous, University of Jambi', 'Jessica Maranatha Tambun', 360, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS366-Jessica Maranatha Tambun.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS366-Jessica Maranatha Tambun.pdf', '2025-11-22 13:46:58', '2025-11-26 11:53:40'),
(367, 'socio_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'Revitalizing Community Livelihood through Empowerment and Management of Lubuk Larangan Guci Emas', 'Tedjo Sukmono\nSigit Indrawijaya\nLauura Hermala Yunita\nYoppie Wulanda\nRizky Janatul Magwa\nFebrina Rolin\nYusyam Leni\nTri Syukria Putra\nPradita Eko Prasetyo Utomo\n', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The Batanghari River has experienced a decline in fish diversity from around 300 to 100 species between 1990 and 2022, indicating ecological degradation. Lubuk Larangan Guci Emas in Muaro Pijoan was designated in 2022 as a local wisdom-based conservation and tourism area. However, management, facilities, and promotion remain major challenges. This community service program aims to enhance the capacity of the Tourism Awareness Group (POKDARWIS) in managing fish conservation and community-based ecotourism through training in management, marketing, and the application of innovative technologies such as the Ber-Ikan Jambi application, fiber boats, and life jackets. The results showed a significant increase in management and marketing scores from 10.2 to 17.1, as well as improved income and community independence. The program contributes to institutional strengthening, technological application for conservation, and sustainable nature-based tourism development.', 'Lubuk Larangan Guci Emas Community Empowerment Fish Conservation Nature-Based Tourism', 'Tedjo Sukmono', 247, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS367-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS367-Tedjo Sukmono.pdf', '2025-11-22 14:52:32', '2025-11-24 05:45:19'),
(368, 'biodiversity', 'oral presentation', 'ZOOPLANKTON COMMUNITY STRUCTURE AND ITS RELATIONSHIP TO PHYSICO-CHEMICAL PARAMETERS IN THE SWAMP ECOSYSTEM OF RAWA BENTO, KERINCI', 'Olifia Rifani, Tedjo Sukmono, Dawam Suprayogi', 'Biology Study Program, Faculty of Science and Technology, Universitas Jambi, Jambi, Indonesia\nDoctoral Student of Biology, Faculty of Biology, Gadjah Mada University, Yogyakarta, Indonesia\n', 'This study aims to analyze the diversity, abundance, and community structure of zooplankton and its relationship with the physico-chemical parameters of the waters in Rawa Bento, Kerinci Regency, Jambi Province. The Bento Swamp Ecosystem is a highland swamp that has an important ecological function as a buffer for water systems, carbon storage, and habitat for various aquatic biota. The research was carried out in February-May 2023 using a quantitative descriptive approach through field survey methods at three stations representing differences in anthropogenic activity levels. Zooplankton samples were taken with a plankton net measuring 64 μm and identified using a binocular microscope. The physico-chemical parameters measured include temperature, pH, brightness, flow velocity, dissolved oxygen (DO), biochemical oxygen requirement (BOD₅), and total suspended solids (TSS). The identification results found 9 species of zooplankton classified into four classes and three phyla (Rotifera, Protozoa, and Ciliophora), with the dominance of Rotifera (Eurotatoria and Monogononta) contributing about 80% to the total community. The diversity index (H\') value ranged from 2.058–2.093 (medium category), the equality index (E) was high (0.936–0.953), and the dominance (C) was low (0.132–0.140), indicating a stable community. Correlation analysis showed that pH and TSS were positively correlated very strongly with zooplankton abundance, while temperature and BOD₅ were negatively correlated very strongly. These results indicate that the water conditions of Rawa Bento still support zooplankton life, despite light anthropogenic pressures. These findings are expected to be basic data for ecological monitoring and sustainable management of highland swamp conservation.', 'Water Quality; Rawa Bento; Community Structure; Zooplankton', 'Dawam Suprayogi', 310, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS368-Dawam Suprayogi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS368-Dawam Suprayogi.pdf', '2025-11-22 15:31:03', '2025-11-24 17:20:40'),
(369, 'stem_education', 'oral presentation', 'Analysis of PM₂.₅ Pollutant Backward Trajectories in Jambi City Using the HYSPLIT Model', '1. Nesly Oktaviani Saragi Napitu\n2. Febri Juita Anggraini, S.T., M.T.\n3. Zuli Rodhiyah, S.Si,. M.T.', 'Jambi University', 'Air pollution in Jambi City shows fluctuations in air quality due to various sources of pollution, both from local activities and long-distance pollutant transportation. In 2024, the average concentration of PM₂.₅ was recorded to have exceeded the annual quality standard of 19 µg/m³, an increase compared to the previous two years, which reached 16.11 µg/m³ and 8.37 µg/m³, respectively. This increase occurred after the COVID-19 pandemic due to changes in people\'s activities returning to normal. Using the HYSPLIT Backward Trajectory Model, this study identified the potential areas of origin of PM₂.₅ pollutant backward trajectories in Jambi City before, during, and after the pandemic, covering the years 2018 to 2024.  The results of the study show that before and after the pandemic, the dominant pollutants originated from Ogan Komering Ilir Regency and Banyuasin Regency, especially in months with low rainfall, such as July. Meanwhile, during the pandemic, the potential areas of origin of the backward trajectory of pollutants were more varied, covering Malaysian waters, Muara Enim Regency, Tanjung Jabung Barat Regency, Bintan Regency, and several other areas.', 'PM₂.₅, air quality, HYSPLIT, COVID-19, Jambi City', 'Nesly Oktaviani Saragi Napitu', 362, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS369-Nesly Oktaviani Saragi Napitu.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS369-Nesly Oktaviani Saragi Napitu.pdf', '2025-11-24 11:05:39', '2025-11-28 01:51:41'),
(370, 'bio_technology', 'oral presentation', 'In Vitro Compatibility Evaluation of Trichoderma harzianum, Bacillus subtilis, and Pseudomonas fluorescens on King’s B and PDA–NA Media', 'Putri Dwi Mulyani, Triyuni Verza Abdilla, Hasna Ul Maritsa', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Trichoderma harzianum, Bacillus subtilis, and Pseudomonas fluorescens are well-known biological control agents and can serve as environmentally friendly alternatives for managing plant diseases. However, their combined application requires compatibility assessment to prevent antagonistic interactions within a microbial consortium. This study evaluated the compatibility of T. harzianum with B. subtilis and P. fluorescens on King’s B and PDA–NA media using a dual-culture assay. Compatibility was assessed based on the presence of inhibition zones and the colony diameter of T. harzianum at 24 and 48 hours, from which the percentage inhibition (PI) was calculated. On King’s B medium, clear inhibition zones were observed in both combinations, with PI values of 57.04% (T. harzianum × B. subtilis) and 62.89% (T. harzianum × P. fluorescens) at 48 hours, indicating strong antagonism. In contrast, no inhibition zones were detected on PDA–NA medium, and PI values were lower (10.96% and 18.40%, respectively), suggesting more compatible interactions. These results indicate that the three microorganisms can be combined in a consortium under conditions that do not stimulate excessive production of antagonistic metabolites, as represented by PDA–NA medium or similar field environments. This study provides important insights for the development of multi-agent biocontrol formulations.', 'Trichoderma harzianum; Bacillus subtilis; Pseudomonas fluorescens; compatibility; biocontrol.', 'Putri Dwi Mulyani', 363, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS370-Putri Dwi Mulyani.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS370-Putri Dwi Mulyani.pdf', '2025-11-24 15:45:55', '2025-11-26 15:50:33'),
(371, 'earth_science', 'oral presentation', 'Identification Of Subsurface Structures Using The Wenner-Schlumberger Configuration Geoelectric Method At The Pematang Jering Temple Site In Muaro Jambi', 'Husnul Hamdi, Indah Khairun Nisa, Maison', 'Universitas Jambi', 'A study entitled \"Identification Of Subsurface Structures Using The Wenner-Schlumberger Configuration Geoelectric Method At The Pematang Jering Temple Site In Muaro Jambi\" has been conducted. Indonesia possesses a rich cultural heritage characterized by the diversity of its ethnic groups, cultures, and historical relics scattered across various regions, including Jambi Province. One of the most significant historical sites in this area is the Muaro Jambi Temple Complex, an archaeological site estimated to contain at least 83 temple structures, although only 9 have been successfully restored to date. The existence of this site highlights its considerable archaeological potential, emphasizing the need for further investigation to uncover the buried structures and artifacts. This study aims to model subsurface structures using two-dimensional resistivity data and to identify the locations of temple structures composed of buried brick formations in the Pematang Jering Temple Site, within the Muaro Jambi area. The method employed is electrical resistivity using the Wenner-Schlumberger configuration. Measurements were conducted along five survey lines, and 2D inversion of the data using RES2DINV software revealed resistivity values ranging from 0.00885 to 82.3 Ωm. Indications of brick structures were found along all survey lines, with resistivity values ranging from 17 to 82.3 Ωm. These findings suggest the presence of potential hidden archaeological structures at the research site.\n', 'Resistivity, Geoelectricity, Wenner-Schlumberger, Subsurface', 'Husnul Hamdi', 249, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS371-Husnul Hamdi.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS371-Husnul Hamdi.pdf', '2025-11-25 15:49:42', '2025-11-27 03:31:37'),
(372, 'climate_change', 'oral presentation', 'ANALYSIS OF MICROPLASTIC CONTENT IN AMBIENT AIR IN THE CAMPUS AREA (Case Study: Mendalo Campus, Jambi University)', 'Zahra Nurulita Amanda1, Febri Juita Anggraini2, Fernando Mersa Putra3', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Microplastic pollution has become a global environmental issue, including microplastic contamination in ambient air, where air plays an important role as an indicator of environmental quality. This study aims to analyze the abundance and characteristics of microplastics contained in the ambient air of the Mendalo Campus, Jambi University. The sampling method uses a gravimetric technique with a High Volume Air Sampler (HVAS) at three locations representing various activities within the campus area, namely: Campus Forest, Campus Main Gate, and Campus Waste Disposal Site. The results of the abundance calculation for one of the samples showed a value of 228,709 particles/gram and 91.304 particles/gram, which indicates significant microplastic contamination in the campus environment, with the types of microplastics found being fibers, fragments, and films. These findings confirm that the campus area, although partially covered by good vegetation, is not completely free from the presence of microplastics in the air, therefore requiring serious attention in plastic waste management and air pollution mitigation.', 'Microplastics; Ambient Air; Abundance; Environmental Pollution.', 'Zahra Nurulita Amanda', 364, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS372-Zahra Nurulita Amanda.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS372-Zahra Nurulita Amanda.pdf', '2025-11-26 04:26:21', '2025-11-27 03:24:04'),
(373, 'sustainable_engineering', 'oral presentation', 'VULNERABILITY ASSESSMENT OF THE WATER DISTRIBUTION NETWORK SYSTEM IN THE SERVICE AREA OF SUNGAI DUREN WATER TREATMENT PLANT', 'Rama Febrian, Freddy Ilfan, Fernando Mersa Putra ', 'Universitas Jambi', 'Clean water distribution system is a vital infrastructure that often faces challenges such as water loss, low pressure, and vulnerability caused by technical and operational factors. PERUMDA Air Minum Tirta Muaro Jambi, particularly the service area of the Sungai Duren Water Treatment Plant (IPA), recorded a water loss rate of 27% in 2024, indicating that the system has not been performing optimally. This study aims to evaluate the vulnerability level of the distribution network and to assess the system’s ability to maintain its service function under disturbance scenarios. A quantitative approach was employed using hydraulic simulation with WaterCAD 2024 to analyze technical parameters including pressure, flow velocity, discharge, and elevation, along with vulnerability index calculation. The results show that the minimum pressure in the distribution network was only 0.7 kg/m², far below the standard minimum of 10 kg/m², while the average flow velocity was 0.039 m/s, which is below the minimum required 0.3 m/s, potentially leading to sedimentation and reduced efficiency. The vulnerability analysis identified five pipes with high vulnerability values (1.3982–0.5490) and four pipes in the moderate category (0.4693–0.4070). Overall, the Sungai Duren distribution system is not yet capable of ensuring continuous and efficient water supply, thus requiring infrastructure rehabilitation, improved pressure management, and system strengthening strategies to enhance reliability and service coverage.', 'Clean Water, Distribution Network, Sungai Duren IPA, Vulnerability, WaterCAD', 'Rama Febrian', 365, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS373-Rama Febrian.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS373-Rama Febrian.pdf', '2025-11-26 14:13:07', '2025-11-26 14:28:12'),
(374, 'digital_transformation_education', 'oral presentation', 'Integration of Technology (IoT and AI) in Environmental Education at Adiwiyata Schools in Jambi Province', 'Bunga Mardhotillah, Zurweni.', 'Universitas Jambi', 'The Integration of IoT and AI Technology in Environmental Education at Adiwiyata Schools in Jambi Province focuses on how technology can enhance the effectiveness of environment-based learning. Adiwiyata Schools are known as institutions committed to sustainability and environmental conservation, so the integration of technologies such as the Internet of Things (IoT) and Artificial Intelligence (AI) can help strengthen students\' ecological awareness through a more interactive and data-driven approach. In this study, IoT is used to monitor school environmental conditions in real time, such as air quality, energy use, and waste management. Data collected from IoT sensors can be analyzed using AI, enabling teachers and students to understand environmental change patterns and take more appropriate action. This study also identified several challenges in implementing this technology, such as limited infrastructure, lack of training for educators, and obstacles to curriculum integration. The results show that, with a technology-based approach, environmental education at Adiwiyata schools can be more adaptive, data-driven, and action-oriented, thus having a more significant impact on shaping a generation that cares about the environment.\n\n', 'IoT and AI Integration; Adiwiyata Schools.', 'Bunga Mardhotillah', 361, 'accepted', 'administrator@unja.ac.id', 'letter-of-acceptance/LOA-ABS374-Bunga Mardhotillah.pdf', 'invoice/Invoice-ABS374-Bunga Mardhotillah.pdf', '2025-11-26 14:22:29', '2025-11-26 14:40:55'),
